var e=[{topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Autacoids are substances made by the body itself, released locally where they are needed, and destroyed close to the site of release. The name means self-remedy. Histamine, serotonin, prostaglandins and leukotrienes are the four you must know for GPAT.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Histamine`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Stored mainly in mast cells and basophils, released when an allergen cross-links IgE on the cell surface.`,`Causes the triple response of Lewis: a red spot (capillary dilatation), a flare (arteriolar dilatation by axon reflex) and a wheal (fluid leaking out of leaky venules).`,`Bronchi constrict, gastric acid secretion rises, blood pressure falls, itching and pain occur at nerve endings.`,`Drugs that release histamine directly without any allergy: morphine, d-tubocurarine, vancomycin (red man syndrome) and radiocontrast dyes.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antihistamines`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`H1 antihistamines block the H1 receptor and are used for allergy, itching, urticaria, motion sickness and as sleep aids.`,`First generation drugs such as chlorpheniramine, promethazine and diphenhydramine cross into the brain and cause sedation and dry mouth.`,`Second generation drugs such as cetirizine, loratadine and fexofenadine stay outside the brain and cause little sedation.`,`H2 blockers such as ranitidine and famotidine reduce gastric acid and are covered with peptic ulcer drugs.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Prostaglandins and NSAIDs`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Injury releases arachidonic acid from the cell membrane. Cyclooxygenase (COX) turns it into prostaglandins and thromboxane; lipoxygenase (LOX) turns it into leukotrienes.`,`Prostaglandins cause pain, fever and inflammation; they also protect the stomach lining and maintain kidney blood flow.`,`NSAID stands for non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drug. NSAIDs block COX, so they relieve pain, fever and inflammation.`,`Because the same enzyme protects the stomach, NSAIDs commonly cause gastric irritation, ulcers and bleeding.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gout in one line`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Gout is inflammation caused by uric acid crystals settling in a joint, classically the big toe.`,`An acute attack is treated with NSAIDs or colchicine; long-term control uses allopurinol to make less uric acid.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Aspirin is not for children with fever`,text:`Aspirin given to a child with a viral fever such as chickenpox or influenza can trigger Reye syndrome, a fatal encephalopathy with fatty liver. Paracetamol is the antipyretic of choice in children.`}]},{topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions here cluster on four things: which histamine receptor does what, where in the eicosanoid cascade each drug class acts, NSAID classification by chemical class and COX selectivity, and the acute versus chronic gout split.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Histamine receptor subtypes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Receptor`,`G protein / signal`,`Main effect`,`Drug example`],rows:[[`H1`,`Gq, IP3 and DAG`,`Bronchoconstriction, itch, vasodilatation`,`Cetirizine (blocker)`],[`H2`,`Gs, raises cAMP`,`Gastric acid secretion, cardiac stimulation`,`Famotidine (blocker)`],[`H3`,`Gi, presynaptic autoreceptor`,`Inhibits histamine release in CNS`,`Pitolisant (inverse agonist)`],[`H4`,`Gi, on eosinophils and mast cells`,`Chemotaxis, immune modulation`,`Investigational`]],caption:`H1 and H2 are the therapeutically exploited pair; H3 is a CNS autoreceptor and H4 is immune.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`First versus second generation H1 blockers`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`First generation`,`Second generation`],rows:[[`Examples`,`Chlorpheniramine, promethazine`,`Cetirizine, loratadine, fexofenadine`],[`CNS entry`,`Crosses blood-brain barrier`,`Poor entry, P-glycoprotein substrate`],[`Sedation`,`Marked`,`Minimal`],[`Anticholinergic effect`,`Present, dry mouth and retention`,`Absent`],[`Antiemetic use`,`Yes, motion sickness and vertigo`,`No`],[`Duration`,`Short, 4 to 6 hours`,`Long, 12 to 24 hours`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Serotonin and the triptans`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`5-HT is stored in enterochromaffin cells of the gut (about 90 percent), platelets and CNS raphe neurons.`,`5-HT1: mostly inhibitory, Gi coupled. 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D agonists (sumatriptan, rizatriptan) constrict dilated cranial vessels and inhibit CGRP release, aborting migraine.`,`5-HT2A: Gq coupled, causes vasoconstriction and platelet aggregation. Blocked by ketanserin and by atypical antipsychotics.`,`5-HT3: the only ligand-gated ion channel in the family, in the CTZ and vagal afferents. Blocked by ondansetron for vomiting.`,`5-HT4: gut motility. Agonists such as cisapride and prucalopride are prokinetics.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`5-HT3 is the odd one out`,text:`Every other 5-HT receptor is G-protein coupled. 5-HT3 is a ligand-gated cation channel. This single-line fact is asked almost every year.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The eicosanoid cascade and where drugs block`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Step`,`Enzyme`,`Product`,`Blocked by`],rows:[[`Membrane phospholipid to arachidonic acid`,`Phospholipase A2`,`Arachidonic acid`,`Corticosteroids (via lipocortin)`],[`Arachidonic acid to PGG2 and PGH2`,`COX-1 and COX-2`,`Prostaglandins, TXA2`,`NSAIDs`],[`PGH2 to TXA2`,`Thromboxane synthase`,`TXA2`,`Low-dose aspirin (indirect)`],[`Arachidonic acid to LTA4`,`5-Lipoxygenase`,`Leukotrienes`,`Zileuton`],[`LTD4 acting on CysLT1`,`Receptor level`,`Bronchoconstriction`,`Montelukast, zafirlukast`]],caption:`Steroids act above the branch point so they suppress both limbs; NSAIDs act only on the COX limb.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`NSAID classification`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Chemical class`,`Drugs`,`Note`],rows:[[`Salicylate`,`Aspirin`,`Irreversible COX inhibitor`],[`Propionic acid`,`Ibuprofen, naproxen, ketoprofen`,`Best gastric tolerance`],[`Acetic acid (indole)`,`Indomethacin`,`Potent, closes patent ductus arteriosus`],[`Acetic acid (aryl)`,`Diclofenac, aceclofenac`,`Most used in India`],[`Oxicam (enolic acid)`,`Piroxicam, tenoxicam`,`Long half-life, once daily`],[`Pyrazolone`,`Phenylbutazone`,`Agranulocytosis, largely withdrawn`],[`Anthranilic acid (fenamate)`,`Mefenamic acid`,`Dysmenorrhoea`],[`Preferential COX-2`,`Nimesulide, meloxicam, etodolac`,`Less gastric injury`],[`Selective COX-2 (coxib)`,`Celecoxib, etoricoxib`,`Cardiovascular risk`],[`Para-aminophenol`,`Paracetamol`,`Analgesic and antipyretic, negligible anti-inflammatory action`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Aspirin is dose dependent`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Dose per day`,`Effect`],rows:[[`75 to 150 mg`,`Antiplatelet, irreversible platelet COX-1 block`],[`0.3 to 2 g`,`Analgesic and antipyretic`],[`3 to 6 g`,`Anti-inflammatory, used in rheumatic fever`],[`Low dose (under 2 g)`,`Decreases urate excretion, worsens gout`],[`High dose (over 5 g)`,`Uricosuric`]],caption:`The biphasic effect of aspirin on uric acid handling is a classic exam item.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Gout: acute versus chronic`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Setting`,`Drugs`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Acute attack`,`NSAIDs (indomethacin, naproxen), colchicine, steroids`,`Anti-inflammatory only`],[`Chronic, overproducer`,`Allopurinol, febuxostat`,`Xanthine oxidase inhibition`],[`Chronic, underexcretor`,`Probenecid, sulfinpyrazone`,`Block urate reabsorption in proximal tubule`],[`Refractory tophaceous`,`Pegloticase, rasburicase`,`Urate oxidase converts urate to allantoin`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Never start allopurinol during an acute attack`,text:`A sudden fall in plasma urate mobilises urate out of tophi and destabilises crystals in the joint, which prolongs or worsens the attack. Start urate-lowering therapy 2 to 3 weeks after the flare settles, under colchicine or NSAID cover.`}]},{topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced items here turn on molecular detail: how aspirin differs kinetically from every other NSAID, why paracetamol poisoning is a glutathione problem, and why blocking COX-2 alone raised cardiovascular events.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`COX-1 versus COX-2`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`COX-1`,`COX-2`],rows:[[`Expression`,`Constitutive`,`Inducible by cytokines`],[`Main sites`,`Stomach, platelets, kidney`,`Inflamed tissue, brain, kidney, endothelium`],[`Key product`,`PGE2 cytoprotection, TXA2`,`PGI2 and PGE2 of inflammation`],[`Active site`,`Narrower, Ile523`,`Wider side pocket, Val523`],[`Constitutive exception`,`—`,`Kidney macula densa, brain, endothelium`]],caption:`The single amino acid Ile to Val at position 523 widens the COX-2 pocket and is the structural basis of coxib selectivity.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Aspirin: irreversible acetylation`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Aspirin acetylates serine 530 of COX-1 and serine 516 of COX-2, an irreversible covalent block.`,`Platelets have no nucleus, so they cannot resynthesise COX. One dose suppresses thromboxane for the platelet lifespan of 7 to 10 days.`,`Endothelium is nucleated and regenerates COX within hours, so PGI2 recovers. This asymmetry is why low-dose aspirin is antithrombotic.`,`Aspirin follows first-order kinetics at low dose but saturates glycine conjugation and shifts to zero-order in overdose, so half-life rises from about 4 hours to 15 to 30 hours.`,`Salicylate overdose gives a mixed picture: early respiratory alkalosis from direct stimulation of the respiratory centre, then metabolic acidosis from uncoupling of oxidative phosphorylation.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Paracetamol toxicity`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`At therapeutic dose about 90 percent of paracetamol is conjugated as glucuronide and sulfate and excreted.`,`A small fraction goes through CYP2E1 to the reactive quinone imine NAPQI (N-acetyl-p-benzoquinone imine).`,`NAPQI is normally detoxified by conjugation with hepatic glutathione.`,`In overdose (above about 10 to 15 g in an adult) conjugation saturates, glutathione is depleted below 30 percent, and free NAPQI binds hepatocyte proteins causing centrilobular necrosis.`,`N-acetylcysteine replenishes glutathione and also acts as a sulfhydryl donor. It is most effective within 8 to 10 hours; benefit falls off after 16 hours but it is still given late.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Why liver damage is delayed`,text:`Transaminases peak at 48 to 72 hours, not on day one. A normal liver panel on admission does not exclude a lethal ingestion, so treatment is decided on the plasma paracetamol level plotted on the Rumack-Matthew nomogram, not on symptoms.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The coxib cardiovascular problem`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Endothelial PGI2 is largely COX-2 derived and is antiplatelet and vasodilator; platelet TXA2 is COX-1 derived and is prothrombotic.`,`A selective COX-2 inhibitor removes PGI2 while leaving platelet TXA2 intact, tilting the balance towards thrombosis.`,`Rofecoxib and valdecoxib were withdrawn on this basis. Celecoxib and etoricoxib carry the same class warning.`,`Ibuprofen taken shortly before aspirin occupies the COX-1 channel and blocks aspirin from reaching Ser530, abolishing cardioprotection. Give aspirin at least 30 minutes before ibuprofen.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`DMARDs and biologics`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Class`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Methotrexate`,`Conventional DMARD`,`Dihydrofolate reductase inhibition, adenosine release`],[`Sulfasalazine`,`Conventional DMARD`,`Split to 5-ASA and sulfapyridine`],[`Hydroxychloroquine`,`Conventional DMARD`,`Raises lysosomal pH, blocks antigen presentation`],[`Leflunomide`,`Conventional DMARD`,`Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase inhibition`],[`Etanercept`,`Biologic`,`Soluble TNF receptor fusion protein`],[`Infliximab, adalimumab`,`Biologic`,`Anti-TNF-alpha monoclonal antibody`],[`Tocilizumab`,`Biologic`,`Anti-IL-6 receptor antibody`],[`Anakinra`,`Biologic`,`IL-1 receptor antagonist`],[`Rituximab`,`Biologic`,`Anti-CD20, depletes B cells`],[`Tofacitinib`,`Targeted synthetic`,`JAK kinase inhibitor`]],caption:`Methotrexate remains the anchor drug in rheumatoid arthritis; screen for latent tuberculosis before any anti-TNF agent.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Colchicine and allopurinol detail`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Colchicine binds tubulin and prevents microtubule polymerisation, so neutrophils cannot migrate into the joint. It is not an analgesic and does not lower urate.`,`Dose-limiting toxicity of colchicine is diarrhoea, which appears before the anti-inflammatory benefit is complete.`,`Allopurinol is itself a substrate of xanthine oxidase and is converted to oxypurinol (alloxanthine), a long-acting non-competitive inhibitor with a half-life of about 18 to 30 hours, which is why once-daily dosing works.`,`Allopurinol blocks the metabolism of 6-mercaptopurine and azathioprine; reduce those doses to about one quarter. Febuxostat does not need renal dose adjustment to the same extent but carries a cardiovascular warning.`]}]},{topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Insulin is the hormone from the beta cells of the pancreas that moves glucose out of blood and into cells. Diabetes mellitus is a state of high blood glucose either because insulin is absent (type 1) or because tissues stop responding to it (type 2).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The two types`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Type 1`,`Type 2`],rows:[[`Cause`,`Autoimmune beta cell destruction`,`Insulin resistance plus relative deficiency`],[`Usual age`,`Children and young adults`,`Middle age and later`],[`Body build`,`Usually lean`,`Usually overweight`],[`Treatment`,`Insulin always`,`Diet, oral drugs, insulin later`],[`Ketosis`,`Common`,`Uncommon`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Insulin basics`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Insulin is a protein, so it is destroyed in the gut and must be injected, usually subcutaneously.`,`It acts on a receptor tyrosine kinase and recruits the GLUT4 transporter to the cell membrane in muscle and fat.`,`The main danger of insulin is hypoglycaemia: sweating, tremor, hunger, confusion, then coma. Treat with glucose, or glucagon if the patient cannot swallow.`,`Insulin also drives potassium into cells, which is why insulin with dextrose is used to treat high blood potassium.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Oral drugs in one line each`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Metformin: reduces glucose output by the liver. First choice in type 2 diabetes. Does not cause hypoglycaemia by itself.`,`Sulfonylureas such as glibenclamide and glimepiride: make the pancreas release more insulin. Can cause hypoglycaemia and weight gain.`,`Pioglitazone: makes tissues more sensitive to insulin.`,`Acarbose: slows digestion of starch in the gut so glucose is absorbed more slowly.`,`Sitagliptin and the gliptins: raise the gut hormone GLP-1.`,`Dapagliflozin and the gliflozins: make the kidney throw glucose out in the urine.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The thyroid gland`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The thyroid makes T4 (thyroxine) and T3 (triiodothyronine) using iodine from the diet. These hormones set the basal metabolic rate.`,`Hypothyroidism means too little hormone: tiredness, weight gain, cold intolerance, slow pulse. Treated with levothyroxine tablets.`,`Hyperthyroidism means too much: weight loss, heat intolerance, fast pulse, tremor. Treated with carbimazole, radioactive iodine or surgery.`,`Propranolol is added in hyperthyroidism to control tremor and fast heart rate while the main drug takes effect.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Levothyroxine on an empty stomach`,text:`Levothyroxine is taken in the morning at least 30 to 60 minutes before food. Calcium, iron and antacids bind it in the gut and must be separated by about 4 hours.`}]},{topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic is dense with tables the examiner can lift directly: insulin preparations by onset and duration, the full antidiabetic classification with mechanisms, and the steps of thyroid hormone synthesis matched to the drug that blocks each one.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Insulin preparations`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Example`,`Onset`,`Peak`,`Duration`],rows:[[`Rapid-acting analogue`,`Lispro, aspart, glulisine`,`10 to 15 min`,`1 to 2 h`,`3 to 5 h`],[`Short-acting (regular)`,`Soluble insulin`,`30 to 60 min`,`2 to 4 h`,`6 to 8 h`],[`Intermediate`,`NPH (isophane)`,`1 to 2 h`,`6 to 12 h`,`12 to 18 h`],[`Long-acting analogue`,`Glargine`,`1 to 2 h`,`No peak`,`20 to 24 h`],[`Long-acting analogue`,`Detemir`,`1 to 2 h`,`Flat`,`12 to 20 h`],[`Ultra-long analogue`,`Degludec`,`1 to 2 h`,`No peak`,`Over 40 h`]],caption:`Only regular (soluble) insulin and the rapid analogues may be given intravenously.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Lispro reverses lysine and proline at B28 and B29; aspart substitutes aspartate at B28. Both weaken hexamer formation so absorption is faster.`,`Glargine adds two arginines and swaps asparagine for glycine, shifting the isoelectric point to pH 7. It is soluble in the acidic vial and precipitates in subcutaneous tissue, giving a flat release.`,`Detemir is acylated with myristic acid and binds albumin; degludec forms multihexamer depots.`,`NPH contains protamine and zinc and is a suspension, so it must be rolled before use and never given intravenously.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Oral and injectable antidiabetics`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Mechanism`,`Hypoglycaemia`,`Weight`],rows:[[`Biguanide`,`Metformin`,`Activates AMP kinase, cuts hepatic gluconeogenesis`,`No`,`Neutral or loss`],[`Sulfonylurea`,`Glibenclamide, glipizide, glimepiride`,`Closes K-ATP channel on beta cell (SUR1)`,`Yes`,`Gain`],[`Meglitinide`,`Repaglinide, nateglinide`,`Same channel, different site, short acting`,`Yes, less`,`Gain`],[`Thiazolidinedione`,`Pioglitazone`,`PPAR-gamma agonist, raises insulin sensitivity`,`No`,`Gain`],[`Alpha-glucosidase inhibitor`,`Acarbose, miglitol, voglibose`,`Blocks intestinal alpha-glucosidase`,`No`,`Neutral`],[`DPP-4 inhibitor`,`Sitagliptin, vildagliptin, linagliptin`,`Stops incretin breakdown`,`No`,`Neutral`],[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Dapagliflozin, empagliflozin`,`Blocks proximal tubule glucose reabsorption`,`No`,`Loss`],[`GLP-1 agonist`,`Liraglutide, exenatide, semaglutide`,`Incretin receptor agonist, slows gastric emptying`,`No`,`Loss`]],caption:`Only the insulin secretagogues (sulfonylureas and meglitinides) cause hypoglycaemia when used alone.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which ones lose weight`,text:`The two classes that push glucose or calories out of the body cause weight loss: gliflozins (glucose out in urine) and GLP-1 agonists (less eaten). Sulfonylureas, meglitinides and glitazones store it, so they cause weight gain.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Adverse effects worth memorising`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug or class`,`Signature adverse effect`],rows:[[`Metformin`,`Lactic acidosis, vitamin B12 malabsorption, diarrhoea`],[`Sulfonylureas`,`Hypoglycaemia (worst with glibenclamide), weight gain`],[`Pioglitazone`,`Fluid retention, heart failure, fractures, bladder cancer signal`],[`Acarbose`,`Flatulence, abdominal bloating, diarrhoea`],[`SGLT2 inhibitors`,`Genital mycotic infection, euglycaemic ketoacidosis`],[`GLP-1 agonists`,`Nausea, pancreatitis, thyroid C-cell tumour warning`],[`DPP-4 inhibitors`,`Nasopharyngitis, arthralgia, pancreatitis`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Thyroid hormone synthesis and drug targets`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Step`,`Process`,`Blocked by`],rows:[[`1 Uptake`,`Iodide trapped by Na-I symporter`,`Thiocyanate, perchlorate, pertechnetate`],[`2 Oxidation`,`Iodide to iodine by thyroid peroxidase`,`Carbimazole, methimazole, propylthiouracil`],[`3 Iodination`,`Tyrosyl residues to MIT and DIT`,`Same thioamides`],[`4 Coupling`,`MIT + DIT to T3, DIT + DIT to T4`,`Same thioamides`],[`5 Release`,`Proteolysis of thyroglobulin`,`Iodides (Lugol iodine), lithium`],[`6 Peripheral conversion`,`T4 to T3 by 5-deiodinase`,`Propylthiouracil, propranolol, amiodarone, steroids`]],caption:`Only propylthiouracil among the thioamides also blocks peripheral conversion.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Carbimazole, methimazole and propylthiouracil`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Carbimazole / methimazole`,`Propylthiouracil`],rows:[[`Relation`,`Carbimazole is a prodrug of methimazole`,`Active as such`],[`Potency`,`About 10 times more potent`,`Less potent`],[`Dosing`,`Once daily`,`Two to three times daily`],[`Peripheral T4 to T3 block`,`No`,`Yes`],[`Placental and milk transfer`,`Higher`,`Lower, protein bound`],[`Preferred in`,`Most patients`,`First trimester pregnancy, thyroid storm`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Iodide has opposite effects`,text:`Small doses of iodide support hormone synthesis, but large doses acutely inhibit release and organification (the Wolff-Chaikoff effect). The block lasts only 10 to 14 days, so Lugol iodine is used for pre-operative preparation and thyroid storm, never as long-term therapy.`}]},{topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced items test the molecular target of each secretagogue, the mechanism of metformin lactic acidosis, the exact protocol of diabetic ketoacidosis, and the drug sequence in thyroid storm.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The beta cell and its channel`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Glucose enters the beta cell through GLUT2 and is phosphorylated by glucokinase, the rate-limiting glucose sensor.`,`Metabolism raises the ATP to ADP ratio.`,`ATP closes the ATP-sensitive potassium channel, a complex of Kir6.2 pore and SUR1 regulatory subunit.`,`The membrane depolarises, voltage-gated L-type calcium channels open, and calcium influx triggers exocytosis of insulin granules.`,`Sulfonylureas bind SUR1 directly and close the same channel independently of glucose, which is why they cause hypoglycaemia; meglitinides bind an overlapping but distinct site with faster on-off kinetics.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Diazoxide opens the K-ATP channel and therefore raises blood glucose; it is used in insulinoma and in hyperinsulinaemic hypoglycaemia.`,`Glibenclamide is long acting with an active metabolite and is the sulfonylurea most associated with prolonged hypoglycaemia in the elderly and in renal impairment.`,`Glimepiride and gliclazide have lower cardiovascular K-ATP (SUR2A) cross-reactivity and are preferred in ischaemic heart disease.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Metformin and lactic acidosis`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Metformin inhibits mitochondrial complex I, lowering ATP and raising AMP; AMP kinase activation suppresses gluconeogenic gene expression.`,`Reduced hepatic gluconeogenesis means lactate is not cleared through the Cori cycle, so lactate accumulates.`,`Risk rises steeply when renal clearance falls. Metformin is avoided when eGFR is below 30 mL/min/1.73 m2 and reviewed between 30 and 45.`,`Withhold metformin before iodinated contrast studies and major surgery, and during any acute hypoxic illness, sepsis or heavy alcohol intake.`,`Metformin is not metabolised, is not protein bound, and is excreted unchanged by renal tubular secretion via OCT2, so cimetidine raises its level.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Diabetic ketoacidosis management`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Fluid first: isotonic saline to restore circulating volume, since the deficit is typically 5 to 7 litres.`,`Regular (soluble) insulin by continuous intravenous infusion, commonly about 0.1 unit/kg/hour. Only regular and rapid analogues may be given intravenously.`,`Potassium: total body potassium is depleted even when the plasma level looks normal or high. Start replacement once potassium is below about 5.2 mmol/L and urine flow is established, because insulin drives potassium into cells.`,`Switch the fluid to dextrose-containing saline when plasma glucose falls to about 200 to 250 mg/dL so that insulin can continue to clear ketones without causing hypoglycaemia.`,`Bicarbonate only in severe acidosis (pH below about 6.9); routine use risks paradoxical CSF acidosis and hypokalaemia.`,`Treat the precipitating cause, most often infection or missed insulin.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Euglycaemic ketoacidosis`,text:`SGLT2 inhibitors can produce ketoacidosis with only mildly raised or even normal glucose, because urinary glucose loss masks the hyperglycaemia. Suspect it on the acid-base picture, not the glucose reading, and stop the drug before surgery or prolonged fasting.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Thyroid pharmacokinetics and radioiodine`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Parameter`,`T4`,`T3`],rows:[[`Plasma protein binding`,`About 99.96 percent`,`About 99.6 percent`],[`Plasma half-life`,`6 to 7 days`,`About 1 to 2 days`],[`Potency`,`Lower, acts as prohormone`,`About 4 times more potent`],[`Onset of full effect`,`Slow, weeks`,`Rapid, hours to days`],[`Therapeutic use`,`Levothyroxine, drug of choice`,`Liothyronine, myxoedema coma`]],caption:`About 80 percent of circulating T3 comes from peripheral 5-deiodination of T4, not from the gland.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Radioactive iodine I-131 emits beta particles with a physical half-life of about 8 days and destroys follicular cells over 6 to 12 weeks. It is contraindicated in pregnancy and lactation.`,`Its commonest long-term consequence is permanent hypothyroidism, which is accepted and treated with levothyroxine.`,`Amiodarone contains about 37 percent iodine by weight and can cause either hypothyroidism (Wolff-Chaikoff escape failure) or thyrotoxicosis (Jod-Basedow, or destructive thyroiditis).`,`Thyroid storm sequence: propylthiouracil first, then iodide at least one hour later so the iodide is not used as substrate, plus propranolol, hydrocortisone, cooling and fluids.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Order matters in thyroid storm`,text:`Give the thioamide before the iodide. If iodide is given first it is trapped and organified into fresh hormone, making the storm worse. Steroids are added because they block peripheral T4 to T3 conversion and cover relative adrenal insufficiency.`}]},{topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The adrenal cortex makes two families of steroid hormone. Glucocorticoids such as cortisol control glucose, protein handling and inflammation. Mineralocorticoids such as aldosterone control sodium and potassium in the kidney.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`What glucocorticoids do`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`They suppress inflammation and the immune response, which is why they are used in asthma, allergy, autoimmune disease and after transplantation.`,`They raise blood glucose by making the liver form glucose from amino acids, and they break down muscle protein.`,`They redistribute fat to the face, neck and trunk, giving the moon face and buffalo hump of long-term use.`,`They are also replacement therapy in Addison disease, where the adrenal gland fails.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Common problems with long use`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cushingoid appearance, weight gain, thin skin, easy bruising and poor wound healing.`,`Osteoporosis, high blood glucose, high blood pressure, cataract and glaucoma.`,`Lowered resistance to infection, and masking of the fever that would warn you about it.`,`Growth retardation in children.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Never stop a steroid suddenly`,text:`Steroids given for more than about two weeks switch off the pituitary and shrink the adrenal cortex. Stopping abruptly leaves the patient unable to make cortisol, causing an acute adrenal crisis with vomiting, low blood pressure and shock. Always taper.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Sex hormones in one line each`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Oestrogens: female sex hormone; used in contraception and in menopausal hormone therapy.`,`Progestins: maintain pregnancy; the backbone of contraceptive pills.`,`Androgens such as testosterone: male sex hormone; anabolic steroids are chemical relatives that build muscle and are abused in sport.`,`Oxytocin contracts the uterus and is used to start or strengthen labour. Drugs that relax the uterus to delay premature labour are called tocolytics.`,`Sildenafil and its relatives improve erection by increasing blood flow in the penis.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Aldosterone keeps salt, loses potassium`,text:`Mineralocorticoid means mineral. Aldosterone retains sodium and water and throws out potassium and hydrogen ions, so excess causes high blood pressure with low potassium.`}]},{topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is the potency table, the receptor mechanism, the rules of withdrawal and alternate-day dosing, and the classification of reproductive drugs with their mechanisms.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Corticosteroid potency comparison`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Anti-inflammatory potency`,`Mineralocorticoid potency`,`Duration`,`Equivalent dose (mg)`],rows:[[`Hydrocortisone`,`1`,`1`,`Short 8-12 h`,`20`],[`Prednisolone`,`4`,`0.8`,`Intermediate 12-36 h`,`5`],[`Methylprednisolone`,`5`,`0.5`,`Intermediate`,`4`],[`Triamcinolone`,`5`,`0`,`Intermediate`,`4`],[`Dexamethasone`,`25 to 30`,`0`,`Long 36-72 h`,`0.75`],[`Betamethasone`,`25 to 30`,`0`,`Long`,`0.75`],[`Fludrocortisone`,`10`,`125 to 150`,`Short`,`—`],[`Aldosterone`,`0`,`300 to 500`,`—`,`—`]],caption:`Fludrocortisone is the mineralocorticoid of choice for replacement; dexamethasone and betamethasone have essentially no salt-retaining action.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mechanism`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The lipophilic steroid crosses the membrane and binds a cytoplasmic glucocorticoid receptor held by heat shock protein 90.`,`HSP90 dissociates and the steroid-receptor complex dimerises and moves to the nucleus.`,`It binds glucocorticoid response elements on DNA, transactivating anti-inflammatory genes such as lipocortin-1 (annexin-1) and transrepressing NF-kB and AP-1.`,`Lipocortin inhibits phospholipase A2, cutting off both prostaglandins and leukotrienes at source.`,`Because the effect is genomic, the clinical onset takes hours, which is why adrenaline and not hydrocortisone is the first drug in anaphylaxis.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Therapeutic uses`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Replacement: Addison disease, congenital adrenal hyperplasia (hydrocortisone plus fludrocortisone).`,`Anti-inflammatory and immunosuppressive: rheumatoid arthritis, SLE, asthma, IBD, nephrotic syndrome, transplant rejection.`,`Allergic and dermatological: severe allergy, eczema, pemphigus.`,`Malignancy: lymphoma and leukaemia protocols (prednisolone), and control of cerebral oedema (dexamethasone).`,`Obstetric: betamethasone or dexamethasone to a mother in preterm labour to mature fetal lung surfactant.`,`Diagnostic: dexamethasone suppression test in Cushing syndrome.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Alternate-day therapy uses short and intermediate drugs only`,text:`Giving a double dose every second morning lets the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis recover on the off day. It only works with prednisolone-type intermediate-acting steroids. Long-acting dexamethasone suppresses the axis continuously and defeats the purpose.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Oestrogens, progestins and SERMs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Class`,`Action at breast`,`Action at bone`,`Action at endometrium`],rows:[[`Ethinylestradiol`,`Oestrogen`,`Agonist`,`Agonist`,`Agonist`],[`Tamoxifen`,`SERM`,`Antagonist`,`Agonist`,`Agonist (cancer risk)`],[`Raloxifene`,`SERM`,`Antagonist`,`Agonist`,`Neutral`],[`Clomiphene`,`SERM`,`Antagonist at hypothalamus`,`—`,`—`],[`Anastrozole`,`Aromatase inhibitor`,`Blocks oestrogen synthesis`,`Bone loss`,`—`],[`Fulvestrant`,`Pure antagonist`,`Receptor degrader`,`—`,`—`]],caption:`Tamoxifen is used in premenopausal breast cancer, aromatase inhibitors in postmenopausal disease because their substrate is peripheral adipose aromatase.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Oral contraceptives`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Combined pill: ethinylestradiol plus a progestin (levonorgestrel, desogestrel, drospirenone). Oestrogen suppresses FSH and stops follicle selection; progestin suppresses the LH surge, thickens cervical mucus and thins the endometrium.`,`Progestin-only pill (minipill): suitable in lactation and where oestrogen is contraindicated; must be taken at the same time each day.`,`Emergency contraception: levonorgestrel 1.5 mg within 72 hours, or ulipristal, or a copper IUCD within 5 days (the most effective option).`,`Combined pills raise the risk of venous thromboembolism and are avoided in smokers over 35, in migraine with aura and in known thrombophilia.`,`Rifampicin, phenytoin, carbamazepine and other enzyme inducers reduce contraceptive efficacy.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Uterine drugs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Purpose`,`Drugs`],rows:[[`Oxytocic, induce or augment labour`,`Oxytocin, dinoprostone (PGE2), misoprostol (PGE1)`],[`Control postpartum haemorrhage`,`Oxytocin, methylergometrine, carboprost, tranexamic acid`],[`Tocolytic, delay preterm labour`,`Nifedipine, ritodrine and isoxsuprine, atosiban, magnesium sulfate, indomethacin`],[`Medical abortion`,`Mifepristone followed by misoprostol`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Ergometrine versus oxytocin`,text:`Methylergometrine causes a sustained tonic contraction, so it is never used before delivery of the baby. Oxytocin gives rhythmic contractions with relaxation between and is the drug for augmenting labour.`}]},{topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced detail: structure-activity of the corticosteroid nucleus, the axis-recovery timeline, androgen and antiandrogen pharmacology, the phosphodiesterase-5 story, and the full osteoporosis armamentarium.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Steroid structure-activity relationships`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The 4-ene-3-one A ring and the C21 hydroxyl are essential for glucocorticoid activity.`,`A 1,2 double bond (prednisolone) raises glucocorticoid potency about fourfold and reduces salt retention.`,`6-alpha methyl (methylprednisolone) and 9-alpha fluoro substitution both increase potency; 9-alpha fluoro alone (fludrocortisone) also greatly increases mineralocorticoid activity.`,`A 16-alpha hydroxyl or 16-methyl group (triamcinolone, dexamethasone, betamethasone) abolishes mineralocorticoid activity.`,`11-keto compounds such as cortisone and prednisone are prodrugs needing hepatic 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase, so they are ineffective topically and unreliable in liver failure.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`HPA axis suppression and withdrawal`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Suppression is likely with more than about 20 to 30 mg prednisolone equivalent daily for longer than 2 to 3 weeks, or with any evening dosing.`,`Recovery order after stopping: pituitary ACTH secretion returns over 2 to 5 months, adrenal cortisol response takes 6 to 9 months, full stress response up to a year.`,`Withdrawal syndrome mimics the original disease and adds fever, myalgia, arthralgia and malaise even with normal cortisol.`,`During major surgery, infection or trauma a patient on chronic steroids needs stress-dose cover, classically hydrocortisone intravenously.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Androgens and antiandrogens`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Class`,`Mechanism or use`],rows:[[`Testosterone undecanoate`,`Androgen`,`Replacement in hypogonadism`],[`Nandrolone, stanozolol`,`Anabolic steroid`,`High anabolic to androgenic ratio, abused in sport`],[`Danazol`,`Weak androgen`,`Endometriosis, hereditary angioedema`],[`Finasteride, dutasteride`,`5-alpha reductase inhibitor`,`Blocks testosterone to dihydrotestosterone; BPH and alopecia`],[`Flutamide, bicalutamide`,`Non-steroidal antiandrogen`,`Androgen receptor blockade in prostate cancer`],[`Cyproterone acetate`,`Steroidal antiandrogen`,`Receptor block plus progestogenic gonadotrophin suppression`],[`Spironolactone`,`Aldosterone antagonist`,`Also blocks androgen receptor, causes gynaecomastia`],[`Leuprolide, goserelin`,`GnRH agonist`,`Continuous use downregulates pituitary, chemical castration`],[`Degarelix`,`GnRH antagonist`,`Immediate suppression without initial flare`],[`Abiraterone`,`CYP17 inhibitor`,`Blocks adrenal and tumour androgen synthesis`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`The GnRH agonist flare`,text:`A GnRH agonist first stimulates the pituitary and causes a testosterone surge that can worsen bone pain or cause cord compression in metastatic prostate cancer. Cover the first two weeks with an antiandrogen such as bicalutamide, or use the antagonist degarelix which has no flare.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Erectile dysfunction and BPH`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Nitric oxide from cavernosal nerve endings raises cGMP, relaxing smooth muscle. PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil, tadalafil, vardenafil) prevent cGMP breakdown, so they need intact nerve-mediated NO release and are not effective without sexual stimulation.`,`Absolute contraindication: any nitrate, because combined vasodilatation causes profound hypotension. Allow 24 hours after sildenafil and 48 hours after tadalafil before a nitrate.`,`Sildenafil has weak PDE-6 cross-reactivity giving a blue-tinged visual disturbance; tadalafil has the longest duration (about 36 hours) and more PDE-11 related back pain.`,`BPH: alpha-1A blockers tamsulosin, alfuzosin and silodosin relax prostatic smooth muscle and give rapid symptom relief; 5-alpha reductase inhibitors shrink the gland over 3 to 6 months and reduce serum PSA by about half.`,`Tamsulosin is associated with intraoperative floppy iris syndrome during cataract surgery.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Osteoporosis drugs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Class`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Alendronate, risedronate, zoledronate`,`Bisphosphonate`,`Farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase inhibition in osteoclasts`],[`Denosumab`,`Monoclonal antibody`,`Anti-RANK ligand, blocks osteoclast maturation`],[`Teriparatide`,`PTH 1-34 fragment`,`Intermittent dosing is anabolic on osteoblasts`],[`Raloxifene`,`SERM`,`Oestrogen agonist at bone`],[`Calcitonin`,`Peptide hormone`,`Inhibits osteoclasts, analgesic in vertebral fracture`],[`Romosozumab`,`Monoclonal antibody`,`Anti-sclerostin, dual anabolic and antiresorptive`]],caption:`Bisphosphonate risks: oesophagitis (take with a full glass of water, stay upright 30 minutes), osteonecrosis of the jaw and atypical femoral fracture.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Continuous PTH resorbs, pulsed PTH builds`,text:`Continuous parathyroid hormone excess causes bone resorption, but once-daily teriparatide injection produces a short pulse that favours osteoblast survival and net bone formation. This paradox is a repeated GPAT question.`}]},{topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic covers drugs for the stomach and bowel and drugs for the airways. The linking idea is that both systems are controlled by receptors you can block or stimulate to change secretion, motility or muscle tone.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Peptic ulcer drugs`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A peptic ulcer is a break in the stomach or duodenal lining, usually caused by the bacterium Helicobacter pylori or by NSAIDs.`,`Antacids such as aluminium hydroxide and magnesium hydroxide simply neutralise acid already present. They act fast but briefly.`,`H2 blockers such as ranitidine and famotidine stop histamine from stimulating the acid pump.`,`Proton pump inhibitors such as omeprazole and pantoprazole block the final pump itself and give the strongest, longest acid suppression.`,`Sucralfate coats the ulcer base like a bandage; misoprostol replaces the protective prostaglandin lost when NSAIDs are used.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Magnesium moves, aluminium anchors`,text:`Magnesium-containing antacids cause diarrhoea; aluminium-containing antacids cause constipation. They are combined so the two effects cancel out.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Vomiting and bowel drugs`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Antiemetics stop vomiting. Ondansetron is used for chemotherapy sickness, metoclopramide and domperidone for gastric causes, hyoscine and promethazine for motion sickness.`,`Laxatives relieve constipation. Bulk agents add fibre, osmotic agents pull water in, stimulants irritate the bowel, and stool softeners lubricate.`,`Antidiarrhoeals such as loperamide slow the bowel; oral rehydration salts replace lost water and salt and are the life-saving treatment in childhood diarrhoea.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Asthma in plain terms`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Asthma has two parts: the airway muscle tightens (bronchospasm) and the airway lining becomes inflamed and swollen.`,`Relievers open the airway quickly. Salbutamol, a short-acting beta-2 agonist, is the standard reliever inhaler.`,`Controllers prevent attacks by reducing inflammation. Inhaled corticosteroids such as budesonide and beclometasone are the main controllers.`,`Inhalation delivers the drug straight to the airway using a small dose, so side effects elsewhere in the body are limited.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rinse the mouth after a steroid inhaler`,text:`Inhaled corticosteroid left in the mouth causes oral thrush and hoarseness. Rinsing and spitting after each dose, or using a spacer, prevents both.`}]},{topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you need the acid-secretion receptor map, eradication regimens, antiemetics grouped by receptor and matched to cause, the laxative classification, and the reliever versus controller framework in asthma.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Control of gastric acid`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Three stimuli converge on the parietal cell: histamine at H2 (Gs, cAMP), acetylcholine at M3 (Gq, calcium) and gastrin at CCK2 (Gq, calcium).`,`All three activate the H+/K+ ATPase, the proton pump, which is the final common step.`,`Histamine is the dominant amplifier, so an H2 blocker reduces the response to all three stimuli.`,`PPIs act on the pump itself and therefore suppress acid regardless of which stimulus is present.`]},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Example`,`Mechanism`,`Key point`],rows:[[`PPI`,`Omeprazole, pantoprazole, rabeprazole`,`Irreversible block of H+/K+ ATPase`,`Prodrug, needs acid activation, give before food`],[`H2 blocker`,`Ranitidine, famotidine, cimetidine`,`Competitive H2 antagonism`,`Good for nocturnal acid; cimetidine inhibits CYP`],[`Antacid`,`Al(OH)3, Mg(OH)2, CaCO3`,`Chemical neutralisation`,`Fast, short, alters absorption of other drugs`],[`Cytoprotective`,`Sucralfate`,`Polymerises in acid, coats ulcer base`,`Needs acid, so do not combine with a PPI at the same time`],[`Prostaglandin analogue`,`Misoprostol`,`PGE1 analogue, restores mucosal defence`,`Prevents NSAID ulcer; abortifacient, avoid in pregnancy`],[`Anticholinergic`,`Pirenzepine`,`M1 selective`,`Largely obsolete`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`PPIs are acid-activated prodrugs`,text:`Omeprazole is an inactive prodrug that must be protonated in the acidic canaliculus to form the sulfenamide that binds cysteine residues of the pump. This is why it is enteric coated, why it is taken 30 to 60 minutes before a meal, and why it works best when pumps are actively secreting.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`H. pylori eradication`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Standard triple therapy for 14 days: a PPI twice daily plus amoxicillin 1 g twice daily plus clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily.`,`Penicillin allergy: replace amoxicillin with metronidazole 400 mg.`,`Quadruple (bismuth) therapy: PPI plus bismuth subsalicylate plus metronidazole plus tetracycline, used where clarithromycin resistance is high or after failure.`,`A single antibiotic never eradicates the organism; combination therapy prevents resistance and reaches both the mucus layer and the epithelium.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antiemetics by receptor`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Drug`,`Receptor`,`Best suited to`],rows:[[`5-HT3 antagonist`,`Ondansetron, granisetron, palonosetron`,`5-HT3 in CTZ and gut`,`Chemotherapy, radiotherapy, postoperative`],[`NK1 antagonist`,`Aprepitant, fosaprepitant`,`Substance P NK1`,`Delayed chemotherapy emesis`],[`D2 antagonist`,`Metoclopramide, domperidone`,`D2 in CTZ`,`Gastric stasis, drug-induced vomiting`],[`Phenothiazine`,`Prochlorperazine, chlorpromazine`,`D2, H1, muscarinic`,`Vertigo, general nausea`],[`H1 antihistamine`,`Promethazine, cinnarizine, meclizine`,`H1 vestibular pathway`,`Motion sickness, vertigo, pregnancy`],[`Anticholinergic`,`Hyoscine (scopolamine)`,`Muscarinic`,`Motion sickness, most effective for it`],[`Corticosteroid`,`Dexamethasone`,`Multiple`,`Adjunct in chemotherapy regimens`],[`Cannabinoid`,`Nabilone, dronabinol`,`CB1`,`Refractory chemotherapy emesis`]],caption:`Cisplatin, the most emetogenic drug, is covered by a 5-HT3 antagonist plus dexamethasone plus aprepitant.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Metoclopramide versus domperidone`,text:`Both are D2 blockers and prokinetics, but metoclopramide crosses the blood-brain barrier and causes extrapyramidal reactions and acute dystonia, especially in the young. Domperidone does not cross appreciably, so it has no extrapyramidal effect but does prolong the QT interval.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Laxatives and antidiarrhoeals`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Examples`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Bulk forming`,`Ispaghula, psyllium, methylcellulose, bran`,`Hold water, increase faecal mass`],[`Osmotic`,`Lactulose, magnesium sulfate, polyethylene glycol`,`Retain water osmotically in the lumen`],[`Stimulant`,`Bisacodyl, senna, sodium picosulfate`,`Stimulate enteric nerves and secretion`],[`Stool softener`,`Docusate sodium, liquid paraffin`,`Detergent or lubricant action`],[`Chloride channel activator`,`Lubiprostone`,`Opens ClC-2 channels, increases fluid`],[`Antidiarrhoeal opioid`,`Loperamide, diphenoxylate`,`Peripheral mu receptor, slows transit`],[`Adsorbent`,`Kaolin, pectin, activated charcoal`,`Binds toxins and water`],[`Antisecretory`,`Racecadotril, bismuth subsalicylate`,`Enkephalinase inhibition, antisecretory`]],caption:`Lactulose is also used in hepatic encephalopathy because it traps ammonia as ammonium in the acidified colon.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Asthma: reliever versus controller`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Category`,`Drugs`,`Role`],rows:[[`Reliever`,`Salbutamol, terbutaline (SABA); ipratropium`,`Rapid bronchodilation in an attack`],[`Controller`,`Budesonide, beclometasone, fluticasone (ICS)`,`Suppress airway inflammation, first-line preventer`],[`Controller add-on`,`Salmeterol, formoterol (LABA)`,`Only with an ICS, never as monotherapy`],[`Controller`,`Montelukast, zafirlukast`,`CysLT1 antagonist, useful in aspirin-sensitive and exercise asthma`],[`Controller`,`Theophylline, doxophylline`,`Phosphodiesterase inhibition, adenosine antagonism`],[`Controller`,`Tiotropium (LAMA)`,`Long-acting antimuscarinic add-on`],[`Biologic`,`Omalizumab (anti-IgE), mepolizumab (anti-IL-5)`,`Severe allergic or eosinophilic asthma`],[`Mast cell stabiliser`,`Sodium cromoglicate, nedocromil`,`Prophylaxis only, no use in an acute attack`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`LABA monotherapy is contraindicated`,text:`A long-acting beta-2 agonist used alone relieves symptoms while inflammation progresses silently, and is associated with increased asthma deaths. LABAs must always be given with an inhaled corticosteroid, usually in a single combination inhaler.`}]},{topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced material: PPI interactions and long-term risks, IBD drug detail, theophylline kinetics as a narrow therapeutic index model, COPD differences and the cough drug groups.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`PPI fine detail`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Omeprazole and esomeprazole inhibit CYP2C19 and reduce activation of clopidogrel, itself a prodrug requiring CYP2C19. Pantoprazole has the least interaction and is preferred with clopidogrel.`,`Raising gastric pH reduces absorption of ketoconazole, itraconazole capsules, atazanavir, iron salts and cyanocobalamin.`,`Long-term PPI use is associated with hypomagnesaemia, vitamin B12 deficiency, Clostridioides difficile and other enteric infections, fracture risk and rebound acid hypersecretion on withdrawal.`,`Pantoprazole is the most acid-stable of the group and is available as a stable intravenous formulation, useful in upper gastrointestinal bleeding.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Inflammatory bowel disease`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Class`,`Comment`],rows:[[`Sulfasalazine`,`5-ASA prodrug`,`Azo bond split by colonic bacteria to 5-ASA plus sulfapyridine`],[`Mesalazine`,`5-ASA`,`Delivered by pH or time-dependent coating, fewer sulfa effects`],[`Balsalazide, olsalazine`,`5-ASA prodrug`,`Colon-targeted delivery`],[`Prednisolone, budesonide`,`Corticosteroid`,`Induce remission; budesonide has high first-pass loss`],[`Azathioprine, 6-mercaptopurine`,`Thiopurine`,`Steroid-sparing maintenance; check TPMT activity`],[`Methotrexate`,`Antimetabolite`,`Maintenance in Crohn disease`],[`Infliximab, adalimumab`,`Anti-TNF biologic`,`Fistulising and refractory disease`],[`Vedolizumab`,`Anti-integrin`,`Gut-selective alpha4beta7 blockade`]],caption:`The active moiety of sulfasalazine in bowel disease is 5-aminosalicylic acid; the sulfapyridine carrier causes most of the toxicity.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Theophylline: a narrow therapeutic index drug`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Therapeutic plasma range is about 10 to 20 micrograms/mL. Above 20 you see nausea and vomiting; above 35 to 40 arrhythmias and seizures.`,`Mechanisms: non-selective phosphodiesterase inhibition raising cAMP, adenosine A1 and A2 receptor antagonism, and at low concentration histone deacetylase activation restoring steroid sensitivity.`,`Metabolised mainly by CYP1A2. Smoking and rifampicin induce it and lower levels; erythromycin, ciprofloxacin, cimetidine and heart failure raise levels.`,`It shows dose-dependent (Michaelis-Menten) kinetics near the top of the therapeutic range, so a small dose increase can cause a disproportionate rise in plasma level.`,`Other actions: cardiac stimulation, diuresis, CNS stimulation, and stimulation of the respiratory centre, which is why aminophylline was used in neonatal apnoea.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Aminophylline is not theophylline`,text:`Aminophylline is theophylline with ethylenediamine, added purely for water solubility so it can be injected. Roughly 100 mg of aminophylline supplies about 80 mg of theophylline, so doses are not interchangeable milligram for milligram.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`COPD versus asthma`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Feature`,`Asthma`,`COPD`],rows:[[`Obstruction`,`Largely reversible`,`Largely irreversible`],[`Dominant cell`,`Eosinophil, mast cell`,`Neutrophil, macrophage`],[`First-line maintenance`,`Inhaled corticosteroid`,`LAMA or LABA bronchodilator`],[`Steroid response`,`Good`,`Limited, reserved for exacerbations and eosinophilic phenotype`],[`Extra options`,`Leukotriene antagonist, anti-IgE`,`Roflumilast (PDE-4), azithromycin prophylaxis, oxygen`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cough drugs`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Central antitussives: codeine and pholcodine (opioid), dextromethorphan and noscapine (non-opioid). They raise the cough centre threshold.`,`Peripheral antitussives: benzonatate and demulcents such as glycerine and liquorice.`,`Expectorants increase the volume and reduce the viscosity of secretions: guaifenesin, ammonium chloride, potassium iodide, sodium citrate.`,`Mucolytics break disulfide bridges in mucus: acetylcysteine, carbocisteine, bromhexine and its metabolite ambroxol.`,`Never suppress a productive cough with a central antitussive; clearing sputum is the point.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Acetylcysteine has two lives`,text:`The same molecule is a mucolytic by cleaving mucus disulfide bonds and the antidote in paracetamol poisoning by replenishing hepatic glutathione. Examiners like this dual role.`}]},{topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`A diuretic is a drug that makes the kidney excrete more sodium and water, so more urine is passed. They are used when the body is holding too much fluid, as in heart failure, and to lower blood pressure.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`How the nephron handles salt`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The nephron is the working unit of the kidney. Blood is filtered at the glomerulus, and the tubule then reabsorbs most of what was filtered.`,`About 65 percent of filtered sodium is taken back in the proximal tubule, about 25 percent in the thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle, about 5 to 10 percent in the distal tubule and about 2 to 3 percent in the collecting duct.`,`A diuretic works by blocking one of these reabsorption steps. The further upstream and the larger the share of sodium handled there, the stronger the diuretic.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The main groups`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Loop diuretics such as furosemide act on the loop of Henle. They are the most powerful and act fast, so they are used in acute pulmonary oedema.`,`Thiazides such as hydrochlorothiazide act on the distal tubule. They are moderate in strength and are widely used for high blood pressure.`,`Potassium-sparing diuretics such as spironolactone and amiloride are weak but stop potassium being lost.`,`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors such as acetazolamide are weak diuretics used mainly for glaucoma and mountain sickness.`,`Osmotic diuretics such as mannitol pull water out by osmosis and are used for raised pressure in the brain or eye.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Potassium is the thing to watch`,text:`Loop diuretics and thiazides make the body lose potassium, which can cause muscle weakness and dangerous heart rhythms. Spironolactone and amiloride do the opposite and can cause potassium to build up. That is why the two groups are often combined.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Common uses`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Heart failure and fluid overload: loop diuretics.`,`High blood pressure: thiazides, usually with another antihypertensive.`,`Liver disease with abdominal fluid (ascites): spironolactone.`,`Glaucoma: acetazolamide. Raised pressure inside the skull: mannitol.`,`Recurrent calcium kidney stones: thiazides, because they reduce calcium in the urine.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Spironolactone spares potassium`,text:`Spare rhymes with spironolactone and amiloride is in the same group. Everything else in the diuretic list wastes potassium.`}]},{topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable core is a single map: which transporter each class blocks, at which nephron segment, with what efficacy and which electrolyte disturbance follows.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Site and target of each class`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Nephron site`,`Molecular target`,`Max Na excretion`],rows:[[`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor`,`Proximal convoluted tubule`,`Carbonic anhydrase`,`About 5 percent`],[`Osmotic diuretic`,`PCT and descending limb`,`None, osmotic effect`,`Variable`],[`Loop diuretic`,`Thick ascending limb`,`Na-K-2Cl (NKCC2) symporter`,`15 to 25 percent`],[`Thiazide`,`Distal convoluted tubule`,`Na-Cl (NCC) symporter`,`5 to 10 percent`],[`Aldosterone antagonist`,`Late DT and collecting duct`,`Mineralocorticoid receptor`,`2 to 3 percent`],[`ENaC blocker`,`Collecting duct`,`Epithelial sodium channel`,`2 to 3 percent`],[`SGLT2 inhibitor`,`Proximal tubule`,`SGLT2 glucose transporter`,`Mild osmotic diuresis`],[`Vaptan`,`Collecting duct`,`V2 vasopressin receptor`,`Water only (aquaretic)`]],caption:`Loop diuretics are called high-ceiling because the dose-response curve keeps rising; thiazides are low-ceiling and plateau early.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Drugs in each class`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitors: acetazolamide, dorzolamide (topical), brinzolamide, methazolamide.`,`Loop: furosemide, bumetanide, torasemide, ethacrynic acid (the only non-sulfonamide, useful in sulfa allergy).`,`Thiazide and thiazide-like: hydrochlorothiazide, chlorthalidone, indapamide, metolazone.`,`Aldosterone antagonists: spironolactone, eplerenone (more selective, less gynaecomastia).`,`ENaC blockers: amiloride, triamterene.`,`Osmotic: mannitol, isosorbide, glycerol, urea.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Electrolyte and metabolic effects`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Potassium`,`Calcium in urine`,`Acid-base`,`Other`],rows:[[`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor`,`Hypokalaemia`,`Increased`,`Metabolic acidosis`,`Alkaline urine, phosphaturia`],[`Loop`,`Hypokalaemia`,`Increased (hypocalcaemia)`,`Metabolic alkalosis`,`Hypomagnesaemia, ototoxicity`],[`Thiazide`,`Hypokalaemia`,`Decreased (hypercalcaemia)`,`Metabolic alkalosis`,`Hyperglycaemia, hyperuricaemia, hyperlipidaemia, hyponatraemia`],[`Aldosterone antagonist`,`Hyperkalaemia`,`Little change`,`Mild acidosis`,`Gynaecomastia with spironolactone`],[`ENaC blocker`,`Hyperkalaemia`,`Little change`,`Mild acidosis`,`Triamterene can form stones`],[`Osmotic`,`Variable loss`,`—`,`—`,`Initial plasma volume expansion`]],caption:`Loops lose calcium, thiazides keep it. This single opposition is the most asked diuretic fact.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Loop loses calcium, thiazide takes it back`,text:`Loop diuretics cause hypocalcaemia and are used in hypercalcaemia; thiazides cause hypercalcaemia and are used to prevent calcium oxalate stones and in idiopathic hypercalciuria.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Clinical uses by class`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Main uses`],rows:[[`Loop`,`Acute pulmonary oedema, heart failure, hypercalcaemia, forced diuresis in poisoning, oedema with renal failure`],[`Thiazide`,`Hypertension, mild heart failure, calcium stones, nephrogenic diabetes insipidus`],[`Aldosterone antagonist`,`Cirrhotic ascites, heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, resistant hypertension, primary hyperaldosteronism`],[`ENaC blocker`,`Added to prevent potassium loss, Liddle syndrome`],[`Carbonic anhydrase inhibitor`,`Glaucoma, acute mountain sickness, epilepsy adjunct, alkalinisation of urine`],[`Osmotic`,`Raised intracranial pressure, acute glaucoma, prevention of anuria in haemolysis`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Diabetes insipidus`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Type`,`Defect`,`Treatment`],rows:[[`Central (neurogenic)`,`ADH not secreted by posterior pituitary`,`Desmopressin (DDAVP), a V2 agonist`],[`Nephrogenic`,`Collecting duct does not respond to ADH`,`Thiazides plus low salt diet, amiloride, indomethacin`]],caption:`Lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is specifically treated with amiloride, which blocks lithium entry through ENaC.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`A diuretic that treats too much urine`,text:`Thiazides paradoxically reduce urine volume in nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Sodium depletion increases proximal reabsorption of salt and water, so less fluid reaches the collecting duct where the concentrating defect lies.`}]},{topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced points: how diuretics reach their luminal target, why alkalosis develops, the mechanism of diuretic resistance, and specific toxicities.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Access to the target`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Loop diuretics and thiazides act from the luminal side but are highly protein bound, so they are barely filtered. They reach the lumen by active secretion in the proximal tubule through the organic anion transporter OAT1.`,`Probenecid and high plasma urate compete for OAT1 secretion and blunt the diuretic response. NSAIDs also reduce the response by removing the vasodilator prostaglandins that support renal blood flow.`,`Spironolactone is the exception: it acts on an intracellular receptor from the blood side and does not need tubular secretion. It is also a prodrug converted to canrenone, so its onset takes 2 to 4 days.`,`Furosemide has erratic oral bioavailability of roughly 10 to 90 percent; torasemide and bumetanide are close to 80 to 100 percent and more predictable.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why loops and thiazides cause alkalosis and potassium loss`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`The diuretic blocks sodium reabsorption upstream, so more sodium is delivered to the collecting duct.`,`Volume loss activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, raising aldosterone.`,`Aldosterone drives sodium uptake through ENaC in the principal cell, creating a lumen-negative potential.`,`That potential drives secretion of potassium through ROMK and of hydrogen ions through the intercalated cell proton pump.`,`The result is hypokalaemic hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis, with contraction of extracellular volume concentrating the bicarbonate further.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`The loop diuretic also kills the countercurrent gradient`,text:`Blocking NKCC2 abolishes the medullary hypertonicity that concentrates urine, so a loop diuretic impairs both concentration and dilution of urine. A thiazide only impairs dilution, which is why thiazides are the classic cause of drug-induced hyponatraemia.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Diuretic resistance and braking`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The braking phenomenon: after the first doses, distal nephron segments hypertrophy and reabsorb more sodium, blunting the effect.`,`Sequential nephron blockade counters it: add metolazone or a thiazide to a loop diuretic so the distal escape route is also blocked. This combination is potent and can cause severe hypokalaemia.`,`In hypoalbuminaemia and nephrotic syndrome less drug is delivered to OAT1 and filtered albumin binds the diuretic in the tubule, so higher doses or continuous infusion are needed.`,`In renal failure, accumulated organic anions compete for secretion, so loop doses must be increased rather than switched to a thiazide, which becomes ineffective below an eGFR of about 30.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Specific toxicities`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Toxicity`,`Note`],rows:[[`Furosemide`,`Dose-related ototoxicity`,`Worse with rapid IV push and with aminoglycosides`],[`Ethacrynic acid`,`Highest ototoxicity, often irreversible`,`Non-sulfonamide, usable in sulfa allergy`],[`Thiazides`,`Hyperuricaemia, hyperglycaemia, hyponatraemia`,`Avoid in gout; reduce insulin secretion via K-ATP`],[`Spironolactone`,`Gynaecomastia, impotence, menstrual upset`,`Progesterone and androgen receptor cross-reactivity`],[`Acetazolamide`,`Metabolic acidosis, paraesthesia, stones`,`Self-limiting diuretic effect once acidosis develops`],[`Mannitol`,`Acute volume expansion, pulmonary oedema`,`Contraindicated in established anuria and heart failure`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Acetazolamide is self-limiting`,text:`Its diuresis stops after two or three days because bicarbonate is depleted and the resulting metabolic acidosis removes its own substrate. Efficacy returns after a few days off the drug, so intermittent dosing is used.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Mannitol is filtered but not reabsorbed and is not metabolised; it must be given intravenously and is contraindicated in active intracranial bleeding and in anuria unresponsive to a test dose.`,`Tolvaptan and conivaptan are V2 antagonists producing pure water loss; they are used in euvolaemic and hypervolaemic hyponatraemia, correcting sodium no faster than about 8 to 10 mmol/L per day to avoid osmotic demyelination.`]}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Antibacterial chemotherapy means using drugs that kill or stop bacteria while doing as little harm as possible to the patient. The idea of selective toxicity is that the drug attacks a structure the bacterium has and we do not, such as the cell wall.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Two ways to describe a drug`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Bactericidal drugs kill bacteria outright: penicillins, cephalosporins, aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, vancomycin, metronidazole, rifampicin.`,`Bacteriostatic drugs stop bacteria multiplying so the immune system can clear them: tetracyclines, macrolides, sulfonamides, chloramphenicol, clindamycin, linezolid.`,`Narrow spectrum means active against few organisms; broad spectrum means active against many, including both gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`What the drug attacks`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Target`,`Drug groups`],rows:[[`Cell wall`,`Penicillins, cephalosporins, carbapenems, vancomycin, bacitracin`],[`Protein synthesis`,`Aminoglycosides, tetracyclines, macrolides, chloramphenicol, clindamycin, linezolid`],[`Nucleic acid`,`Fluoroquinolones, rifampicin, metronidazole`],[`Folate pathway`,`Sulfonamides, trimethoprim`],[`Cell membrane`,`Polymyxins, daptomycin`]]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Why resistance happens`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Bacteria make enzymes that destroy the drug, such as beta-lactamase which breaks penicillin.`,`They change the target so the drug no longer fits.`,`They pump the drug back out or stop it entering.`,`Taking too small a dose, stopping early, or using antibiotics for viral illness all encourage resistance.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Tetracyclines and children`,text:`Tetracyclines bind calcium in growing teeth and bone, so they stain teeth permanently and are avoided in children under 8 years, in pregnancy and in lactation.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Which drugs are bacteriostatic`,text:`ECSTaCLM: Erythromycin, Clindamycin, Sulfonamides, Tetracyclines, Chloramphenicol, Linezolid, Macrolides. Most of these are protein synthesis inhibitors, and aminoglycosides are the famous exception because they are cidal.`}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This is the highest-yield table topic in pharmacology. Learn the class, its exact molecular target, its spectrum, its signature toxicity, and the drug of choice for each organism.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Protein synthesis: 30S versus 50S`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Subunit`,`Drugs`,`Exact action`],rows:[[`30S`,`Aminoglycosides`,`Bind 30S, cause misreading of mRNA; bactericidal`],[`30S`,`Tetracyclines, tigecycline`,`Block aminoacyl-tRNA binding to the A site`],[`50S`,`Macrolides, clindamycin`,`Block translocation at the P site`],[`50S`,`Chloramphenicol`,`Inhibits peptidyl transferase`],[`50S`,`Linezolid`,`Prevents formation of the 70S initiation complex`],[`50S`,`Streptogramins`,`Bind adjacent 50S sites, synergistic`]],caption:`Mnemonic for 30S: buy AT 30, CCEL at 50 (Aminoglycosides, Tetracyclines at 30S; Chloramphenicol, Clindamycin, Erythromycin, Linezolid at 50S).`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Penicillins`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Drugs`,`Spectrum`],rows:[[`Natural`,`Benzyl penicillin, penicillin V`,`Gram-positive cocci, spirochaetes; acid-labile except V`],[`Penicillinase resistant`,`Cloxacillin, methicillin, nafcillin`,`Staphylococcus aureus producing penicillinase`],[`Aminopenicillin`,`Ampicillin, amoxicillin`,`Extended, adds many gram-negatives, H. influenzae`],[`Carboxypenicillin`,`Carbenicillin, ticarcillin`,`Antipseudomonal`],[`Ureidopenicillin`,`Piperacillin, mezlocillin`,`Antipseudomonal, broadest`],[`Beta-lactamase inhibitor`,`Clavulanic acid, sulbactam, tazobactam`,`No useful antibacterial action alone`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`All beta-lactams bind penicillin binding proteins and inhibit transpeptidase, the enzyme that cross-links peptidoglycan. They also activate autolysins, so the cell lyses.`,`They are bactericidal only against actively dividing organisms, which is why combining them with a bacteriostatic drug can be antagonistic.`,`Probenecid blocks tubular secretion of penicillin and prolongs its action.`,`The commonest serious adverse effect is hypersensitivity, from rash to anaphylaxis; there is partial cross-reactivity with cephalosporins.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cephalosporin generations`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Generation`,`Examples`,`Feature`],rows:[[`First`,`Cefazolin, cefalexin, cefadroxil`,`Best gram-positive cover; surgical prophylaxis`],[`Second`,`Cefuroxime, cefaclor, cefoxitin`,`More gram-negative; cefoxitin covers anaerobes`],[`Third`,`Ceftriaxone, cefotaxime, ceftazidime, cefixime`,`Strong gram-negative, CSF penetration; ceftazidime covers Pseudomonas`],[`Fourth`,`Cefepime, cefpirome`,`Both gram-positive and gram-negative, stable to many beta-lactamases`],[`Fifth`,`Ceftaroline, ceftobiprole`,`Active against MRSA`]],caption:`Moving up the generations, gram-negative cover and beta-lactamase stability increase while gram-positive cover dips in the middle generations.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Other cell wall agents`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Carbapenems (imipenem, meropenem, ertapenem, doripenem) have the broadest spectrum of all beta-lactams. Imipenem is given with cilastatin, an inhibitor of renal dehydropeptidase I, to prevent its breakdown. Imipenem lowers the seizure threshold; ertapenem lacks antipseudomonal activity.`,`Aztreonam is the only monobactam. It is active only against aerobic gram-negative organisms and does not cross-react with penicillin allergy.`,`Vancomycin binds the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of the peptidoglycan precursor, one step before transpeptidation. It is given intravenously for MRSA and orally (not absorbed) for C. difficile colitis. Rapid infusion causes red man syndrome from histamine release, which is a rate-related reaction, not an allergy.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Signature toxicities`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug or class`,`Signature adverse effect`],rows:[[`Aminoglycosides`,`Nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity, neuromuscular blockade`],[`Tetracyclines`,`Teeth staining, photosensitivity, hepatotoxicity in pregnancy`],[`Chloramphenicol`,`Aplastic anaemia (idiosyncratic), grey baby syndrome`],[`Clindamycin`,`Pseudomembranous colitis (C. difficile)`],[`Fluoroquinolones`,`Tendinitis and tendon rupture, QT prolongation, cartilage damage in the young`],[`Sulfonamides`,`Stevens-Johnson syndrome, crystalluria, kernicterus in neonates`],[`Metronidazole`,`Metallic taste, disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol`],[`Linezolid`,`Thrombocytopenia, serotonin syndrome with SSRIs`],[`Nitrofurantoin`,`Pulmonary fibrosis, haemolysis in G6PD deficiency`],[`Polymyxin B, colistin`,`Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Cotrimoxazole is sequential blockade`,text:`Sulfamethoxazole blocks dihydropteroate synthase and trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase, two consecutive steps of the same folate pathway. The result is synergy and a bactericidal effect from two bacteriostatic drugs, in a fixed 5 to 1 ratio giving about 20 to 1 in plasma.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Drug of choice by organism`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Organism or condition`,`Drug of choice`],rows:[[`Streptococcus pyogenes, syphilis`,`Benzyl penicillin`],[`MRSA`,`Vancomycin (or linezolid, daptomycin)`],[`Pseudomonas aeruginosa`,`Piperacillin-tazobactam or ceftazidime, often with an aminoglycoside`],[`Typhoid fever`,`Ceftriaxone or azithromycin`],[`Anaerobes below the diaphragm`,`Metronidazole`],[`Mycoplasma, Chlamydia, Legionella`,`Macrolide or doxycycline`],[`Cholera`,`Doxycycline plus rehydration`],[`Uncomplicated urinary infection`,`Nitrofurantoin or cotrimoxazole`],[`Clostridioides difficile colitis`,`Oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin`],[`Gas gangrene`,`Penicillin plus clindamycin`]]}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced work: pharmacodynamic dosing categories, the molecular basis of resistance, aminoglycoside kinetics including the post-antibiotic effect, quinolone generations and chemoprophylaxis rules.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Concentration-dependent versus time-dependent killing`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Pattern`,`Best PK-PD index`,`Drugs`,`Dosing consequence`],rows:[[`Concentration-dependent`,`Cmax to MIC ratio`,`Aminoglycosides, fluoroquinolones, daptomycin`,`Large infrequent doses, once daily`],[`Time-dependent`,`Time above MIC`,`Beta-lactams`,`Frequent dosing or extended infusion`],[`Time-dependent with long persistence`,`AUC to MIC ratio`,`Vancomycin, macrolides, tetracyclines, linezolid`,`Total daily exposure matters most`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`The post-antibiotic effect is continued suppression of bacterial growth after the drug concentration has fallen below the MIC. It is prolonged for aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones and short or absent for beta-lactams against gram-negatives.`,`Once-daily aminoglycoside dosing exploits concentration-dependent killing and the post-antibiotic effect while allowing a trough low enough to let the saturable renal cortical uptake reverse, reducing nephrotoxicity.`,`Aminoglycosides are polycationic and highly polar, so they are not absorbed orally, do not enter the CSF, are not metabolised, and are cleared entirely by glomerular filtration.`,`Their uptake into bacteria needs an oxygen-dependent transport step, which is why they are inactive against anaerobes and why they synergise with cell wall agents that ease entry.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Vestibular versus cochlear damage`,text:`Streptomycin and gentamicin damage the vestibular apparatus (vertigo, ataxia); amikacin, kanamycin and neomycin are more cochlear (deafness). Neomycin is the most nephrotoxic and is restricted to topical and oral bowel preparation use.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Resistance mechanisms in detail`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mechanism`,`Example`],rows:[[`Enzymatic destruction`,`Beta-lactamases, ESBLs, carbapenemases (NDM-1, KPC)`],[`Enzymatic modification`,`Aminoglycoside acetyl-, adenylyl- and phosphotransferases; chloramphenicol acetyltransferase`],[`Target alteration`,`PBP2a from mecA gene in MRSA; D-Ala-D-Lac in VRE; altered DNA gyrase; ribosomal methylase erm gene`],[`Reduced permeability`,`Loss of OprD porin causing carbapenem resistance in Pseudomonas`],[`Efflux pumps`,`Tet efflux for tetracyclines, MexAB-OprM in Pseudomonas`],[`Bypass pathway`,`Plasmid-encoded dihydrofolate reductase insensitive to trimethoprim`]],caption:`Transfer is by conjugation (plasmids), transduction (phage) or transformation. Conjugation spreads multidrug resistance fastest.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Fluoroquinolone generations`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Generation`,`Examples`,`Spectrum note`],rows:[[`First`,`Nalidixic acid`,`Gram-negative urinary only, no systemic level`],[`Second`,`Norfloxacin, ciprofloxacin, ofloxacin`,`Gram-negative plus Pseudomonas; cipro is the most active against Pseudomonas`],[`Third`,`Levofloxacin, sparfloxacin`,`Added gram-positive and atypical (respiratory quinolones)`],[`Fourth`,`Moxifloxacin, gatifloxacin`,`Adds anaerobes; moxifloxacin has negligible urinary excretion`]],caption:`They inhibit DNA gyrase (topoisomerase II) in gram-negatives and topoisomerase IV in gram-positives.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Divalent and trivalent cations in antacids, iron, calcium and sucralfate chelate quinolones and tetracyclines, cutting absorption; separate doses by at least 2 hours.`,`Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and raises theophylline and caffeine levels.`,`Moxifloxacin is excreted mainly by the liver, so it is unsuitable for urinary infection.`,`Doxycycline and minocycline are the tetracyclines safest in renal impairment because elimination is largely non-renal.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chloramphenicol and grey baby syndrome`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Chloramphenicol is inactivated by hepatic glucuronidation. Neonates have immature UDP-glucuronyl transferase and immature renal excretion of the conjugate.`,`The drug accumulates and causes vomiting, abdominal distension, hypothermia, flaccidity, an ashen grey colour and cardiovascular collapse.`,`Its dose-related reversible bone marrow depression must be distinguished from the rare idiosyncratic irreversible aplastic anaemia, which is not dose related and can follow even topical use.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Chemoprophylaxis you must know`,text:`Rheumatic fever: benzathine penicillin. Meningococcal contacts: rifampicin or ciprofloxacin. Surgical: cefazolin within 60 minutes of incision. Recurrent urinary infection: low-dose nitrofurantoin or cotrimoxazole. Post-splenectomy: penicillin V.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Metronidazole is a prodrug reduced by ferredoxin in anaerobes and protozoa to a nitro radical that fragments DNA, which is exactly why it is inactive against aerobes.`,`Nitrofurantoin concentrates in urine and reaches no useful plasma level, so it treats cystitis but never pyelonephritis or systemic infection; it is ineffective when creatinine clearance falls below about 45 to 60 mL/min.`,`Linezolid is a monoamine oxidase inhibitor, hence the tyramine and SSRI interactions, and cross-resistance with other protein synthesis inhibitors is rare because its binding site is unique.`]}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`This topic covers drugs for organisms that are harder to kill than ordinary bacteria: the tubercle bacillus with its waxy wall, fungi whose cells resemble ours, viruses that live inside our cells, and protozoal parasites such as the malaria parasite.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Tuberculosis`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Tuberculosis is caused by Mycobacterium tuberculosis, a slow-growing organism with a thick lipid-rich cell wall that ordinary antibiotics cannot penetrate.`,`Treatment always uses several drugs together, because a single drug quickly selects resistant bacilli.`,`The first-line drugs are isoniazid, rifampicin, pyrazinamide and ethambutol, remembered as HRZE.`,`Treatment lasts at least six months: an intensive phase of about two months with four drugs, then a continuation phase of about four months with fewer drugs.`,`DOTS means directly observed treatment, short course. A health worker watches the patient swallow the tablets so that doses are not missed and resistance does not develop.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rifampicin turns body fluids orange`,text:`Urine, sweat, tears and saliva become orange-red. It is harmless but soft contact lenses stain permanently. Warning the patient in advance prevents them from stopping the drug in alarm.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Fungal infections`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Fungal cells have a membrane containing ergosterol where our cells have cholesterol, and a cell wall of glucan. Both are drug targets.`,`Amphotericin B binds ergosterol and punches holes in the fungal membrane. It is powerful but toxic to the kidney.`,`Azoles such as fluconazole and itraconazole stop the fungus making ergosterol.`,`Griseofulvin and terbinafine are used for skin, hair and nail infections.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Viruses and HIV`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Aciclovir treats herpes simplex, chickenpox and shingles. Oseltamivir treats influenza.`,`HIV is treated with a combination of at least three drugs, called HAART or antiretroviral therapy. The combination suppresses the virus and prevents resistance but does not cure the infection.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Malaria in one paragraph`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Malaria is caused by Plasmodium parasites carried by the Anopheles mosquito.`,`Chloroquine kills the parasite in red blood cells. Artemisinin-based combination therapy is now the standard treatment for falciparum malaria.`,`Primaquine is added to kill the dormant liver forms of P. vivax so the illness does not come back.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`First-line TB drugs`,text:`RIPE: Rifampicin, Isoniazid, Pyrazinamide, Ethambutol. Add streptomycin and you have the classical five.`}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`The examinable heart of this topic is four tables: antitubercular drugs with mechanism and signature toxicity, antifungal classes by target, antiretroviral classes, and antimalarials by life-cycle stage.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`First-line antitubercular drugs`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Mechanism`,`Signature adverse effect`,`Cidal or static`],rows:[[`Isoniazid (H)`,`Inhibits InhA, blocks mycolic acid synthesis`,`Peripheral neuropathy, hepatitis, lupus-like syndrome`,`Cidal for dividing bacilli`],[`Rifampicin (R)`,`Inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase`,`Hepatitis, orange secretions, flu-like syndrome, enzyme induction`,`Cidal, including dormant bacilli`],[`Pyrazinamide (Z)`,`Prodrug to pyrazinoic acid, acts in acidic phagosome`,`Hepatotoxicity, hyperuricaemia and gout, arthralgia`,`Cidal in acid pH`],[`Ethambutol (E)`,`Inhibits arabinosyl transferase, blocks arabinogalactan`,`Retrobulbar optic neuritis, loss of red-green vision`,`Static`],[`Streptomycin (S)`,`Binds 30S ribosome`,`Ototoxicity (vestibular), nephrotoxicity`,`Cidal, extracellular only`]],caption:`Isoniazid and rifampicin are the two indispensable drugs; pyrazinamide is what shortens therapy from 9 months to 6.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Pyridoxine with isoniazid`,text:`Isoniazid is structurally similar to pyridoxine and increases its urinary excretion, causing peripheral neuropathy. Pyridoxine 10 to 25 mg daily prevents it, and is given routinely to malnourished patients, alcoholics, diabetics, pregnant women and those with HIV.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Isoniazid is acetylated by N-acetyltransferase 2. Slow acetylators are more prone to neuropathy and lupus-like reactions; fast acetylators may need attention to dosing.`,`Rifampicin is a powerful inducer of CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein. It lowers levels of oral contraceptives, warfarin, protease inhibitors, ciclosporin, sulfonylureas and phenytoin.`,`Ethambutol requires a baseline colour vision check and dose reduction in renal impairment; the optic neuritis is dose related and usually reversible if caught early.`,`Pyrazinamide is the drug most likely to raise uric acid because pyrazinoic acid blocks urate secretion in the proximal tubule.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Regimens and resistance`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Standard new-case regimen: two months of HRZE (intensive phase) then four months of HRE or HR (continuation phase), given daily in the current Indian programme.`,`Second-line and MDR-TB drugs: fluoroquinolones (levofloxacin, moxifloxacin), bedaquiline, linezolid, clofazimine, cycloserine, ethionamide, injectable amikacin or capreomycin, and delamanid.`,`MDR-TB means resistance to at least isoniazid and rifampicin. XDR-TB adds resistance to a fluoroquinolone and at least one further group A drug.`,`The Indian programme (now the National TB Elimination Programme) provides free diagnosis and treatment, uses fixed-dose combinations, and relies on direct observation and adherence support.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Leprosy`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Multidrug therapy is used to prevent dapsone resistance. Paucibacillary: rifampicin monthly plus dapsone daily for 6 months. Multibacillary: rifampicin monthly, clofazimine, and dapsone daily for 12 months.`,`Dapsone inhibits dihydropteroate synthase and causes haemolysis, especially in G6PD deficiency, and methaemoglobinaemia.`,`Clofazimine causes reddish-black skin pigmentation and also has anti-inflammatory value in erythema nodosum leprosum, where thalidomide is the most effective drug.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antifungal classes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Target`],rows:[[`Polyene`,`Amphotericin B, nystatin`,`Binds ergosterol, forms membrane pores`],[`Azole`,`Fluconazole, itraconazole, voriconazole, ketoconazole`,`Inhibits 14-alpha-demethylase (CYP51)`],[`Echinocandin`,`Caspofungin, micafungin, anidulafungin`,`Inhibits beta-1,3-glucan synthase in the cell wall`],[`Allylamine`,`Terbinafine`,`Inhibits squalene epoxidase`],[`Antimetabolite`,`Flucytosine`,`Converted to 5-fluorouracil, blocks DNA and RNA synthesis`],[`Antimitotic`,`Griseofulvin`,`Disrupts fungal microtubules, deposits in keratin`]],caption:`Only the echinocandins act on the cell wall; every other class acts on the membrane or inside the cell.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antiretroviral classes`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`NRTI`,`Zidovudine, lamivudine, tenofovir, abacavir`,`Chain terminator after phosphorylation`],[`NNRTI`,`Nevirapine, efavirenz, rilpivirine`,`Allosteric non-competitive block of reverse transcriptase`],[`Protease inhibitor`,`Ritonavir, lopinavir, atazanavir, darunavir`,`Block viral protease, immature non-infectious virions`],[`Integrase inhibitor`,`Dolutegravir, raltegravir, bictegravir`,`Block strand transfer into host DNA`],[`Entry or fusion inhibitor`,`Enfuvirtide (gp41), maraviroc (CCR5)`,`Block viral entry`]],caption:`HAART uses at least three drugs from two or more classes, typically two NRTIs plus an integrase inhibitor, to suppress viral load and prevent resistance.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Ritonavir is used as a booster`,text:`At low dose ritonavir is not there for antiviral effect but as a potent CYP3A4 inhibitor that raises the level of the companion protease inhibitor. Cobicistat is a pure booster with no antiviral activity at all.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antimalarials by parasite stage`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Action`,`Stage attacked`,`Drugs`],rows:[[`Tissue schizonticide (causal)`,`Pre-erythrocytic liver stage`,`Primaquine, proguanil`],[`Hypnozoiticide (radical cure)`,`Dormant liver forms of vivax and ovale`,`Primaquine, tafenoquine`],[`Blood schizonticide`,`Erythrocytic asexual forms`,`Chloroquine, artemisinins, quinine, mefloquine`],[`Gametocidal`,`Sexual forms, blocks transmission`,`Primaquine, artemisinins`],[`Sporontocidal`,`Development in the mosquito`,`Primaquine, proguanil`]]}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced material: prodrug activation and resistance genes in TB, amphotericin toxicity and lipid formulations, selective activation of antiviral prodrugs, and the chemistry behind artemisinin and primaquine.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Molecular detail in tuberculosis`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Isoniazid is a prodrug activated by the mycobacterial catalase-peroxidase KatG. Mutation or deletion of katG is the commonest cause of isoniazid resistance; inhA promoter mutations give lower-level resistance.`,`Rifampicin resistance is almost always a point mutation in rpoB, the beta subunit of RNA polymerase. Because it is a single well-defined locus, molecular tests such as cartridge-based nucleic acid amplification detect it rapidly, and rifampicin resistance is used as a surrogate marker for MDR-TB.`,`Pyrazinamide is activated by the bacterial enzyme pyrazinamidase encoded by pncA; loss of pncA causes resistance.`,`Rifampicin is uniquely able to kill semi-dormant bacilli in caseous lesions with only short bursts of metabolism, which is why it allows short-course therapy.`,`Bedaquiline inhibits mycobacterial ATP synthase, an entirely new target, and prolongs the QT interval.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Rifampicin plus isoniazid hepatotoxicity`,text:`Isoniazid hepatotoxicity is caused by its metabolite acetylhydrazine. Rifampicin induces the enzymes that generate it, so the combination is more hepatotoxic than either drug alone, particularly in slow acetylators and in the elderly.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Amphotericin B and the azoles`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Amphotericin B has infusion-related toxicity (fever, rigors, hypotension) and dose-related nephrotoxicity with potassium and magnesium wasting and renal tubular acidosis.`,`Liposomal and lipid-complex formulations deliver the same drug with much less nephrotoxicity by keeping it away from renal tubular cholesterol; they are far more expensive.`,`Flucytosine is combined with amphotericin B in cryptococcal meningitis: amphotericin increases flucytosine entry, and the pair prevents rapid emergence of flucytosine resistance. Flucytosine causes bone marrow suppression.`,`Azoles inhibit human CYP enzymes to differing degrees. Ketoconazole is the worst offender and also inhibits steroid synthesis, causing gynaecomastia. Fluconazole has excellent CSF penetration and is largely renally excreted. Voriconazole causes reversible visual disturbance. Itraconazole is negatively inotropic and avoided in heart failure.`,`Terbinafine and griseofulvin both concentrate in keratin; terbinafine is fungicidal for dermatophytes while griseofulvin is fungistatic and needs a fatty meal for absorption.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Selective activation of antivirals`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Aciclovir is monophosphorylated only by herpesvirus thymidine kinase, so it is concentrated inside infected cells.`,`Host cellular kinases add the second and third phosphate.`,`Aciclovir triphosphate inhibits viral DNA polymerase and acts as a chain terminator because it lacks a 3-hydroxyl group.`,`This two-step selectivity explains its remarkably low toxicity; crystalluria with rapid intravenous dosing is the main risk.`,`Ganciclovir is activated by the CMV UL97 kinase and is far more myelosuppressive; foscarnet and cidofovir need no viral kinase and are used in thymidine kinase deficient resistant strains.`]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Oseltamivir and zanamivir are neuraminidase inhibitors that prevent release of new influenza virions; they must be started within 48 hours of symptom onset. Amantadine blocks the M2 ion channel and is now largely useless because of resistance.`,`Chronic hepatitis B: tenofovir and entecavir. Chronic hepatitis C: direct-acting antivirals such as sofosbuvir (NS5B polymerase), ledipasvir and daclatasvir (NS5A), and the previr group (NS3/4A protease).`,`Lamivudine and tenofovir are active against both HIV and hepatitis B, so hepatitis B status must be known before starting or stopping them to avoid a flare.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antimalarial chemistry and pitfalls`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Chloroquine is a weak base concentrated several thousand-fold in the acidic food vacuole, where it prevents polymerisation of toxic haem into inert haemozoin. Resistance is due to the PfCRT transporter pumping the drug out.`,`Artemisinins contain an endoperoxide bridge that is cleaved by haem iron to generate carbon-centred free radicals that alkylate parasite proteins. They give the fastest parasite clearance of any antimalarial but have a very short half-life.`,`Because of that short half-life, artemisinin is always paired with a longer-acting partner in ACT (artemether-lumefantrine, artesunate plus sulfadoxine-pyrimethamine) to clear residual parasites and prevent resistance. Monotherapy is prohibited.`,`Primaquine is an 8-aminoquinoline whose oxidative metabolites cause haemolysis in G6PD deficiency, so screening is advised before radical cure. It is also contraindicated in pregnancy.`,`Quinine causes cinchonism (tinnitus, headache, blurred vision, nausea) and can cause hypoglycaemia by stimulating pancreatic insulin release; mefloquine causes neuropsychiatric reactions.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Chloroquine does not cure vivax`,text:`Chloroquine clears the blood stage and stops the fever, but the hypnozoites in the liver survive and cause relapse weeks or months later. Only primaquine (or tafenoquine) gives radical cure, which is why the two are prescribed together in P. vivax malaria.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antiamoebics and anthelmintics`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Use`,`Mechanism or note`],rows:[[`Metronidazole, tinidazole`,`Tissue amoebiasis, giardiasis, trichomoniasis`,`Nitro radical damages DNA in anaerobes`],[`Diloxanide furoate`,`Luminal amoebiasis, cyst carriers`,`Given after a tissue amoebicide to clear cysts`],[`Albendazole, mebendazole`,`Most nematodes, hydatid, neurocysticercosis`,`Bind beta-tubulin, block glucose uptake`],[`Pyrantel pamoate`,`Ascaris, hookworm, pinworm`,`Depolarising neuromuscular block, spastic paralysis`],[`Ivermectin`,`Strongyloides, filariasis, scabies`,`Opens glutamate-gated chloride channels, flaccid paralysis`],[`Diethylcarbamazine`,`Lymphatic filariasis`,`Kills microfilariae; Mazzotti-type reaction`],[`Praziquantel`,`Schistosomes and tapeworms`,`Increases calcium permeability, tetanic contraction`],[`Niclosamide`,`Tapeworms`,`Uncouples oxidative phosphorylation in the worm`]],caption:`Pyrantel causes spastic paralysis and piperazine causes flaccid paralysis, so the two must never be combined.`}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Cancer chemotherapy uses drugs that damage dividing cells. Because cancer cells divide faster than most normal cells, they are hit hardest, but normal fast-dividing tissues such as bone marrow, hair follicles and the gut lining are also damaged. That is the origin of almost every side effect.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The common toxicities`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Bone marrow suppression: low white cells (infection), low platelets (bleeding), anaemia. This is the dose-limiting toxicity of most anticancer drugs.`,`Nausea and vomiting, mouth ulcers and diarrhoea from damage to the gut lining.`,`Hair loss, which is usually reversible.`,`Infertility and a small long-term risk of a second cancer.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The main groups`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Group`,`Example`,`What it does`],rows:[[`Alkylating agent`,`Cyclophosphamide`,`Attaches alkyl groups to DNA and cross-links it`],[`Antimetabolite`,`Methotrexate, 5-fluorouracil`,`Looks like a normal building block and jams the pathway`],[`Antitumour antibiotic`,`Doxorubicin, bleomycin`,`Damages DNA directly`],[`Plant alkaloid`,`Vincristine, paclitaxel`,`Disturbs the spindle so the cell cannot divide`],[`Platinum compound`,`Cisplatin`,`Cross-links DNA like an alkylating agent`],[`Hormonal agent`,`Tamoxifen`,`Blocks the hormone that the tumour depends on`],[`Targeted drug`,`Imatinib, trastuzumab`,`Hits one specific abnormal protein in the cancer cell`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Combination chemotherapy`,text:`Drugs are combined so that they attack by different mechanisms, have different toxicities that do not overlap, and act at different points of the cell cycle. This gives a better kill with tolerable side effects and slows resistance.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Immunopharmacology in one line each`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Immunosuppressants damp down the immune system. They are used after organ transplantation and in autoimmune disease. Examples: ciclosporin, tacrolimus, azathioprine, corticosteroids.`,`Immunostimulants boost immunity, for example interferons, BCG vaccine used in bladder cancer, and levamisole.`,`Checkpoint inhibitors are a newer group that release the brakes on the patient own T cells so they attack the tumour.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Three famous organ toxicities`,text:`Doxorubicin hurts the heart, bleomycin hurts the lung, cisplatin hurts the kidney. Heart, lung, kidney: D, B, C.`}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Exam questions here are almost always one of three kinds: is the drug cell cycle specific or not, what is its exact molecular mechanism, and what is its signature toxicity with the agent used to prevent it.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cell cycle specificity`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Category`,`Phase`,`Drugs`],rows:[[`Cell cycle specific`,`S phase`,`Methotrexate, 5-fluorouracil, cytarabine, 6-mercaptopurine, hydroxyurea`],[`Cell cycle specific`,`M phase`,`Vincristine, vinblastine, paclitaxel, docetaxel`],[`Cell cycle specific`,`G2 phase`,`Bleomycin, etoposide`],[`Cell cycle specific`,`G1 phase`,`Asparaginase, corticosteroids`],[`Cell cycle non-specific`,`Any phase`,`Alkylating agents, cisplatin, doxorubicin, dactinomycin, nitrosoureas`]],caption:`Cycle-specific drugs suit slow-growing tumours given by prolonged infusion; non-specific drugs kill by first-order kinetics regardless of phase and suit large bulky tumours.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Mechanisms by class`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Class`,`Drugs`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Nitrogen mustard`,`Cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide, chlorambucil, melphalan`,`Alkylate N7 of guanine, cross-link DNA strands`],[`Nitrosourea`,`Carmustine, lomustine`,`Alkylate and carbamoylate; lipid soluble, cross into CNS`],[`Antifolate`,`Methotrexate, pemetrexed`,`Inhibit dihydrofolate reductase`],[`Pyrimidine antagonist`,`5-fluorouracil, cytarabine, gemcitabine`,`Block thymidylate synthase or incorporate into DNA`],[`Purine antagonist`,`6-mercaptopurine, 6-thioguanine, fludarabine`,`False purines, block nucleotide synthesis`],[`Anthracycline`,`Doxorubicin, daunorubicin`,`Intercalate DNA, inhibit topoisomerase II, generate free radicals`],[`Other antibiotic`,`Bleomycin, dactinomycin, mitomycin C`,`Free radical DNA strand breaks or intercalation`],[`Vinca alkaloid`,`Vincristine, vinblastine, vinorelbine`,`Prevent tubulin polymerisation, spindle does not form`],[`Taxane`,`Paclitaxel, docetaxel`,`Stabilise microtubules, spindle cannot disassemble`],[`Platinum`,`Cisplatin, carboplatin, oxaliplatin`,`Intrastrand cross-links between adjacent guanines`],[`Topoisomerase I inhibitor`,`Irinotecan, topotecan`,`Stabilise the cleavable complex, single-strand breaks`],[`Topoisomerase II inhibitor`,`Etoposide, teniposide`,`Double-strand breaks`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Vinca and taxane are opposites with the same result`,text:`Vinca alkaloids prevent microtubules assembling; taxanes prevent them disassembling. Either way the mitotic spindle cannot function and the cell arrests in metaphase. The drugs are opposite in mechanism but identical in outcome.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Signature toxicities and rescues`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Signature toxicity`,`Prevention or rescue`],rows:[[`Cyclophosphamide, ifosfamide`,`Haemorrhagic cystitis from acrolein`,`Mesna plus hydration`],[`Doxorubicin`,`Dose-related dilated cardiomyopathy`,`Dexrazoxane, limit cumulative dose`],[`Bleomycin`,`Pulmonary fibrosis`,`Limit cumulative dose, monitor lung function`],[`Cisplatin`,`Nephrotoxicity, ototoxicity, severe emesis`,`Hydration with mannitol, amifostine, 5-HT3 blocker`],[`Vincristine`,`Peripheral neuropathy, no marrow toxicity`,`Dose cap, monitor reflexes`],[`Vinblastine`,`Bone marrow suppression`,`Dose adjustment`],[`Methotrexate`,`Mucositis, marrow suppression, nephrotoxicity`,`Folinic acid (leucovorin) rescue`],[`5-fluorouracil`,`Mucositis, diarrhoea, hand-foot syndrome`,`Dose modification, check DPD deficiency`],[`Busulfan`,`Pulmonary fibrosis, skin pigmentation`,`Dose limitation`],[`L-asparaginase`,`Pancreatitis, hypersensitivity, clotting defects`,`Monitor amylase and fibrinogen`],[`Irinotecan`,`Early cholinergic diarrhoea, late severe diarrhoea`,`Atropine early, loperamide late`],[`Oxaliplatin`,`Cold-triggered peripheral neuropathy`,`Avoid cold exposure`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Vincristine and vinblastine differ in toxicity`,text:`They differ by a single formyl versus methyl group but their dose-limiting toxicities are opposite: vincristine causes neurotoxicity and spares the marrow, vinblastine causes myelosuppression and spares the nerves. Both are fatal if given intrathecally.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Hormonal and targeted agents`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Agent`,`Target`,`Cancer`],rows:[[`Tamoxifen`,`Oestrogen receptor`,`ER-positive breast cancer`],[`Anastrozole, letrozole`,`Aromatase`,`Postmenopausal breast cancer`],[`Leuprolide, bicalutamide`,`Androgen axis`,`Prostate cancer`],[`Imatinib`,`BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase, c-KIT`,`CML, GIST`],[`Trastuzumab`,`HER2 receptor`,`HER2-positive breast cancer`],[`Rituximab`,`CD20 on B cells`,`Non-Hodgkin lymphoma, CLL, autoimmune disease`],[`Gefitinib, erlotinib, osimertinib`,`EGFR tyrosine kinase`,`Non-small cell lung cancer`],[`Bevacizumab`,`VEGF`,`Colorectal, lung, renal cancer`],[`Pembrolizumab, nivolumab`,`PD-1 checkpoint`,`Melanoma, lung and many solid tumours`],[`Ipilimumab`,`CTLA-4 checkpoint`,`Melanoma`]],caption:`Trastuzumab causes cardiomyopathy that is usually reversible, unlike the irreversible anthracycline damage, and the two together compound the risk.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Immunosuppressants`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Drug`,`Mechanism`,`Key toxicity`],rows:[[`Ciclosporin`,`Binds cyclophilin, inhibits calcineurin, blocks IL-2`,`Nephrotoxicity, hypertension, gum hyperplasia, hirsutism`],[`Tacrolimus`,`Binds FKBP-12, inhibits calcineurin`,`Nephrotoxicity, neurotoxicity, new-onset diabetes`],[`Sirolimus`,`Binds FKBP-12, inhibits mTOR`,`Hyperlipidaemia, poor wound healing, no nephrotoxicity`],[`Azathioprine`,`Converted to 6-mercaptopurine, blocks purine synthesis`,`Marrow suppression; check TPMT`],[`Mycophenolate mofetil`,`Inhibits inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase`,`GI upset, teratogenic`],[`Corticosteroids`,`Broad cytokine suppression`,`Cushingoid effects, infection`],[`Basiliximab`,`Anti-CD25 (IL-2 receptor)`,`Induction therapy, few effects`],[`Anti-thymocyte globulin`,`Depletes T lymphocytes`,`Cytokine release, serum sickness`]]}]},{topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced items test kinetics of tumour cell kill, prodrug activation, resistance mechanisms and the specific chemistry behind each rescue agent.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Log kill and growth fraction`},{kind:`formula`,expr:`Cells remaining = initial cells x (fraction surviving per cycle)^n`,caption:`n = number of treatment cycles. Chemotherapy kills a constant fraction, not a constant number, of cells (first-order kill), which is why cycles are repeated.`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`A tumour is only detectable at about 10 to the power 9 cells (roughly 1 gram). A 3-log kill takes 10^9 to 10^6 cells, still far from cure, so many cycles are needed.`,`Growth fraction is the proportion of cells actively in cycle. Small, well-perfused tumours have a high growth fraction and respond best; large necrotic tumours have many resting G0 cells and respond poorly.`,`Gompertzian growth means growth slows as the tumour enlarges, which is the rationale for debulking surgery before chemotherapy and for adjuvant chemotherapy after it.`,`Cells in G0 escape cycle-specific drugs entirely, which is a major source of treatment failure and relapse.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Prodrugs and rescues`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Cyclophosphamide is inactive until hepatic CYP2B6 converts it to 4-hydroxycyclophosphamide, which decomposes to phosphoramide mustard (the cytotoxic species) and acrolein (the urotoxic species).`,`Acrolein damages the bladder urothelium and causes haemorrhagic cystitis; mesna (sodium 2-mercaptoethanesulfonate) supplies a free sulfhydryl group that conjugates acrolein in the urine without inactivating the drug in plasma.`,`Methotrexate blocks dihydrofolate reductase, depleting tetrahydrofolate and stopping thymidylate and purine synthesis.`,`Folinic acid (leucovorin, N5-formyl tetrahydrofolate) is already reduced and bypasses the blocked enzyme, so it rescues normal cells after high-dose methotrexate. Timing matters: it is given 24 to 36 hours later.`,`In colorectal cancer the same folinic acid is used for the opposite purpose: it stabilises the 5-FU metabolite FdUMP on thymidylate synthase and potentiates the drug.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Folinic acid, not folic acid`,text:`Folic acid still requires dihydrofolate reductase, the very enzyme methotrexate has blocked, so it cannot rescue. Only folinic acid, which enters the pathway beyond the block, works. Examiners test this distinction directly.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Resistance mechanisms`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Mechanism`,`Example`],rows:[[`Efflux by P-glycoprotein (MDR1)`,`Anthracyclines, vinca alkaloids, taxanes, etoposide`],[`Decreased uptake`,`Loss of the reduced folate carrier reduces methotrexate entry`],[`Target amplification`,`Dihydrofolate reductase gene amplification`],[`Target mutation`,`T315I mutation in BCR-ABL causes imatinib resistance`],[`Increased inactivation`,`Rising glutathione S-transferase inactivates alkylating agents`],[`Enhanced DNA repair`,`MGMT expression reverses nitrosourea alkylation`],[`Loss of apoptotic pathway`,`p53 mutation or BCL-2 overexpression`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Doxorubicin cardiotoxicity is driven by iron-catalysed free radical formation in cardiac tissue, which is poor in catalase. Risk rises steeply beyond a cumulative dose of about 400 to 550 mg/m2. Dexrazoxane chelates iron and is the specific protectant.`,`Cisplatin causes proximal tubular damage and magnesium wasting; carboplatin substitutes myelosuppression (thrombocytopenia) for nephrotoxicity, and oxaliplatin substitutes neuropathy.`,`Tumour lysis syndrome follows rapid cell kill in bulky leukaemias and lymphomas: hyperuricaemia, hyperkalaemia, hyperphosphataemia and hypocalcaemia with acute kidney injury. Prevent with hydration and allopurinol or rasburicase.`,`All-trans retinoic acid is a differentiating agent in acute promyelocytic leukaemia and can cause the differentiation syndrome with fever, fluid retention and respiratory distress.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Immunostimulants and biologicals`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Interferon alpha: chronic hepatitis, hairy cell leukaemia, melanoma. Interferon beta: multiple sclerosis. Interferon gamma: chronic granulomatous disease.`,`BCG instilled into the bladder is a non-specific immunostimulant used in superficial bladder cancer.`,`Levamisole, thalidomide and lenalidomide have immunomodulatory actions; thalidomide is severely teratogenic (phocomelia) and its use is strictly controlled.`,`Filgrastim (G-CSF) shortens the neutropenic period after chemotherapy; erythropoietin corrects anaemia; interleukin-11 and thrombopoietin agonists raise platelets.`,`Checkpoint inhibitors release T cell inhibition and therefore produce immune-related adverse effects: colitis, hepatitis, pneumonitis, thyroiditis and hypophysitis, treated with corticosteroids.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Imatinib is the model targeted drug`,text:`It occupies the ATP binding pocket of the BCR-ABL fusion kinase produced by the Philadelphia chromosome t(9;22), and also inhibits c-KIT and PDGFR. It transformed chronic myeloid leukaemia from a fatal disease into a chronically managed one, and is the standard example of rational drug design in every syllabus.`}]},{topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`basic`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Toxicology is the study of poisons: how they harm the body and how the harm is treated. An antidote is a substance that specifically counteracts a particular poison. Most poisoned patients, however, are saved by good supportive care rather than by an antidote.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`The order of treatment`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Support the airway, breathing and circulation first. This saves more lives than any antidote.`,`Stop further absorption: wash the skin, or use gastric lavage or activated charcoal if the poison was swallowed recently.`,`Speed up removal: forced diuresis, urinary pH change, or dialysis in selected poisons.`,`Give the specific antidote if one exists.`,`Treat symptoms such as convulsions, low blood pressure and abnormal heart rhythm as they arise.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Antidotes to memorise`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Poison`,`Antidote`],rows:[[`Organophosphate insecticide`,`Atropine plus pralidoxime`],[`Morphine and other opioids`,`Naloxone`],[`Paracetamol`,`N-acetylcysteine`],[`Benzodiazepines`,`Flumazenil`],[`Warfarin`,`Vitamin K1 (phytomenadione)`],[`Heparin`,`Protamine sulfate`],[`Iron`,`Desferrioxamine`],[`Lead`,`Calcium disodium EDTA, succimer`],[`Methanol and ethylene glycol`,`Fomepizole or ethanol`],[`Cyanide`,`Sodium nitrite plus sodium thiosulfate, or hydroxocobalamin`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Never lavage a corrosive or a kerosene`,text:`Acids, alkalis and petroleum products must not be removed by gastric lavage. Corrosives can perforate the already damaged oesophagus, and kerosene can be aspirated into the lung causing chemical pneumonitis.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Activated charcoal`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`It is finely divided carbon with a huge surface area that adsorbs many drugs in the gut so they cannot be absorbed.`,`It works best within about one hour of ingestion.`,`It does not adsorb iron, lithium, alcohols, strong acids and alkalis, or cyanide.`,`It must not be given if the airway is unprotected in an unconscious patient, because inhaling it is dangerous.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`mnemonic`,title:`Charcoal does not bind PHAILS`,text:`Pesticides, Hydrocarbons, Acids and alkalis, Iron, Lithium, Solvents and alcohols. These pass straight through.`}]},{topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`intermediate`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`At exam level you must know each antidote with the mechanism by which it works, the syndrome the poison produces, and the situations where a decontamination step is contraindicated.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Decontamination methods`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Method`,`Best used`,`Contraindicated in`],rows:[[`Gastric lavage`,`Within 1 hour, large ingestion`,`Corrosives, hydrocarbons, unprotected airway, convulsions`],[`Activated charcoal`,`Within 1 hour, adsorbable poison`,`Ileus, unprotected airway, non-adsorbed poisons`],[`Multiple-dose charcoal`,`Enterohepatic recycling drugs`,`Same as above`],[`Whole bowel irrigation`,`Iron, lithium, sustained-release, body packers`,`Obstruction, perforation, ileus`],[`Induced emesis (ipecac)`,`Essentially obsolete`,`Not recommended in modern practice`]],caption:`Multiple-dose activated charcoal is useful for carbamazepine, dapsone, phenobarbitone, quinine and theophylline.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Enhancing elimination`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Urinary alkalinisation with sodium bicarbonate traps weak acids in the tubule: salicylates, phenobarbitone, methotrexate.`,`Urinary acidification traps weak bases such as amphetamine, but is rarely used because of the risk of acidosis and rhabdomyolysis.`,`Haemodialysis is effective for poisons with low molecular weight, low protein binding, low volume of distribution and water solubility: methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylate, lithium, theophylline, phenobarbitone.`,`Dialysis is useless for drugs with a huge volume of distribution such as digoxin, tricyclic antidepressants and phenothiazines.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`The specific antidote table`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Poison`,`Antidote`,`Mechanism`],rows:[[`Organophosphates`,`Atropine, pralidoxime`,`Muscarinic block; oxime reactivates cholinesterase`],[`Carbamates`,`Atropine only`,`Spontaneous reactivation, oxime not needed`],[`Opioids`,`Naloxone`,`Competitive mu receptor antagonist`],[`Paracetamol`,`N-acetylcysteine`,`Replenishes glutathione, detoxifies NAPQI`],[`Benzodiazepines`,`Flumazenil`,`Competitive GABA-A benzodiazepine site antagonist`],[`Methanol, ethylene glycol`,`Fomepizole or ethanol`,`Competes for alcohol dehydrogenase`],[`Cyanide`,`Sodium nitrite, thiosulfate, hydroxocobalamin`,`Methaemoglobin traps cyanide; thiosulfate donates sulfur`],[`Carbon monoxide`,`100 percent oxygen, hyperbaric oxygen`,`Displaces CO from haemoglobin`],[`Warfarin`,`Vitamin K1, fresh frozen plasma`,`Restores factor II, VII, IX, X synthesis`],[`Heparin`,`Protamine sulfate`,`Strongly basic, neutralises acidic heparin`],[`Digoxin`,`Digoxin-specific Fab antibody`,`Binds and removes digoxin`],[`Iron`,`Desferrioxamine`,`Chelates ferric iron, vin rose urine`],[`Lead`,`Calcium disodium EDTA, dimercaprol, succimer, penicillamine`,`Chelation`],[`Arsenic and mercury`,`Dimercaprol (BAL), succimer, penicillamine`,`Sulfhydryl chelation`],[`Copper (Wilson disease)`,`Penicillamine, trientine`,`Chelation`],[`Methotrexate`,`Folinic acid`,`Bypasses blocked dihydrofolate reductase`],[`Isoniazid`,`Pyridoxine`,`Restores GABA synthesis, stops seizures`],[`Dabigatran`,`Idarucizumab`,`Monoclonal antibody fragment`]]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Flumazenil is not routine`,text:`In a mixed overdose or in a chronic benzodiazepine user, flumazenil can precipitate uncontrollable seizures by removing the anticonvulsant cover, especially when tricyclic antidepressants are also present. It is used mainly for reversing procedural sedation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Organophosphate poisoning`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Organophosphates inhibit acetylcholinesterase irreversibly, so acetylcholine accumulates everywhere.`,`Muscarinic features (DUMBBELS): diarrhoea, urination, miosis, bronchorrhoea and bronchospasm, bradycardia, emesis, lacrimation, salivation.`,`Nicotinic features: fasciculations, muscle weakness, then paralysis including the respiratory muscles.`,`Central features: confusion, convulsions, coma and respiratory depression.`,`Atropine reverses only the muscarinic effects; the end point of atropinisation is drying of secretions and clear chest, not pupil size.`,`Pralidoxime reactivates the enzyme and therefore also relieves the nicotinic weakness, but only if given before ageing, the irreversible dealkylation of the enzyme-phosphate bond.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Give pralidoxime early`,text:`Once ageing has occurred the enzyme cannot be reactivated by any oxime and recovery must wait for synthesis of new cholinesterase over days to weeks. Ageing is fast with some agents, so oxime therapy is started as early as possible alongside atropine.`}]},{topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`advanced`,blocks:[{kind:`para`,text:`Advanced toxicology asks for chelator selection, the biochemistry of methanol and cyanide poisoning, and the specific clinical signature of each heavy metal.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Chelating agents`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Chelator`,`Metal of choice`,`Note`],rows:[[`Dimercaprol (BAL)`,`Arsenic, mercury, gold`,`Oily intramuscular injection, painful, in peanut oil`],[`Succimer (DMSA)`,`Lead in children, arsenic, mercury`,`Oral, safer, water soluble`],[`Calcium disodium EDTA`,`Inorganic lead`,`Given as calcium salt to avoid hypocalcaemia`],[`Penicillamine`,`Copper (Wilson disease), lead, mercury`,`Oral; pyridoxine deficiency, nephrotic syndrome`],[`Desferrioxamine`,`Iron, aluminium`,`Chelate excreted in urine gives vin rose colour`],[`Prussian blue`,`Thallium, radioactive caesium`,`Not absorbed, binds in the gut`]],caption:`Dimercaprol must not be used for lead alone or for cadmium and iron, because the complexes formed redistribute to the brain or kidney.`},{kind:`callout`,tone:`trap`,title:`Use disodium calcium EDTA, not disodium EDTA`,text:`Plain disodium EDTA avidly chelates plasma calcium and can cause fatal hypocalcaemic tetany. The calcium salt exchanges its calcium for lead and is the only form used for chelation.`},{kind:`heading`,text:`Methanol and ethylene glycol`},{kind:`numbered`,items:[`Methanol is oxidised by alcohol dehydrogenase to formaldehyde and then by aldehyde dehydrogenase to formic acid.`,`Formic acid inhibits cytochrome oxidase in the optic nerve, causing blindness, and produces a severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis.`,`Ethanol has about 10 to 20 times higher affinity for alcohol dehydrogenase, so it competitively prevents the formation of the toxic metabolite; fomepizole does the same more safely and predictably.`,`Folinic acid accelerates formate breakdown to carbon dioxide and water.`,`Ethylene glycol follows the same enzyme to glycolic and oxalic acid, causing acidosis and calcium oxalate crystals in the urine and renal failure.`,`Bicarbonate corrects the acidosis and haemodialysis removes both parent alcohol and metabolites.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Cyanide`},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Cyanide binds ferric iron in cytochrome oxidase a3, stopping the electron transport chain so tissues cannot use oxygen. Venous blood stays bright red and lactate rises steeply.`,`Sodium nitrite oxidises haemoglobin to methaemoglobin, whose ferric iron competes for cyanide to form cyanmethaemoglobin.`,`Sodium thiosulfate then supplies sulfur to rhodanese, converting cyanide to the much less toxic thiocyanate excreted in urine.`,`Hydroxocobalamin binds cyanide directly to form cyanocobalamin and is safer in smoke inhalation victims, where added methaemoglobin would worsen carbon monoxide-impaired oxygen carriage.`,`The classic smell of bitter almonds is detectable by only a genetically determined minority, so its absence proves nothing.`]},{kind:`heading`,text:`Heavy metal signatures`},{kind:`table`,headers:[`Metal`,`Clinical features`],rows:[[`Lead`,`Burtonian blue gum line, wrist drop, colic, basophilic stippling, encephalopathy in children`],[`Arsenic`,`Rice-water stools, garlic breath, Mees lines on nails, hyperpigmentation with raindrop depigmentation`],[`Mercury (inorganic)`,`Acrodynia, corrosive gastroenteritis, renal tubular necrosis`],[`Mercury (organic)`,`Minamata disease, ataxia, constricted visual fields, deafness`],[`Iron`,`Haemorrhagic gastroenteritis, then a latent phase, then shock and hepatic failure`],[`Copper`,`Wilson disease with Kayser-Fleischer rings, tremor, cirrhosis`],[`Cadmium`,`Itai-itai disease, osteomalacia, proximal tubular damage`]]},{kind:`bullets`,items:[`Lead inhibits delta-aminolevulinic acid dehydratase and ferrochelatase, so ALA and free erythrocyte protoporphyrin rise; blood lead level confirms the diagnosis.`,`Iron poisoning is staged: gastrointestinal phase, a deceptive quiescent phase of 6 to 24 hours, then metabolic acidosis and shock, hepatic necrosis, and late gastric scarring. The quiescent phase is the classic trap.`,`Digoxin toxicity is worsened by hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and hypercalcaemia; Fab fragments are indicated for life-threatening arrhythmia or a serum potassium above about 5 mmol/L in acute overdose.`,`Naloxone has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so a single dose can wear off and the patient re-sedates; infusion or repeated dosing with observation is required, especially with methadone.`]},{kind:`callout`,tone:`exam`,title:`Toxic alcohols share the same enzyme`,text:`Methanol, ethylene glycol and ethanol all compete for alcohol dehydrogenase. That single fact explains why ethanol is an antidote, why fomepizole replaced it, and why both poisonings present with a high anion gap and a raised osmolar gap.`}]}],t=[{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-1`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which antihistamine is least likely to cause sedation?`,options:[`Promethazine`,`Chlorpheniramine`,`Fexofenadine`,`Diphenhydramine`],answer:2,explanation:`Fexofenadine is a second generation H1 blocker; it is a P-glycoprotein substrate and barely crosses the blood-brain barrier, so sedation is minimal. Promethazine, chlorpheniramine and diphenhydramine are all first generation drugs that enter the CNS and cause marked drowsiness.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-2`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Aspirin is avoided in children with viral fever because of the risk of:`,options:[`Reye syndrome`,`Grey baby syndrome`,`Kernicterus`,`Stevens-Johnson syndrome`],answer:0,explanation:`Aspirin in a child with a viral illness such as varicella or influenza can precipitate Reye syndrome, an acute encephalopathy with fatty degeneration of the liver. Grey baby syndrome belongs to chloramphenicol in neonates, and kernicterus to sulfonamides displacing bilirubin.`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-3`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which serotonin receptor is a ligand-gated ion channel rather than a G-protein coupled receptor?`,options:[`5-HT1A`,`5-HT2A`,`5-HT3`,`5-HT4`],answer:2,explanation:`5-HT3 is the only member of the serotonin family that is a ligand-gated cation channel, which is why ondansetron acts rapidly. 5-HT2A is tempting because it is well known in psychopharmacology, but like 5-HT1A and 5-HT4 it is G-protein coupled.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-4`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Corticosteroids suppress both prostaglandins and leukotrienes because they act on:`,options:[`Cyclooxygenase-1`,`Thromboxane synthase`,`5-Lipoxygenase`,`Phospholipase A2`],answer:3,explanation:`Steroids induce lipocortin (annexin-1), which inhibits phospholipase A2 and stops arachidonic acid being released from membrane phospholipid. Because the block is above the branch point, both the COX and LOX limbs are shut down. NSAIDs block only COX, so leukotrienes are unaffected.`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-5`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which drug is NOT useful in the treatment of an acute attack of gout?`,options:[`Colchicine`,`Indomethacin`,`Allopurinol`,`Prednisolone`],answer:2,explanation:`Allopurinol lowers urate but has no anti-inflammatory action, and a sudden fall in plasma urate mobilises crystals and can prolong the attack. Colchicine, NSAIDs such as indomethacin and corticosteroids all suppress the inflammation and are appropriate acutely.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-6`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Colchicine relieves acute gout by:`,options:[`Inhibiting xanthine oxidase`,`Inhibiting cyclooxygenase-2 selectively`,`Blocking urate reabsorption in the proximal tubule`,`Binding tubulin and preventing neutrophil migration`],answer:3,explanation:`Colchicine binds tubulin, prevents microtubule polymerisation and therefore stops neutrophils migrating into the inflamed joint. It neither lowers urate nor inhibits COX, so it is not an analgesic in the general sense; xanthine oxidase inhibition is the mechanism of allopurinol.`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-7`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient takes ibuprofen for arthritis each morning shortly before his low-dose aspirin. Why is this a problem?`,options:[`Ibuprofen increases aspirin absorption and causes salicylate toxicity`,`Ibuprofen displaces aspirin from plasma albumin`,`Ibuprofen induces the enzymes that metabolise aspirin`,`Ibuprofen occupies the COX-1 channel and prevents aspirin acetylating Ser530`],answer:3,explanation:`Ibuprofen is a reversible competitive COX inhibitor that sits in the same hydrophobic channel aspirin must traverse to acetylate serine 530 of platelet COX-1. Occupying that channel blocks the irreversible acetylation and abolishes cardioprotection. Displacement from albumin would raise free aspirin, not reduce its antiplatelet effect.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-8`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Hepatic injury in paracetamol overdose results from:`,options:[`Direct binding of paracetamol to hepatocyte DNA`,`Accumulation of the glucuronide conjugate`,`Depletion of glutathione and accumulation of NAPQI`,`Inhibition of mitochondrial complex I`],answer:2,explanation:`In overdose the glucuronide and sulfate routes saturate, more paracetamol is diverted through CYP2E1 to the reactive quinone imine NAPQI, and hepatic glutathione is depleted so NAPQI binds hepatocyte proteins causing centrilobular necrosis. The glucuronide conjugate is inert and simply excreted; complex I inhibition is metformin.`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-1`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which oral antidiabetic is the first-line drug in a newly diagnosed overweight patient with type 2 diabetes?`,options:[`Metformin`,`Glibenclamide`,`Pioglitazone`,`Acarbose`],answer:0,explanation:`Metformin is first-line because it lowers HbA1c effectively, does not cause hypoglycaemia on its own and is weight neutral or weight reducing. Glibenclamide and pioglitazone both cause weight gain, which is undesirable in an overweight patient.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-2`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which insulin preparation can be given intravenously?`,options:[`NPH (isophane) insulin`,`Insulin glargine`,`Regular (soluble) insulin`,`Insulin detemir`],answer:2,explanation:`Regular soluble insulin is a clear solution and is the standard intravenous insulin, for example in diabetic ketoacidosis; the rapid analogues may also be used. NPH is a protamine suspension and glargine precipitates at tissue pH, so neither can be injected into a vein.`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-3`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sulfonylureas stimulate insulin release by acting on:`,options:[`The GLP-1 receptor on beta cells`,`The L-type calcium channel directly`,`PPAR-gamma nuclear receptors`,`The SUR1 subunit of the ATP-sensitive potassium channel`],answer:3,explanation:`Sulfonylureas bind the SUR1 regulatory subunit and close the K-ATP channel, depolarising the beta cell so voltage-gated calcium channels open and insulin is released. They act downstream of glucose sensing, which is exactly why they cause hypoglycaemia. PPAR-gamma is the target of the glitazones.`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-4`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antidiabetic class is most likely to cause genital mycotic infection and euglycaemic ketoacidosis?`,options:[`SGLT2 inhibitors`,`DPP-4 inhibitors`,`Meglitinides`,`Alpha-glucosidase inhibitors`],answer:0,explanation:`SGLT2 inhibitors cause glycosuria, which feeds genital candidal infection, and shift metabolism towards ketogenesis so ketoacidosis can occur with only mildly raised glucose. DPP-4 inhibitors are largely weight and glucose neutral in this respect and do not cause glycosuria.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-5`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antithyroid drug also inhibits peripheral conversion of T4 to T3?`,options:[`Carbimazole`,`Methimazole`,`Propylthiouracil`,`Perchlorate`],answer:2,explanation:`Propylthiouracil is the only thioamide that additionally inhibits the peripheral 5-deiodinase, which is why it is preferred in thyroid storm. Carbimazole is a prodrug of methimazole and neither blocks the deiodinase; perchlorate blocks iodide uptake at the symporter.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-6`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A type 2 diabetic patient on metformin is scheduled for a contrast-enhanced CT scan. The correct action is to:`,options:[`Continue metformin and add extra fluids`,`Double the metformin dose to offset contrast-induced hyperglycaemia`,`Replace metformin permanently with a sulfonylurea`,`Withhold metformin around the procedure and check renal function before restarting`],answer:3,explanation:`Iodinated contrast can transiently impair renal function, and metformin is cleared unchanged by the kidney, so accumulation raises the risk of lactic acidosis. The drug is withheld and restarted once renal function is confirmed stable. Permanent substitution is unnecessary.`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-7`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In thyroid storm, why must the thioamide be given before iodide?`,options:[`Iodide inactivates propylthiouracil chemically`,`Iodide accelerates renal clearance of the thioamide`,`Iodide blocks intestinal absorption of the thioamide`,`Iodide would otherwise be organified into fresh hormone`],answer:3,explanation:`If iodide is given first it is trapped and organified, providing substrate for new hormone synthesis and worsening the storm. Giving the thioamide at least an hour earlier blocks peroxidase so the iodide can only exert its acute inhibition of hormone release. There is no chemical inactivation or absorption interference.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-8`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`During insulin treatment of diabetic ketoacidosis, potassium replacement is started early because:`,options:[`Insulin increases renal potassium excretion`,`Saline infusion causes potassium to bind to albumin`,`Ketone bodies chelate plasma potassium`,`Insulin drives potassium into cells and total body potassium is already depleted`],answer:3,explanation:`Acidosis shifts potassium out of cells so the measured plasma level looks normal or high, masking a large total body deficit. Insulin reverses that shift and drives potassium intracellularly, so replacement is begun once the level falls below about 5.2 mmol/L and urine output is established. Insulin does not act on renal potassium handling as the primary mechanism here.`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-1`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which corticosteroid has essentially no mineralocorticoid activity?`,options:[`Hydrocortisone`,`Aldosterone`,`Fludrocortisone`,`Dexamethasone`],answer:3,explanation:`The 16-methyl substitution in dexamethasone abolishes salt-retaining activity while giving about 25 to 30 times the anti-inflammatory potency of hydrocortisone. Fludrocortisone is at the opposite extreme, with over a hundred times the mineralocorticoid potency of hydrocortisone.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-2`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Abrupt withdrawal of long-term corticosteroid therapy chiefly risks:`,options:[`Acute adrenal insufficiency`,`Hypertensive crisis`,`Hyperglycaemic coma`,`Thyroid storm`],answer:0,explanation:`Prolonged exogenous steroid suppresses ACTH and causes adrenal atrophy, so sudden withdrawal leaves the patient unable to generate cortisol, producing vomiting, hypotension and shock. Steroids raise rather than lower blood pressure and glucose during therapy, so a crisis of those kinds is not the withdrawal risk.`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-3`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which SERM is an oestrogen antagonist in breast tissue but an agonist at the endometrium, carrying a risk of endometrial carcinoma?`,options:[`Raloxifene`,`Fulvestrant`,`Anastrozole`,`Tamoxifen`],answer:3,explanation:`Tamoxifen antagonises the oestrogen receptor in breast but acts as a partial agonist at endometrium and bone, hence the endometrial cancer signal. Raloxifene is the tempting distractor but is endometrium-neutral, and anastrozole is an aromatase inhibitor rather than a receptor modulator.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-4`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Alternate-day corticosteroid therapy is best carried out with:`,options:[`Dexamethasone`,`Betamethasone`,`Prednisolone`,`Fludrocortisone`],answer:2,explanation:`The purpose of alternate-day dosing is to leave a drug-free interval in which the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal axis can recover, which requires an intermediate-acting steroid such as prednisolone. Dexamethasone and betamethasone act for 36 to 72 hours and suppress the axis continuously, defeating the schedule.`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-5`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which drug is used as a tocolytic to delay preterm labour?`,options:[`Nifedipine`,`Methylergometrine`,`Carboprost`,`Dinoprostone`],answer:0,explanation:`Nifedipine relaxes uterine smooth muscle by blocking L-type calcium channels and is a widely used tocolytic. Methylergometrine, carboprost and dinoprostone are all oxytocics that contract the uterus and are used to induce labour or control postpartum haemorrhage.`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-6`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Finasteride relieves benign prostatic hyperplasia by:`,options:[`Blocking alpha-1A receptors in prostatic smooth muscle`,`Suppressing pituitary LH release`,`Blocking the androgen receptor competitively`,`Inhibiting 5-alpha reductase so less dihydrotestosterone is formed`],answer:3,explanation:`Finasteride inhibits 5-alpha reductase, reducing conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone and shrinking the gland over 3 to 6 months. Alpha-1A blockade is the mechanism of tamsulosin, which gives faster symptom relief but does not reduce prostate volume.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-7`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Sildenafil is absolutely contraindicated with nitrates because:`,options:[`Both drugs are metabolised by CYP2D6`,`Nitrates inhibit phosphodiesterase-6 and worsen visual toxicity`,`Sildenafil displaces nitrates from plasma proteins`,`Nitrates raise cGMP and sildenafil prevents its breakdown, causing profound hypotension`],answer:3,explanation:`Nitrates donate nitric oxide and increase cGMP formation while sildenafil blocks PDE-5 and prevents cGMP degradation; the two together cause uncontrolled vasodilatation and severe hypotension. The interaction is pharmacodynamic, not a protein-binding or CYP2D6 effect.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-8`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Teriparatide builds bone despite parathyroid hormone being a resorptive hormone because:`,options:[`It is a PTH antagonist at the osteoclast receptor`,`It inhibits RANK ligand like denosumab`,`It chelates calcium and forces mineral deposition`,`Intermittent once-daily exposure favours osteoblast survival, unlike continuous exposure`],answer:3,explanation:`Teriparatide is the 1-34 fragment of PTH; given once daily it produces a brief pulse that promotes osteoblast differentiation and survival, so bone formation exceeds resorption. Continuous high PTH, as in hyperparathyroidism, favours resorption. Anti-RANK ligand action belongs to denosumab.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-1`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which antacid combination is used to balance out bowel effects?`,options:[`Aluminium hydroxide with magnesium hydroxide`,`Calcium carbonate with sodium bicarbonate`,`Magnesium trisilicate with magnesium hydroxide`,`Aluminium hydroxide with sucralfate`],answer:0,explanation:`Aluminium salts constipate and magnesium salts cause diarrhoea, so combining them cancels the bowel effect. Two magnesium salts together would only intensify the diarrhoea, and sucralfate is a cytoprotective agent rather than an antacid.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-2`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The standard reliever inhaler for an acute asthma attack is:`,options:[`Budesonide`,`Salmeterol`,`Salbutamol`,`Montelukast`],answer:2,explanation:`Salbutamol is a short-acting beta-2 agonist that produces bronchodilation within minutes, making it the reliever of choice. Budesonide and montelukast are controllers with no rapid effect, and salmeterol has a slower onset and must never be used alone.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-3`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Proton pump inhibitors are given about 30 to 60 minutes before a meal because:`,options:[`Food destroys the enteric coating`,`Food increases their first-pass metabolism`,`They are absorbed only from the stomach`,`They are acid-activated prodrugs and act best on actively secreting pumps`],answer:3,explanation:`A PPI is an inactive prodrug that must be protonated in the acidic parietal cell canaliculus to form the reactive sulfenamide, and it can only bind pumps that are actively secreting. Timing the dose before a meal ensures peak plasma level coincides with maximal pump activation. They are absorbed from the small intestine, not the stomach.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-4`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antiemetic is most appropriate for cisplatin-induced acute vomiting?`,options:[`Ondansetron`,`Hyoscine`,`Cinnarizine`,`Loperamide`],answer:0,explanation:`Cisplatin releases large amounts of serotonin from gut enterochromaffin cells, which acts on 5-HT3 receptors on vagal afferents and in the chemoreceptor trigger zone, so ondansetron is the drug of choice. Hyoscine and cinnarizine target the vestibular pathway of motion sickness, and loperamide is an antidiarrhoeal.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-5`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Domperidone is preferred to metoclopramide in a young patient because it:`,options:[`Is a more potent prokinetic`,`Acts on 5-HT3 rather than D2 receptors`,`Has no effect on the QT interval`,`Does not appreciably cross the blood-brain barrier, so it does not cause dystonia`],answer:3,explanation:`Both are D2 antagonists, but metoclopramide enters the CNS and can cause acute dystonic reactions, which are commonest in the young. Domperidone stays largely peripheral so extrapyramidal effects are rare. Note that it does prolong the QT interval, so the claim that it has no QT effect is the trap — that is a genuine safety concern with domperidone, not a point in its favour.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-6`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which laxative is also used to lower blood ammonia in hepatic encephalopathy?`,options:[`Lactulose`,`Bisacodyl`,`Ispaghula`,`Docusate sodium`],answer:0,explanation:`Colonic bacteria ferment lactulose to organic acids, acidifying the lumen so ammonia is trapped as non-absorbable ammonium and expelled. The other three have purely mechanical or stimulant actions on the bowel and no effect on ammonia handling.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-7`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient stabilised on theophylline starts ciprofloxacin and develops nausea, tremor and palpitation. The best explanation is:`,options:[`Ciprofloxacin displaces theophylline from albumin`,`The two drugs chelate each other in the gut`,`Ciprofloxacin induces CYP3A4 and produces toxic metabolites`,`Ciprofloxacin inhibits CYP1A2 and raises theophylline levels`],answer:3,explanation:`Theophylline is cleared chiefly by CYP1A2, which ciprofloxacin inhibits, so plasma levels rise into the toxic range of a drug whose therapeutic window is only about 10 to 20 micrograms/mL. Enzyme induction would lower, not raise, the level, and theophylline is not highly protein bound enough for displacement to matter.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-8`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A long-acting beta-2 agonist must not be prescribed as monotherapy in asthma because:`,options:[`It causes rapid tachyphylaxis within 24 hours`,`It antagonises the effect of inhaled corticosteroids`,`It cannot be delivered by inhalation`,`It relieves symptoms while underlying inflammation progresses, and is linked to increased asthma deaths`],answer:3,explanation:`A LABA controls bronchospasm but has no anti-inflammatory action, so airway inflammation worsens unnoticed until a severe attack occurs; trials linked LABA monotherapy to increased asthma mortality. In fact LABAs and inhaled corticosteroids are synergistic, not antagonistic, which is why they are combined in one inhaler.`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-1`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Furosemide acts on which part of the nephron?`,options:[`Proximal convoluted tubule`,`Collecting duct`,`Distal convoluted tubule`,`Thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle`],answer:3,explanation:`Furosemide blocks the Na-K-2Cl symporter in the thick ascending limb, where about 25 percent of filtered sodium is reabsorbed, which is why it is the most efficacious diuretic class. The distal convoluted tubule is the thiazide site and the collecting duct is where the potassium-sparing agents act.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-2`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which diuretic is most likely to cause hyperkalaemia?`,options:[`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Furosemide`,`Spironolactone`,`Acetazolamide`],answer:2,explanation:`Spironolactone antagonises aldosterone at the collecting duct, so sodium is not exchanged for potassium and potassium is retained. Thiazides, loop diuretics and acetazolamide all increase distal sodium delivery and cause potassium loss instead.`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-3`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which diuretic reduces urinary calcium excretion and is therefore useful in recurrent calcium stones?`,options:[`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Furosemide`,`Acetazolamide`,`Mannitol`],answer:0,explanation:`Thiazides enhance calcium reabsorption in the distal convoluted tubule, lowering urinary calcium and reducing stone formation, though at the cost of mild hypercalcaemia. Furosemide is the opposite, increasing calcium loss, which is why it is used to treat hypercalcaemia rather than to prevent stones.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-4`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Acetazolamide typically produces which acid-base disturbance?`,options:[`Metabolic alkalosis`,`Respiratory acidosis`,`Respiratory alkalosis`,`Metabolic acidosis`],answer:3,explanation:`Inhibiting carbonic anhydrase makes the kidney lose bicarbonate in an alkaline urine, so plasma bicarbonate falls and hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis develops. Loop diuretics and thiazides cause the opposite, a hypokalaemic hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis.`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-5`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus is paradoxically treated with:`,options:[`Desmopressin`,`Thiazide diuretics`,`Furosemide`,`Mannitol`],answer:1,explanation:`A thiazide with salt restriction produces mild volume depletion, which increases proximal reabsorption of salt and water so less fluid reaches the defective collecting duct, and urine volume falls. Desmopressin is useless because the defect is receptor unresponsiveness rather than ADH deficiency, which is the central form.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-6`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which diuretic is safe to use in a patient with a documented severe sulfonamide allergy?`,options:[`Furosemide`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Ethacrynic acid`,`Acetazolamide`],answer:2,explanation:`Ethacrynic acid is the only loop diuretic that is not a sulfonamide derivative, so it is the option in sulfa allergy. Furosemide, thiazides and acetazolamide all contain a sulfonamide group. The trade-off is that ethacrynic acid is the most ototoxic loop diuretic.`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-7`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Furosemide reaches its site of action mainly by:`,options:[`Glomerular filtration, because it is minimally protein bound`,`Active secretion into the proximal tubule by the organic anion transporter`,`Passive diffusion across the tubular epithelium from blood`,`Binding an intracellular receptor on the basolateral side`],answer:1,explanation:`Furosemide is more than 95 percent protein bound, so very little is filtered; it is actively secreted by OAT1 in the proximal tubule and then acts from the lumen. This explains why probenecid and accumulated organic anions in renal failure blunt its effect. Spironolactone is the one that acts on an intracellular receptor from the blood side.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-8`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Thiazides are the classic cause of drug-induced hyponatraemia, whereas loop diuretics are less so, because:`,options:[`Thiazides impair urinary dilution while sparing the medullary concentrating gradient`,`Thiazides directly stimulate ADH release from the pituitary`,`Loop diuretics increase proximal sodium reabsorption`,`Thiazides are more potent natriuretics than loop diuretics`],answer:0,explanation:`A thiazide blocks sodium reabsorption in the diluting segment but leaves the medullary countercurrent gradient intact, so water can still be retained under ADH while sodium is lost. A loop diuretic destroys that gradient too, impairing concentration as well as dilution, so free water is not retained to the same extent. Thiazides are in fact less potent natriuretics than loop diuretics.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-1`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which of the following is a bactericidal protein synthesis inhibitor?`,options:[`Tetracycline`,`Erythromycin`,`Gentamicin`,`Chloramphenicol`],answer:2,explanation:`Aminoglycosides such as gentamicin bind the 30S subunit and cause misreading of mRNA, producing non-functional proteins that damage the membrane, so they kill rather than merely inhibit. Tetracyclines, macrolides and chloramphenicol are the classic bacteriostatic protein synthesis inhibitors.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-2`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Tetracyclines are contraindicated in children under 8 years because they cause:`,options:[`Optic neuritis`,`Permanent staining of teeth and bone deposition`,`Aplastic anaemia`,`Tendon rupture`],answer:1,explanation:`Tetracyclines chelate calcium and are deposited in developing teeth and growing bone, causing permanent yellow-brown discoloration and enamel defects. Optic neuritis belongs to ethambutol, aplastic anaemia to chloramphenicol and tendon rupture to fluoroquinolones.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-3`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Cilastatin is combined with imipenem in order to:`,options:[`Broaden the antibacterial spectrum to anaerobes`,`Inhibit renal dehydropeptidase I and prevent breakdown of imipenem in the tubule`,`Inhibit bacterial beta-lactamase`,`Reduce the risk of seizures`],answer:1,explanation:`Cilastatin has no antibacterial activity; it inhibits renal dehydropeptidase I, which would otherwise hydrolyse imipenem in the proximal tubule, reducing urinary levels and generating a nephrotoxic metabolite. Beta-lactamase inhibition is the role of clavulanic acid and tazobactam.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-4`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`The synergy of cotrimoxazole is explained by:`,options:[`Sequential blockade of two consecutive steps of folate synthesis`,`Both drugs inhibiting dihydrofolate reductase`,`One drug increasing the absorption of the other`,`Trimethoprim inhibiting the efflux pump for sulfamethoxazole`],answer:0,explanation:`Sulfamethoxazole inhibits dihydropteroate synthase and trimethoprim inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, two consecutive enzymes in the same pathway, so the combined block is bactericidal although each drug alone is bacteriostatic. Only trimethoprim acts on dihydrofolate reductase, which rules out the distractor claiming both drugs hit that enzyme.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-5`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Red man syndrome after vancomycin is caused by:`,options:[`IgE-mediated anaphylaxis`,`Non-immune histamine release related to rapid infusion`,`Direct nephrotoxicity`,`Accumulation of an active metabolite`],answer:1,explanation:`Vancomycin infused too rapidly causes direct degranulation of mast cells with histamine release, producing flushing over the face and upper body. Because it is not IgE mediated it is managed by slowing the infusion and giving an antihistamine, not by permanently avoiding the drug.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-6`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antibiotic is most classically associated with pseudomembranous colitis?`,options:[`Clindamycin`,`Nitrofurantoin`,`Gentamicin`,`Doxycycline`],answer:0,explanation:`Clindamycin suppresses normal colonic flora and allows Clostridioides difficile to overgrow and elaborate toxins, causing pseudomembranous colitis. Gentamicin is not absorbed orally and reaches little of the colon, and nitrofurantoin concentrates in urine rather than the gut lumen.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-7`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Once-daily rather than thrice-daily aminoglycoside dosing is used because aminoglycosides show:`,options:[`Time-dependent killing with a short post-antibiotic effect`,`Concentration-dependent killing with a prolonged post-antibiotic effect and saturable renal uptake`,`Zero-order elimination at therapeutic doses`,`Extensive enterohepatic recycling`],answer:1,explanation:`Killing correlates with the Cmax to MIC ratio, and growth stays suppressed after levels fall below the MIC, so a single large dose kills more effectively. Renal cortical uptake is saturable, so the long low trough of once-daily dosing actually reduces nephrotoxicity. Beta-lactams are the time-dependent class.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-8`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Methicillin resistance in Staphylococcus aureus is due to:`,options:[`Production of an extended spectrum beta-lactamase`,`Acquisition of the mecA gene encoding penicillin binding protein 2a with low beta-lactam affinity`,`Loss of an outer membrane porin`,`Upregulation of an efflux pump`],answer:1,explanation:`MRSA carries mecA, which encodes PBP2a, an altered transpeptidase with very low affinity for essentially all beta-lactams, so adding a beta-lactamase inhibitor does not help. Porin loss and efflux are gram-negative resistance mechanisms, since S. aureus has no outer membrane.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-1`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Pyridoxine is given with isoniazid to prevent:`,options:[`Optic neuritis`,`Peripheral neuropathy`,`Hyperuricaemia`,`Ototoxicity`],answer:1,explanation:`Isoniazid resembles pyridoxine and increases its urinary excretion, causing a dose-related peripheral neuropathy that pyridoxine 10 to 25 mg daily prevents. Optic neuritis is an ethambutol effect, hyperuricaemia belongs to pyrazinamide and ototoxicity to streptomycin.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-2`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which antitubercular drug causes orange-red discoloration of urine and other body fluids?`,options:[`Isoniazid`,`Ethambutol`,`Rifampicin`,`Pyrazinamide`],answer:2,explanation:`Rifampicin is intensely coloured and imparts an orange-red tinge to urine, sweat, tears and saliva, which is harmless but stains soft contact lenses. None of the other first-line drugs discolours secretions.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-3`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which antifungal class acts on the fungal cell wall rather than the membrane?`,options:[`Polyenes`,`Azoles`,`Echinocandins`,`Allylamines`],answer:2,explanation:`Echinocandins such as caspofungin inhibit beta-1,3-glucan synthase, an enzyme of cell wall synthesis with no human counterpart, which gives them a very good safety profile. Polyenes bind membrane ergosterol while azoles and allylamines block ergosterol synthesis, all membrane-directed actions.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-4`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A woman on a combined oral contraceptive is started on rifampicin. The likely consequence is:`,options:[`Contraceptive failure due to enzyme induction`,`Oestrogen toxicity due to enzyme inhibition`,`No interaction, since rifampicin is renally cleared`,`Increased risk of thromboembolism`],answer:0,explanation:`Rifampicin is a powerful inducer of CYP3A4 and P-glycoprotein and greatly accelerates metabolism of ethinylestradiol and progestins, so contraceptive efficacy is lost and an additional method is required. Enzyme induction lowers, never raises, the hormone level.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-5`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Primaquine is added to chloroquine in Plasmodium vivax malaria in order to:`,options:[`Eradicate dormant hypnozoites in the liver and prevent relapse`,`Increase blood schizonticidal potency`,`Prevent chloroquine resistance from emerging`,`Reduce the nausea caused by chloroquine`],answer:0,explanation:`Chloroquine clears only the erythrocytic stage; the hypnozoites persisting in hepatocytes cause relapse weeks or months later, and primaquine is the drug that eradicates them, giving radical cure. Screening for G6PD deficiency is advised because primaquine metabolites cause haemolysis.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-6`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Low-dose ritonavir is included in antiretroviral regimens mainly as:`,options:[`An integrase inhibitor`,`A pharmacokinetic booster through CYP3A4 inhibition`,`A drug to prevent NRTI-induced lactic acidosis`,`An agent that improves CNS penetration of NRTIs`],answer:1,explanation:`At sub-therapeutic doses ritonavir contributes little antiviral effect but potently inhibits CYP3A4, raising and sustaining the level of the companion protease inhibitor so that dosing can be simplified. Cobicistat performs the same booster role with no antiviral activity at all.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-7`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Isoniazid resistance in Mycobacterium tuberculosis most commonly arises from:`,options:[`Mutation in rpoB`,`Mutation or deletion of katG, the catalase-peroxidase that activates the prodrug`,`Loss of pncA pyrazinamidase`,`Overexpression of arabinosyl transferase`],answer:1,explanation:`Isoniazid is a prodrug and needs the mycobacterial catalase-peroxidase KatG for activation, so katG mutation prevents activation and confers high-level resistance. Mutations in rpoB give rifampicin resistance and loss of pncA gives pyrazinamide resistance.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-8`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Aciclovir is highly selective for herpes-infected cells because:`,options:[`It is actively transported only into infected cells`,`Its first phosphorylation is carried out by viral thymidine kinase`,`It binds a receptor expressed only on infected cells`,`It is activated by the low pH inside infected cells`],answer:1,explanation:`Aciclovir monophosphate is formed almost exclusively by herpesvirus thymidine kinase, so the active triphosphate accumulates in infected cells while uninfected cells remain unaffected. This two-step selectivity, followed by chain termination of viral DNA polymerase, explains its very low toxicity.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-1`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which anticancer drug is associated with dose-related cardiomyopathy?`,options:[`Bleomycin`,`Doxorubicin`,`Cisplatin`,`Vincristine`],answer:1,explanation:`Doxorubicin generates iron-catalysed free radicals in cardiac tissue, causing a cumulative dose-related dilated cardiomyopathy. Bleomycin damages the lung, cisplatin the kidney and vincristine the peripheral nerves, so each has its own signature organ.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-2`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Mesna is given with cyclophosphamide to prevent:`,options:[`Haemorrhagic cystitis`,`Cardiotoxicity`,`Pulmonary fibrosis`,`Peripheral neuropathy`],answer:0,explanation:`Cyclophosphamide metabolism produces acrolein, which injures the bladder urothelium; mesna donates a free sulfhydryl group that conjugates acrolein in the urine. Dexrazoxane is the agent used against anthracycline cardiotoxicity.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-3`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Paclitaxel and vincristine both arrest cells in mitosis, but their mechanisms differ in that:`,options:[`Paclitaxel prevents tubulin polymerisation while vincristine stabilises microtubules`,`Paclitaxel stabilises microtubules and prevents depolymerisation while vincristine prevents polymerisation`,`Both prevent polymerisation but at different tubulin sites`,`Paclitaxel inhibits topoisomerase II while vincristine binds tubulin`],answer:1,explanation:`Taxanes bind beta-tubulin and hyperstabilise microtubules so the spindle cannot disassemble, whereas vinca alkaloids prevent tubulin polymerising so the spindle never forms. The two are mechanistically opposite yet both cause metaphase arrest, which is precisely why the pairing is tested.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-4`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which of the following is a cell cycle non-specific anticancer agent?`,options:[`Methotrexate`,`Vincristine`,`Cyclophosphamide`,`Cytarabine`],answer:2,explanation:`Alkylating agents such as cyclophosphamide damage DNA in any phase of the cycle, including resting cells, so they are cell cycle non-specific. Methotrexate and cytarabine act in S phase and vincristine in M phase, all of which require the cell to be actively cycling.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-5`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Imatinib is effective in chronic myeloid leukaemia because it inhibits:`,options:[`HER2 receptor`,`VEGF`,`The BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase`,`CD20 on B cells`],answer:2,explanation:`Imatinib occupies the ATP binding site of the BCR-ABL fusion kinase produced by the Philadelphia chromosome translocation t(9;22), and also inhibits c-KIT, which is why it works in GIST. HER2 is targeted by trastuzumab and CD20 by rituximab.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-6`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Ciclosporin suppresses immunity by:`,options:[`Binding cyclophilin and inhibiting calcineurin, reducing IL-2 production`,`Inhibiting mTOR after binding FKBP-12`,`Blocking inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase`,`Depleting circulating T lymphocytes directly`],answer:0,explanation:`Ciclosporin binds cyclophilin, and the complex inhibits calcineurin so NFAT is not dephosphorylated and IL-2 transcription falls, blocking T cell activation. Sirolimus is the drug that binds FKBP-12 and inhibits mTOR, and mycophenolate blocks IMP dehydrogenase.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-7`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Folinic acid rather than folic acid is used to rescue normal cells after high-dose methotrexate because:`,options:[`Folic acid is not absorbed at high doses`,`Folinic acid is already reduced and bypasses the blocked dihydrofolate reductase`,`Folic acid increases methotrexate renal clearance`,`Folinic acid displaces methotrexate from plasma proteins`],answer:1,explanation:`Methotrexate blocks dihydrofolate reductase, so folic acid cannot be converted to its active form and gives no rescue. Folinic acid is N5-formyl tetrahydrofolate, already in the reduced state, and enters the pathway beyond the block. Timing is critical, typically 24 to 36 hours after the methotrexate dose.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-8`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A tumour of 10 to the power 12 cells is treated with a regimen that produces a 3-log kill each cycle. How many cells remain after two cycles, and what does this illustrate?`,options:[`10 to the power 9, illustrating zero-order kill`,`10 to the power 6, illustrating first-order fractional cell kill`,`10 to the power 3, illustrating complete eradication`,`Zero, illustrating cure after two cycles`],answer:1,explanation:`Each 3-log kill removes 99.9 percent of the cells, so 10^12 becomes 10^9 after one cycle and 10^6 after two. Chemotherapy kills a constant fraction rather than a constant number of cells, which is first-order kinetics, and it is exactly why many repeated cycles are needed and why a residual burden is not cure.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-1`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The specific antidote for opioid overdose is:`,options:[`Naloxone`,`Flumazenil`,`Pralidoxime`,`Protamine sulfate`],answer:0,explanation:`Naloxone is a competitive antagonist at the mu opioid receptor and rapidly reverses respiratory depression and coma. Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines, pralidoxime reactivates cholinesterase in organophosphate poisoning and protamine neutralises heparin.`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-2`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Activated charcoal is NOT useful in poisoning by:`,options:[`Carbamazepine`,`Theophylline`,`Iron salts`,`Phenobarbitone`],answer:2,explanation:`Charcoal adsorbs organic molecules but not metal ions, so it does not bind iron, lithium, alcohols or strong acids and alkalis. Carbamazepine, theophylline and phenobarbitone are all well adsorbed and are classic indications for multiple-dose charcoal.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-3`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In organophosphate poisoning, the end point for adequate atropinisation is:`,options:[`Pupillary dilatation`,`Drying of secretions with a clear chest`,`A heart rate above 140 per minute`,`Complete resolution of muscle fasciculations`],answer:1,explanation:`Death is caused by bronchorrhoea and bronchospasm, so atropine is titrated until secretions dry and the chest is clear. Pupil size is an unreliable guide because miosis may persist from local exposure, and fasciculations are nicotinic and are not relieved by atropine at all, only by an oxime.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-4`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Ethanol or fomepizole is used in methanol poisoning because it:`,options:[`Competitively inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and prevents formation of formic acid`,`Neutralises formic acid chemically`,`Increases renal excretion of methanol`,`Blocks the absorption of methanol from the stomach`],answer:0,explanation:`Methanol is harmless until alcohol dehydrogenase converts it to formaldehyde and then formic acid, which causes blindness and severe acidosis. Ethanol has far higher affinity for the enzyme and fomepizole is a direct inhibitor, so the toxic metabolite is never formed and the parent alcohol is cleared or dialysed off.`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-5`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Sodium nitrite is given in cyanide poisoning in order to:`,options:[`Chelate cyanide directly in plasma`,`Generate methaemoglobin, whose ferric iron competes with cytochrome oxidase for cyanide`,`Alkalinise the urine to trap cyanide`,`Donate sulfur for conversion to thiocyanate`],answer:1,explanation:`Nitrite oxidises haemoglobin to methaemoglobin, which contains ferric iron that binds cyanide as cyanmethaemoglobin and pulls it off cytochrome oxidase. Sodium thiosulfate then supplies the sulfur for rhodanese to make excretable thiocyanate, so the two are given in sequence, not as alternatives.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-6`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which chelator would you select for a child with symptomatic inorganic lead poisoning, and which must be avoided?`,options:[`Use plain disodium EDTA; avoid succimer`,`Use calcium disodium EDTA or succimer; avoid plain disodium EDTA`,`Use desferrioxamine; avoid penicillamine`,`Use Prussian blue; avoid dimercaprol`],answer:1,explanation:`Calcium disodium EDTA exchanges its calcium for lead and succimer is an effective oral option in children. Plain disodium EDTA must never be used because it avidly binds plasma calcium and can cause fatal hypocalcaemic tetany. Desferrioxamine is for iron and Prussian blue for thallium and caesium.`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-9`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A patient collapses with stridor, wheeze and hypotension minutes after a penicillin injection. The first drug to give is:`,options:[`Intramuscular adrenaline`,`Intravenous hydrocortisone`,`Intravenous chlorpheniramine`,`Nebulised salbutamol`],answer:0,explanation:`Adrenaline given intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh is the only agent that reverses all three problems at once: alpha-1 vasoconstriction restores blood pressure, beta-2 action relieves bronchospasm and it stabilises mast cells. Hydrocortisone takes hours to act and only prevents a late-phase reaction, chlorpheniramine treats urticaria but not shock, and salbutamol does nothing for the hypotension.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-10`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Sumatriptan aborts a migraine attack by acting as:`,options:[`A 5-HT2A antagonist`,`A 5-HT3 antagonist`,`A 5-HT1B and 5-HT1D agonist`,`A 5-HT4 partial agonist`],answer:2,explanation:`Sumatriptan is an agonist at 5-HT1B receptors on cranial vessels, producing vasoconstriction, and at 5-HT1D receptors on trigeminal endings, inhibiting release of CGRP and other neuropeptides. Blockade of 5-HT2A describes methysergide and cyproheptadine, which are prophylactic rather than abortive, and 5-HT3 blockade is the antiemetic action of ondansetron.`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-11`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Selective COX-2 inhibitors spare the stomach but raise cardiovascular risk because:`,options:[`They inhibit platelet thromboxane far more than vascular prostacyclin`,`They suppress endothelial prostacyclin while leaving platelet thromboxane A2 intact`,`They cause sodium loss and hypovolaemia`,`They are converted to a vasoconstrictor metabolite in the liver`],answer:1,explanation:`Endothelial prostacyclin, which is antiaggregatory and vasodilator, comes largely from COX-2, whereas platelet thromboxane A2 comes from COX-1; blocking only COX-2 tips the balance towards thrombosis. Platelets contain essentially no COX-2, so the claim that these drugs inhibit platelet thromboxane preferentially is exactly backwards. Sodium is retained rather than lost, which adds hypertension to the risk.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-12`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`In doses below about 2 g per day, aspirin alters uric acid handling by:`,options:[`Increasing urate excretion, so plasma urate falls`,`Inhibiting xanthine oxidase`,`Having no effect on urate at any dose`,`Decreasing urate excretion, so plasma urate rises`],answer:3,explanation:`Aspirin has a dose-dependent, biphasic effect on the urate transporters: low doses inhibit tubular secretion and cause urate retention, while doses above about 5 g per day also block reabsorption and become uricosuric. Because the low-dose effect predominates in practice, aspirin is a poor analgesic choice in a gouty patient. Xanthine oxidase inhibition belongs to allopurinol and febuxostat.`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-13`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 70-year-old with heart failure on ramipril and furosemide starts diclofenac for back pain, and his serum creatinine rises sharply within a week. The mechanism is:`,options:[`Diclofenac blocks prostaglandin-mediated dilatation of the afferent arteriole, so filtration pressure collapses`,`Diclofenac displaces furosemide from albumin and causes over-diuresis`,`Diclofenac directly destroys proximal tubular epithelium, causing acute tubular necrosis`,`Ramipril and diclofenac form an insoluble complex in the tubular lumen`],answer:0,explanation:`In a volume-depleted patient renal perfusion depends on PGE2 and prostacyclin keeping the afferent arteriole open; an NSAID removes that support while the ACE inhibitor simultaneously removes efferent constriction, so glomerular filtration falls, the classic triple whammy of diuretic, ACE inhibitor and NSAID. Analgesic nephropathy with tubular and papillary damage is a chronic problem after years of use, not a change over one week.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-14`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient stabilised on azathioprine is given full-dose allopurinol for gout. The predictable consequence is:`,options:[`Loss of the immunosuppressive effect of azathioprine`,`Severe bone marrow suppression`,`Acute haemolysis from oxidative stress`,`Urate crystal deposition in the renal tubules`],answer:1,explanation:`Azathioprine is converted to 6-mercaptopurine, which is inactivated chiefly by xanthine oxidase; allopurinol blocks that enzyme, so 6-mercaptopurine levels rise several-fold and profound myelosuppression follows. The thiopurine dose must be cut to roughly a quarter or the pair avoided, and febuxostat is no safer since it inhibits the same enzyme. Enzyme inhibition raises, not lowers, the immunosuppressive effect.`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-15`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A woman with asthma and nasal polyps develops severe bronchospasm within an hour of taking aspirin. The best explanation is:`,options:[`IgE antibodies directed against salicylate`,`Direct histamine release from lung mast cells by the salicylate molecule`,`Blockade of COX-1 shunting arachidonic acid down the 5-lipoxygenase route, with a surge of cysteinyl leukotrienes`,`Antagonism of leukotriene receptors in the airway`],answer:2,explanation:`Aspirin-exacerbated respiratory disease is pharmacological, not immunological: inhibiting COX-1 both removes inhibitory PGE2 and diverts arachidonic acid to 5-lipoxygenase, so LTC4, LTD4 and LTE4 rise sharply. Because it is not IgE mediated, every COX-1 inhibitor cross-reacts, while selective COX-2 agents and low-dose paracetamol are usually tolerated, and montelukast is useful prophylaxis.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-autacoids-nsaids-16`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`The half-life of salicylate rises from about 3 to 4 hours at analgesic doses to 15 to 30 hours in overdose because:`,options:[`Salicylate induces the enzymes that metabolise it`,`Plasma protein binding increases as concentration rises`,`Renal blood flow falls at toxic concentrations`,`Glycine and glucuronide conjugation saturate, so elimination shifts towards zero order`],answer:3,explanation:`Conjugation with glycine to salicyluric acid is a capacity-limited pathway that saturates within the therapeutic range, so above it a small increase in dose produces a disproportionately large rise in plasma level and a lengthening apparent half-life. Protein binding in fact falls as albumin sites saturate, which raises the free fraction and worsens toxicity, the opposite of the binding claim. Salicylate is not an enzyme inducer.`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-9`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Radioactive iodine (I-131) therapy for hyperthyroidism is absolutely contraindicated in:`,options:[`Pregnancy and lactation`,`An elderly patient with ischaemic heart disease`,`Relapse after previous thyroid surgery`,`A patient who has developed a rash on carbimazole`],answer:0,explanation:`Iodide crosses the placenta and is concentrated by the fetal thyroid from about 10 to 12 weeks, so I-131 ablates the fetal gland; it is also secreted in milk. The elderly patient with heart disease is in fact a preferred candidate because surgery is risky, and a rash on carbimazole is a reason to consider radioiodine rather than avoid it.`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-10`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Levothyroxine is best taken:`,options:[`With breakfast to improve tolerance`,`On an empty stomach 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast`,`Together with the daily calcium and iron supplement`,`At bedtime immediately after a milky drink`],answer:1,explanation:`Absorption of levothyroxine occurs in the jejunum and ileum and is reduced by food, and specifically by calcium salts, ferrous sulfate, antacids and soya, which bind the drug in the gut. Taking it fasting 30 to 60 minutes before breakfast gives consistent absorption; if any of those supplements are needed they should be separated by about 4 hours.`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-11`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A type 1 diabetic takes a total daily insulin dose of 60 units of rapid-acting analogue and basal insulin. Using the 1800 rule, how much correction insulin is needed to bring a pre-meal glucose of 280 mg/dL down to a target of 130 mg/dL?`,options:[`3 units`,`5 units`,`8 units`,`12 units`],answer:1,explanation:`The correction factor is 1800 divided by the total daily dose, so 1800 / 60 = 30 mg/dL fall per unit. The excess is 280 - 130 = 150 mg/dL, and 150 / 30 = 5 units. Note the rule must match the insulin: for regular soluble insulin the 1500 rule is used, which would give 1500 / 60 = 25 mg/dL per unit and a 6 unit answer.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-12`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A woman with Graves disease is 8 weeks pregnant. The correct approach to antithyroid drug therapy is:`,options:[`Carbimazole throughout, because propylthiouracil is teratogenic`,`Radioiodine ablation, which avoids drug exposure altogether`,`Propylthiouracil in the first trimester, switching to carbimazole afterwards`,`Stop both thioamides and control symptoms with a beta blocker alone`],answer:2,explanation:`Methimazole and its prodrug carbimazole are linked to an embryopathy of aplasia cutis and choanal or oesophageal atresia during organogenesis, so propylthiouracil is used in the first trimester; because propylthiouracil carries the higher risk of severe hepatotoxicity, many switch back afterwards. Radioiodine is absolutely contraindicated as it destroys the fetal thyroid, and a beta blocker treats symptoms without controlling hormone synthesis.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-13`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which oral antidiabetic should be avoided in a patient with congestive cardiac failure because it causes fluid retention?`,options:[`Sitagliptin`,`Pioglitazone`,`Acarbose`,`Glimepiride`],answer:1,explanation:`Pioglitazone activates PPAR-gamma in the collecting duct, increasing sodium and water reabsorption, so it causes oedema, weight gain and worsening heart failure; fracture risk and bladder cancer concerns add to the cautions. Acarbose acts only in the gut lumen and glimepiride causes weight gain through insulin release rather than by retaining fluid.`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-14`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A type 1 diabetic on insulin is started on propranolol. The specific hazard is:`,options:[`Insulin resistance leading to ketoacidosis`,`Increased renal clearance of insulin`,`Direct destruction of pancreatic beta cells`,`Masking of the adrenergic warning signs of hypoglycaemia and delayed glucose recovery`],answer:3,explanation:`Non-selective beta blockade abolishes the tachycardia, palpitation and tremor that warn the patient of a falling glucose, while sweating, which is cholinergic, persists as the only clue; blocking beta-2 receptors also impairs hepatic glycogenolysis so recovery is slower. If a beta blocker is essential, a cardioselective agent is preferred. Insulin clearance is not appreciably altered.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-15`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on bedtime NPH insulin has persistently high fasting glucose. A 3 a.m. sample reads 45 mg/dL. The correct action is:`,options:[`Increase the bedtime NPH dose`,`Reduce the bedtime NPH dose or add a bedtime snack`,`Add a sulfonylurea in the morning`,`Change the dinner-time regular insulin to a larger dose`],answer:1,explanation:`A low 3 a.m. value identifies the Somogyi phenomenon, in which nocturnal hypoglycaemia triggers a counter-regulatory surge of glucagon, cortisol and catecholamines and rebound morning hyperglycaemia, so the evening insulin must be reduced. Raising the bedtime dose would be right only for the dawn phenomenon, where the 3 a.m. glucose is normal or high and morning hyperglycaemia reflects the early-morning growth hormone and cortisol rise.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-16`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`An 80-year-old with an eGFR of 35 mL/min/1.73 m2 has repeated night-time hypoglycaemia on glibenclamide. The most appropriate change is to:`,options:[`Switch to chlorpropamide, which is better tolerated in the elderly`,`Keep glibenclamide but give the whole dose with dinner`,`Add metformin so that a smaller dose of glibenclamide can be used`,`Switch to gliclazide or to a DPP-4 inhibitor with appropriate dose adjustment`],answer:3,explanation:`Glibenclamide is long-acting and generates renally excreted active metabolites, so it accumulates in renal impairment and in the elderly and is the sulfonylurea most often responsible for prolonged hypoglycaemia. Gliclazide and glipizide are shorter acting and largely inactivated in the liver, and DPP-4 inhibitors carry almost no hypoglycaemia risk. Chlorpropamide is worse still, with a half-life of over 30 hours and a tendency to cause hyponatraemia, and metformin does not correct hypoglycaemia caused by another drug.`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-9`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which corticosteroid is given to a mother in threatened preterm labour to accelerate fetal lung maturity?`,options:[`Betamethasone`,`Hydrocortisone`,`Prednisolone`,`Fludrocortisone`],answer:0,explanation:`Betamethasone, and equally dexamethasone, escape inactivation by placental 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, so they reach the fetus and induce surfactant production over 24 to 48 hours. Hydrocortisone and prednisolone are largely destroyed by that placental enzyme, and fludrocortisone is a mineralocorticoid with no role here.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-10`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Mifepristone, used with misoprostol for medical termination of pregnancy, is:`,options:[`An oestrogen receptor antagonist`,`A prostaglandin E1 analogue`,`A progesterone receptor antagonist`,`An aromatase inhibitor`],answer:2,explanation:`Mifepristone blocks the progesterone receptor at the decidua, so the pregnancy is no longer maintained and the cervix softens and the myometrium is sensitised to prostaglandin. Misoprostol is the prostaglandin E1 analogue given 36 to 48 hours later to expel the products, so the two act at different targets rather than being alternatives.`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-11`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Clomiphene citrate induces ovulation by:`,options:[`Blocking hypothalamic and pituitary oestrogen receptors so GnRH and gonadotrophin output rise`,`Acting as a direct agonist at the ovarian FSH receptor`,`Inhibiting aromatase and lowering oestrogen synthesis`,`Suppressing prolactin through dopamine D2 agonism`],answer:0,explanation:`Clomiphene is a SERM that occupies hypothalamic oestrogen receptors and removes the negative feedback of endogenous oestrogen, so GnRH pulses increase and FSH and LH rise enough to recruit a follicle. Aromatase inhibition describes letrozole, which is also used for ovulation induction but by lowering oestrogen synthesis, and dopamine agonists such as bromocriptine are used only when anovulation is due to hyperprolactinaemia.`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-12`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which is an absolute contraindication to a combined oral contraceptive?`,options:[`Acne in a 22-year-old woman`,`Well-controlled hypothyroidism on levothyroxine`,`A 36-year-old smoker with migraine with aura`,`A family history of type 2 diabetes`],answer:2,explanation:`Ethinylestradiol raises clotting factor synthesis, and smoking above the age of 35 together with migraine with aura multiplies the risk of arterial thrombosis and ischaemic stroke, so a combined pill is barred and a progestogen-only method is used. Acne is actually an indication, since the pill raises sex hormone binding globulin and lowers free androgen, and hypothyroidism only calls for a levothyroxine dose review because oestrogen raises thyroxine binding globulin.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-13`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Levonorgestrel 1.5 mg taken as emergency contraception works chiefly by:`,options:[`Blocking the progesterone receptor at the endometrium`,`Dislodging an already implanted embryo`,`Permanently thinning the endometrium`,`Delaying or inhibiting ovulation`],answer:3,explanation:`A single 1.5 mg dose of levonorgestrel suppresses or postpones the LH surge, so ovulation does not occur in that cycle, with thickened cervical mucus as a secondary effect; it should be taken as early as possible and within 72 hours. It is not an abortifacient and has no effect once implantation has occurred. Progesterone receptor blockade describes ulipristal and mifepristone.`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-14`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`When leuprolide is started for advanced prostate cancer, an antiandrogen such as bicalutamide is given for the first few weeks because:`,options:[`Bicalutamide prevents hepatic metabolism of leuprolide`,`Continuous GnRH agonism first raises LH and testosterone, causing a tumour flare`,`Leuprolide alone causes gynaecomastia that bicalutamide prevents`,`Leuprolide cannot reach prostatic tissue without an antiandrogen`],answer:1,explanation:`Physiological GnRH release is pulsatile; continuous exposure to an agonist first stimulates the pituitary, so testosterone surges for 1 to 2 weeks and can worsen bone pain or precipitate spinal cord compression before receptor down-regulation produces medical castration. Bicalutamide blocks the androgen receptor during that window. A GnRH antagonist such as degarelix avoids the flare altogether.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-15`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A woman with asthma and hypertension has postpartum haemorrhage that persists despite oxytocin. Which uterotonic is the safest next choice?`,options:[`Carboprost`,`Methylergometrine`,`Misoprostol`,`Ergotamine`],answer:2,explanation:`Misoprostol, a PGE1 analogue given rectally or sublingually, contracts the uterus without bronchoconstriction or a pressor effect, so it suits both comorbidities. Carboprost is 15-methyl PGF2-alpha and is contraindicated in asthma because it constricts bronchi, while methylergometrine causes vasoconstriction and is contraindicated in hypertension and pre-eclampsia. Ergotamine is a migraine drug and is not used obstetrically.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-steroids-reproductive-16`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Testosterone itself is ineffective by mouth. Which structural change makes an androgen orally active, and at what cost?`,options:[`Esterification at C17-beta, at the cost of poor aqueous solubility`,`17-alpha-alkylation, at the cost of cholestatic jaundice and hepatic injury`,`Aromatisation of ring A, at the cost of feminisation`,`5-alpha reduction, at the cost of prostatic enlargement`],answer:1,explanation:`Unmodified testosterone is almost completely metabolised on first pass through the liver, so it is given as a depot ester by injection or transdermally. Adding a methyl or ethyl group at the 17-alpha position, as in methyltestosterone and stanozolol, resists that metabolism and allows oral use but causes raised transaminases, cholestasis and peliosis hepatis. The C17-beta esters such as enanthate and cypionate exist to prolong depot release, not to permit oral dosing.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-9`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which drug acts by blocking the cysteinyl leukotriene CysLT1 receptor?`,options:[`Zileuton`,`Montelukast`,`Omalizumab`,`Sodium cromoglicate`],answer:1,explanation:`Montelukast and zafirlukast are CysLT1 receptor antagonists, taken orally once daily and particularly useful in exercise-induced and aspirin-sensitive asthma and in allergic rhinitis. Zileuton is the tempting distractor but inhibits the 5-lipoxygenase enzyme rather than the receptor; omalizumab is an anti-IgE antibody and cromoglicate a mast cell stabiliser.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-10`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which antitussive suppresses the cough centre without analgesic action or dependence at therapeutic doses?`,options:[`Codeine`,`Dextromethorphan`,`Bromhexine`,`Guaifenesin`],answer:1,explanation:`Dextromethorphan is the d-isomer of a codeine analogue and raises the medullary cough threshold, but it lacks analgesic activity, constipation and dependence liability at antitussive doses. Codeine is a true opioid antitussive, while bromhexine is a mucolytic and guaifenesin an expectorant, neither of which suppresses the reflex.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-11`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient with a coronary stent on clopidogrel is prescribed omeprazole for dyspepsia. The concern is that:`,options:[`Omeprazole inhibits CYP2C19 and reduces conversion of clopidogrel to its active metabolite`,`The rise in gastric pH chemically destroys clopidogrel`,`Clopidogrel prevents omeprazole reaching the parietal cell canaliculus`,`Both drugs compete for binding sites on plasma albumin`],answer:0,explanation:`Clopidogrel is a prodrug that must be oxidised by CYP2C19 to its active thiol metabolite, and omeprazole and esomeprazole are strong inhibitors of that isoenzyme, so antiplatelet activity falls. Pantoprazole or rabeprazole, which inhibit CYP2C19 only weakly, are preferred. Gastric acid does not degrade clopidogrel, and prasugrel is unaffected because it is activated by other enzymes.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-12`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sucralfate must be taken on an empty stomach and not together with an antacid because it:`,options:[`Is inactivated by pepsin in the fasting stomach`,`Needs an acidic pH to polymerise and adhere to the ulcer base`,`Is absorbed systemically only when the stomach is alkaline`,`Chelates the aluminium contained in the antacid`],answer:1,explanation:`Sucralfate is a sulfated sucrose-aluminium complex that polymerises below about pH 4 into a sticky paste which binds the exposed proteins of the ulcer crater, so raising the pH defeats it. It is essentially not absorbed, and it binds phenytoin, digoxin, levothyroxine and fluoroquinolones in the gut, so other drugs must be separated by about 2 hours.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-13`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Misoprostol is effective in preventing NSAID-induced peptic ulcer, but its use is limited because it:`,options:[`Causes dose-related diarrhoea and stimulates the uterus, so it is contraindicated in pregnancy`,`Irreversibly inhibits the proton pump for several days`,`Causes severe constipation and faecal impaction`,`Is deposited in growing bone and teeth`],answer:0,explanation:`Misoprostol is a PGE1 analogue that restores the mucosal prostaglandin an NSAID removes, increasing mucus and bicarbonate and reducing acid, but cramping diarrhoea affects many patients and it contracts the uterus, so it must not be given to a woman who could be pregnant. That same uterine action is what makes it useful in medical abortion and postpartum haemorrhage.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-14`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Roflumilast, used in severe COPD with chronic bronchitis, is:`,options:[`A long-acting muscarinic antagonist`,`An anti-IgE monoclonal antibody`,`A selective phosphodiesterase-4 inhibitor`,`An inhibitor of leukotriene synthesis`],answer:2,explanation:`Roflumilast selectively inhibits PDE-4, raising cAMP in neutrophils and other inflammatory cells and reducing exacerbations; it is anti-inflammatory rather than a bronchodilator, and nausea, diarrhoea and weight loss limit its use. Tiotropium is the long-acting antimuscarinic and omalizumab the anti-IgE antibody, both used in obstructive airway disease but by quite different mechanisms.`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-15`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A 60 kg patient needs an intravenous loading dose of aminophylline to reach a plasma theophylline concentration of 15 mg/L. Theophylline Vd is 0.5 L/kg and the salt factor of aminophylline is 0.8. The loading dose is about:`,options:[`360 mg`,`450 mg`,`563 mg`,`720 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`Loading dose = target concentration x Vd / salt factor. Vd = 0.5 x 60 = 30 L, so the theophylline base required is 15 mg/L x 30 L = 450 mg. Aminophylline is only about 80 percent theophylline, so 450 / 0.8 = 563 mg of aminophylline is needed. Quoting 450 mg is the standard error of forgetting the salt factor.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-git-respiratory-16`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on cisplatin has no vomiting on day 1 with ondansetron and dexamethasone, but vomits repeatedly on days 2 to 4. The most appropriate addition is:`,options:[`Aprepitant`,`A doubled dose of ondansetron`,`Hyoscine transdermal patch`,`Loperamide`],answer:0,explanation:`Delayed emesis after cisplatin is mediated mainly by substance P acting at central NK1 receptors, so an NK1 antagonist such as aprepitant added to dexamethasone is the standard measure. Serotonin release drives only the first 24 hours, so escalating ondansetron adds headache and constipation without benefit, and hyoscine targets the vestibular pathway of motion sickness.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-9`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Mannitol is contraindicated in:`,options:[`Raised intracranial pressure`,`Acute pulmonary oedema with left ventricular failure`,`Acute angle-closure glaucoma awaiting surgery`,`Forced diuresis in early rhabdomyolysis`],answer:1,explanation:`Mannitol first expands the plasma volume by drawing water out of cells and interstitium, which a failing left ventricle cannot handle, so pulmonary oedema is precipitated; anuria is the other bar to its use. Reducing intracranial and intraocular pressure is precisely what it is given for, since it pulls water across intact barriers along an osmotic gradient.`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-10`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A man taking spironolactone for heart failure develops painful gynaecomastia. Which substitution avoids the gynaecomastia while keeping the mineralocorticoid receptor blockade and its survival benefit in heart failure?`,options:[`Eplerenone`,`Amiloride`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Bumetanide`],answer:0,explanation:`Spironolactone is a non-selective steroid that also blocks androgen receptors and interacts with progesterone receptors, so gynaecomastia, impotence and menstrual upset are common; eplerenone binds the mineralocorticoid receptor selectively and keeps the heart failure benefit without those effects. Amiloride spares potassium but works at the sodium channel and has no proven mortality benefit, and neither a thiazide nor a loop diuretic provides aldosterone blockade at all.`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-11`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Amiloride and triamterene retain potassium by:`,options:[`Competitive antagonism at the cytoplasmic mineralocorticoid receptor`,`Inhibiting the Na-K-2Cl symporter of the thick ascending limb`,`Blocking the epithelial sodium channel of the late distal tubule and collecting duct directly`,`Inhibiting carbonic anhydrase in the collecting duct`],answer:2,explanation:`Amiloride and triamterene plug the ENaC pore itself, so sodium entry falls, the lumen stays less electronegative and potassium secretion drops; because the action is independent of aldosterone it still works in an adrenalectomised patient, unlike spironolactone and eplerenone which need aldosterone to be driving the receptor. Amiloride is the specific choice in lithium-induced nephrogenic diabetes insipidus because lithium enters the cell through that same channel.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-12`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient on digoxin and furosemide develops nausea, yellow-green visual haloes and ventricular ectopics although the plasma digoxin level is only mid-range. The likeliest contributor is:`,options:[`Diuretic-induced hypokalaemia increasing digoxin binding to Na-K ATPase`,`Furosemide displacing digoxin from plasma albumin`,`Increased renal clearance of digoxin`,`Hypercalcaemia produced by the loop diuretic`],answer:0,explanation:`Potassium and digoxin compete for the same site on the sodium pump, so when a loop diuretic lowers plasma potassium, digoxin binds more avidly and toxicity appears at ordinary plasma levels; the accompanying hypomagnesaemia adds to the risk. Loop diuretics increase calcium excretion, so hypercalcaemia is the opposite of what they cause, and raised clearance would reduce rather than produce toxicity.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-13`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Using the Cockcroft-Gault equation, estimate the creatinine clearance of a 60-year-old man weighing 70 kg whose serum creatinine is 1.5 mg/dL.`,options:[`26 mL/min`,`52 mL/min`,`62 mL/min`,`78 mL/min`],answer:1,explanation:`CrCl = (140 - age) x body weight / (72 x serum creatinine) = (140 - 60) x 70 / (72 x 1.5) = 5600 / 108 = 51.9, so about 52 mL/min. For a woman the result is multiplied by 0.85, giving about 44 mL/min. A creatinine that looks only mildly raised therefore already means renally cleared drugs such as digoxin, aminoglycosides and metformin need dose adjustment.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-14`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient with resistant oedema stops responding to a high dose of furosemide. Adding which drug restores the diuresis, and on what principle?`,options:[`A second loop diuretic, doubling the block at the same transporter`,`Metolazone, blocking the distal reabsorption that has compensated for the loop block`,`Mannitol, osmotically drawing sodium back into the tubule`,`Acetazolamide, blocking the same symporter in the collecting duct`],answer:1,explanation:`Chronic loop diuretic therapy causes the distal convoluted tubule to hypertrophy and reabsorb the extra sodium delivered to it, so adding a thiazide-like drug such as metolazone blocks that adaptation, a strategy called sequential nephron blockade; the combination is powerful and demands close monitoring for hypokalaemia and volume depletion. A second loop agent merely competes for the same carrier, and acetazolamide acts in the proximal tubule, not the collecting duct.`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-15`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A nephrotic patient needs unusually large doses of furosemide. The best explanation is:`,options:[`The nephrotic kidney metabolises furosemide rapidly`,`Nephrotic urine is alkaline, so the drug is ionised and inactive`,`Oedema fluid dilutes the drug within the interstitium`,`Low plasma albumin reduces delivery to the secretory transporter, and albumin in the tubular fluid binds the drug that does get there`],answer:3,explanation:`Furosemide must be carried to the proximal tubule bound to albumin and secreted by the organic anion transporter to reach its luminal site of action, so hypoalbuminaemia cuts that delivery, and the albumin leaking into the filtrate binds whatever reaches the lumen. Larger single doses rather than more frequent small ones are used because the dose-response relationship has a threshold and is steep above it. Furosemide undergoes little metabolism, and its activity does not depend on urinary pH.`},{id:`q-ph-diuretics-renal-16`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Long-term furosemide in heart failure has produced a hypokalaemic, hypochloraemic metabolic alkalosis. Which drug can be added to correct the acid-base disturbance while maintaining diuresis?`,options:[`Acetazolamide`,`Hydrochlorothiazide`,`Mannitol`,`Torsemide`],answer:0,explanation:`Acetazolamide makes the kidney excrete bicarbonate in an alkaline urine, which is exactly what is needed to reverse the alkalosis, and it adds a mild proximal diuresis; potassium must be replaced because it also increases potassium loss. A thiazide or another loop diuretic such as torsemide would deepen the alkalosis by increasing distal sodium-for-hydrogen exchange, and mannitol does not alter bicarbonate handling.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-9`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Which antibacterial acts by inhibiting DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV?`,options:[`Rifampicin`,`Metronidazole`,`Ciprofloxacin`,`Trimethoprim`],answer:2,explanation:`Fluoroquinolones such as ciprofloxacin inhibit DNA gyrase in gram-negative organisms and topoisomerase IV in gram-positive ones, so supercoiling fails and the chromosome fragments, giving rapid bactericidal action. Rifampicin inhibits DNA-dependent RNA polymerase, metronidazole is reduced to a nitro radical that breaks DNA directly, and trimethoprim blocks dihydrofolate reductase.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-10`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Absorption of a tetracycline is markedly reduced when it is taken with:`,options:[`A glass of water on an empty stomach`,`Milk, antacids or iron tablets`,`Vitamin C`,`A high-protein meal`],answer:1,explanation:`Tetracyclines chelate divalent and trivalent cations, so calcium in milk and antacids, magnesium and aluminium in antacids, and iron in haematinics all form non-absorbable complexes; a two-hour gap is needed. Doxycycline and minocycline are more lipid soluble and much less affected, which is one reason they are preferred. Water on an empty stomach gives the best absorption rather than the worst.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-11`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A 70-year-old taking prednisolone is given levofloxacin and develops sudden pain and swelling behind the ankle. The likeliest cause is:`,options:[`Achilles tendinitis or rupture`,`Acute gout of the ankle`,`Deep vein thrombosis`,`Fluoroquinolone-induced peripheral neuropathy`],answer:0,explanation:`Fluoroquinolones impair tenocyte collagen matrix and the risk of tendinitis and rupture is highest above 60 years of age, with concurrent corticosteroids and in transplant recipients; the drug must be stopped and the tendon rested. Fluoroquinolones do also cause a peripheral neuropathy, but that presents as burning and numbness in a stocking distribution rather than sudden focal tendon pain, and acute gout classically strikes the first metatarsophalangeal joint.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-12`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Aminoglycosides must be used with great caution in myasthenia gravis and just after anaesthesia with a neuromuscular blocker because they:`,options:[`Inhibit acetylcholinesterase at the end plate`,`Reduce calcium-dependent release of acetylcholine and lower end-plate sensitivity`,`Block muscarinic receptors on skeletal muscle`,`Cause hypokalaemia severe enough to paralyse muscle`],answer:1,explanation:`Aminoglycosides interfere with calcium entry at the motor nerve terminal so less acetylcholine is released, and they also reduce postjunctional sensitivity, which deepens residual neuromuscular blockade and can cause apnoea; calcium gluconate and neostigmine are used to reverse it. Inhibiting cholinesterase would improve myasthenia rather than worsen it, and skeletal muscle end plates carry nicotinic, not muscarinic, receptors.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-13`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient taking metronidazole drinks alcohol and develops flushing, throbbing headache, vomiting and hypotension. The mechanism is:`,options:[`Inhibition of aldehyde dehydrogenase with accumulation of acetaldehyde`,`Additive central nervous system depression`,`Inhibition of alcohol dehydrogenase with accumulation of ethanol`,`Displacement of ethanol from plasma proteins`],answer:0,explanation:`Metronidazole produces a disulfiram-like reaction, so alcohol must be avoided during treatment and for about 48 hours afterwards; tinidazole, cefoperazone, chlorpropamide and griseofulvin behave similarly. Blocking alcohol dehydrogenase, which is what fomepizole does, would raise ethanol but not acetaldehyde and would not cause this syndrome.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-14`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Why is a tetracycline not combined with a penicillin in pneumococcal meningitis?`,options:[`The two chelate one another in plasma`,`Tetracycline induces the enzymes that metabolise penicillin`,`A bacteriostatic drug stops cell growth, so the cell-wall-active penicillin loses its target`,`Both compete for the same renal tubular secretory carrier`],answer:2,explanation:`Penicillins kill only organisms that are actively growing and cross-linking new peptidoglycan, so a bacteriostatic drug that arrests division antagonises them, a result demonstrated classically in pneumococcal meningitis. Competition for tubular secretion is a real phenomenon, as with probenecid, but it raises penicillin levels rather than reducing efficacy.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-15`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on fluoxetine is given linezolid and becomes agitated and hyperthermic with hyperreflexia and clonus. The explanation is that linezolid:`,options:[`Is a reversible non-selective monoamine oxidase inhibitor, so serotonin accumulates`,`Is a direct agonist at 5-HT2A receptors`,`Blocks the hepatic metabolism of fluoxetine`,`Displaces fluoxetine from plasma albumin`],answer:0,explanation:`Besides binding the 50S subunit and preventing formation of the initiation complex, linezolid inhibits monoamine oxidase reversibly, so combining it with an SSRI, pethidine or a sympathomimetic risks serotonin syndrome and tyramine-rich foods should be limited. Linezolid is not a cytochrome P450 substrate or inhibitor, so a metabolic interaction cannot be the answer; its own long-term hazards are reversible myelosuppression and optic neuropathy.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-antibacterial-16`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Ceftriaxone is avoided in the neonate, especially one receiving calcium-containing intravenous fluids, because:`,options:[`Calcium chemically opens the beta-lactam ring`,`Calcium prevents its biliary excretion`,`It chelates plasma calcium and causes hypocalcaemic tetany`,`It forms insoluble calcium-ceftriaxone precipitates in lung and kidney and displaces bilirubin from albumin`],answer:3,explanation:`Fatal precipitates have been reported when ceftriaxone and calcium-containing fluids are given to neonates, and because ceftriaxone is highly protein bound it displaces bilirubin from albumin and can precipitate kernicterus in a jaundiced newborn, so cefotaxime is the preferred third-generation cephalosporin at that age. Ceftriaxone is largely excreted in bile, which is why it needs no renal dose adjustment but can cause biliary sludging.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-9`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A patient two months into antitubercular therapy reports blurred vision and difficulty telling red from green. The drug responsible is:`,options:[`Ethambutol`,`Isoniazid`,`Rifampicin`,`Pyrazinamide`],answer:0,explanation:`Ethambutol causes a dose-related retrobulbar optic neuritis with loss of acuity and of red-green discrimination, usually reversible if the drug is stopped at once, so vision is checked at baseline and monthly and the drug is avoided in small children who cannot report symptoms. Isoniazid damages peripheral nerves rather than the optic nerve, pyrazinamide raises uric acid and rifampicin causes hepatitis with orange secretions.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-10`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The dose-limiting toxicity of conventional amphotericin B is:`,options:[`Optic neuritis`,`Nephrotoxicity`,`Aplastic anaemia`,`Pulmonary fibrosis`],answer:1,explanation:`Amphotericin B binds host cholesterol as well as fungal ergosterol, injuring the renal tubule and producing azotaemia, potassium and magnesium wasting and distal renal tubular acidosis; saline preloading and lipid or liposomal formulations reduce it. The chills, fever and rigors during infusion are a separate infusion reaction managed with premedication, not the dose-limiting problem.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-11`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Pyrazinamide earns its place in the intensive phase of antitubercular therapy because it:`,options:[`Is uniquely active at the acidic pH inside macrophages, killing intracellular bacilli`,`Is the only drug that kills fast-multiplying extracellular bacilli`,`Prevents rifampicin resistance by binding RNA polymerase`,`Works only in the alkaline environment of an open cavity`],answer:0,explanation:`Pyrazinamide is a prodrug converted by mycobacterial pyrazinamidase to pyrazinoic acid and is sterilising at the acid pH of the phagolysosome, which is why adding it allowed the regimen to be shortened from nine months to six. Its costs are hepatotoxicity and hyperuricaemia, since pyrazinoic acid blocks urate secretion. Rifampicin is the drug that binds RNA polymerase and covers the actively dividing extracellular population.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-12`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient on a ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitor develops pulmonary tuberculosis. The correct step is to:`,options:[`Give rifampicin as usual, since it does not affect protease inhibitors`,`Stop antiretroviral therapy until antitubercular treatment is complete`,`Substitute rifabutin for rifampicin, with dose adjustment`,`Halve the dose of the protease inhibitor and continue rifampicin`],answer:2,explanation:`Rifampicin is a powerful inducer of CYP3A4 and drives protease inhibitor concentrations to subtherapeutic levels, inviting virological failure and resistance; rifabutin induces the enzyme far more weakly and is the accepted substitute, with dose modification in both directions. Interrupting antiretroviral therapy during tuberculosis increases mortality, and reducing the protease inhibitor dose would worsen the very problem.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-13`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Terbinafine, the oral drug of choice for onychomycosis, acts by:`,options:[`Binding ergosterol and forming membrane pores`,`Inhibiting fungal 14-alpha-demethylase`,`Inhibiting squalene epoxidase, so squalene accumulates and ergosterol is lost`,`Inhibiting beta-1,3-glucan synthase`],answer:2,explanation:`Terbinafine blocks squalene epoxidase at an early step of ergosterol synthesis, and the squalene that piles up is itself fungicidal for dermatophytes; the drug concentrates in keratin, nail and sebum, which is why it outperforms griseofulvin in tinea unguium. Blocking 14-alpha-demethylase is the azole mechanism, pore formation is amphotericin B and glucan synthase inhibition is the echinocandins.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-14`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient on simvastatin is started on itraconazole and within two weeks has severe muscle pain with a markedly raised creatine kinase. The interaction is that:`,options:[`Itraconazole inhibits CYP3A4, so simvastatin accumulates and causes rhabdomyolysis`,`Simvastatin blocks the absorption of itraconazole`,`Itraconazole induces simvastatin metabolism to a myotoxic product`,`Both drugs compete for the same renal transporter`],answer:0,explanation:`Itraconazole and ketoconazole are among the strongest CYP3A4 inhibitors, and simvastatin, lovastatin and atorvastatin are CYP3A4 substrates, so exposure can rise several-fold and precipitate myopathy or rhabdomyolysis; pravastatin and rosuvastatin, which avoid that pathway, are safer partners. Induction would lower the statin concentration rather than raise it.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-15`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which test must be done before starting abacavir, and why?`,options:[`HLA-B*5701 typing, because carriers suffer a severe hypersensitivity reaction`,`G6PD assay, because of oxidative haemolysis`,`Audiometry, because of cochlear damage`,`Serum uric acid, because of urate nephropathy`],answer:0,explanation:`Abacavir hypersensitivity appears in the first six weeks with fever, rash, gastrointestinal and respiratory symptoms and can be fatal on rechallenge; it is tightly linked to HLA-B*5701, and routine screening has almost abolished it. G6PD screening belongs to primaquine, dapsone and rasburicase, and audiometry to aminoglycosides such as streptomycin.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-16`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Slow acetylators, who have low N-acetyltransferase 2 activity, are particularly prone to which effect of isoniazid?`,options:[`Orange discoloration of urine`,`Peripheral neuropathy and a lupus-like syndrome`,`Optic neuritis`,`Failure of a once-weekly intermittent regimen`],answer:1,explanation:`Acetylation of isoniazid is bimodally distributed, and slow acetylators, who form roughly half the Indian population, clear the drug slowly so plasma levels run higher; pyridoxine-responsive peripheral neuropathy and drug-induced lupus are commoner in them, which is why routine pyridoxine cover matters. It is the fast acetylator who risks subtherapeutic levels on widely spaced intermittent regimens. Optic neuritis belongs to ethambutol and orange urine to rifampicin, neither related to acetylator status.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-9`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`Allopurinol or rasburicase with generous hydration is given before treating a bulky lymphoma in order to prevent:`,options:[`Haemorrhagic cystitis`,`Anthracycline cardiomyopathy`,`Tumour lysis syndrome with urate nephropathy`,`Peripheral neuropathy`],answer:2,explanation:`Rapid destruction of a large tumour mass releases potassium, phosphate and nucleic acids, and the resulting urate crystallises in the renal tubules and causes acute kidney injury; allopurinol prevents further urate formation while rasburicase converts urate already present to soluble allantoin, though it must be avoided in G6PD deficiency. Mesna is the agent used against acrolein cystitis and dexrazoxane against anthracycline cardiotoxicity.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-10`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`5-fluorouracil kills tumour cells chiefly by:`,options:[`Cross-linking the two strands of DNA`,`Inhibiting thymidylate synthase after conversion to FdUMP`,`Inhibiting topoisomerase I`,`Preventing tubulin polymerisation`],answer:1,explanation:`5-fluorouracil is converted to FdUMP, which forms a stable ternary complex with thymidylate synthase and the folate cofactor, so dTMP cannot be made and the cell dies a thymineless death. Folinic acid is deliberately added here to stabilise that complex and increase toxicity, the exact opposite of its rescue role after methotrexate. Topoisomerase I inhibition describes irinotecan and topotecan.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-11`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which measure is used specifically to reduce the nephrotoxicity of cisplatin?`,options:[`Mesna`,`Dexrazoxane`,`Folinic acid rescue`,`Vigorous saline hydration with forced diuresis`],answer:3,explanation:`Cisplatin is concentrated by proximal tubular cells, so a high urine flow shortens contact time and limits injury; magnesium and potassium are replaced because they are wasted, and amifostine is a thiol scavenger licensed for the same purpose. Each of the other agents is matched to a different drug: mesna to the acrolein of cyclophosphamide, dexrazoxane to anthracycline cardiotoxicity and folinic acid to methotrexate.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-12`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A patient on weekly low-dose methotrexate for rheumatoid arthritis takes regular naproxen and develops mouth ulcers and pancytopenia. The mechanism is that NSAIDs:`,options:[`Compete for renal tubular secretion of methotrexate and reduce renal perfusion, so its level rises`,`Increase conversion of methotrexate to its polyglutamates in the gut`,`Are metabolised more slowly because methotrexate inhibits their clearance`,`Form an insoluble chelate with methotrexate in the intestine`],answer:0,explanation:`Methotrexate is cleared largely by renal tubular secretion, which NSAIDs, probenecid, penicillins and sulfonamides all compete with, and prostaglandin inhibition simultaneously lowers renal perfusion, so the plasma level climbs and mucositis and myelosuppression follow. Folic acid supplementation, a weekly rather than daily schedule and regular blood counts are the standard safeguards.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-13`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`By the Mosteller formula, BSA in m2 is the square root of (height in cm x weight in kg / 3600). What dose should a 160 cm, 60 kg patient receive of a drug prescribed at 75 mg/m2?`,options:[`98 mg`,`110 mg`,`122 mg`,`150 mg`],answer:2,explanation:`160 x 60 = 9600, and 9600 / 3600 = 2.67, whose square root is 1.63 m2. The dose is 1.63 x 75 = 122 mg. Body surface area is used rather than body weight because it tracks cardiac output, renal blood flow and hence drug clearance more closely, which matters for agents with so narrow a therapeutic index.`,tag:`Numerical`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-14`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Which adverse effect distinguishes ciclosporin from tacrolimus?`,options:[`Nephrotoxicity`,`Gum hypertrophy with hirsutism`,`Hypertension`,`Fine tremor`],answer:1,explanation:`Gingival hyperplasia and hirsutism are characteristic of ciclosporin and are absent with tacrolimus, so a patient troubled by them is often switched; tacrolimus in turn causes more new-onset diabetes, alopecia and neurotoxicity. Dose-related nephrotoxicity, hypertension and tremor occur with both calcineurin inhibitors, so none of those can separate them.`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-15`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A renal transplant patient stable on tacrolimus is given fluconazole for oesophageal candidiasis and develops tremor, headache and a rising creatinine. The reason is that:`,options:[`Fluconazole is directly nephrotoxic at antifungal doses`,`Tacrolimus prevents renal excretion of fluconazole`,`Fluconazole inhibits CYP3A4, so tacrolimus accumulates`,`The two act additively on calcineurin`],answer:2,explanation:`Tacrolimus and ciclosporin are CYP3A4 substrates with a narrow therapeutic index, so azoles, macrolides, diltiazem and grapefruit juice raise their levels and produce exactly this picture of neurotoxicity and nephrotoxicity, while rifampicin, phenytoin and St John wort lower them and risk rejection. The calcineurin inhibitor dose is cut and trough levels are monitored. Fluconazole has no calcineurin activity of its own.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-16`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`A patient on pembrolizumab develops severe colitis and hypothyroidism. These effects occur because the drug:`,options:[`Is directly cytotoxic to gut epithelium and thyroid follicular cells`,`Suppresses regulatory cytokines in the manner of ciclosporin`,`Cross-reacts with thyroid peroxidase`,`Blocks the PD-1 checkpoint, so unrestrained T cells attack normal tissue`],answer:3,explanation:`Checkpoint inhibitors work by removing the brake that PD-1, or CTLA-4 in the case of ipilimumab, places on T cells, and the price of that anti-tumour immunity is immune-related colitis, thyroiditis, hepatitis, pneumonitis and hypophysitis. Management is to withhold the drug and give high-dose corticosteroids, not simply antimotility treatment. These antibodies are immunomodulatory rather than directly cytotoxic.`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-7`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`A patient on warfarin presents with an INR of 9 and gum bleeding. The specific antidote is:`,options:[`Protamine sulfate`,`Vitamin K1 (phytomenadione)`,`N-acetylcysteine`,`Tranexamic acid alone`],answer:1,explanation:`Warfarin blocks vitamin K epoxide reductase so factors II, VII, IX and X cannot be carboxylated; phytomenadione restores that pathway, although the effect takes 6 to 12 hours, which is why fresh frozen plasma or prothrombin complex concentrate is added when bleeding is major. Protamine is the antidote to heparin, with which it forms an inactive complex, and has no effect on warfarin.`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-8`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`basic`,difficulty:`easy`,stem:`The definitive treatment of life-threatening digoxin toxicity with ventricular arrhythmia and hyperkalaemia is:`,options:[`Digoxin-specific antibody Fab fragments`,`Intravenous calcium gluconate`,`Atropine alone`,`Repeated gastric lavage`],answer:0,explanation:`Fab fragments bind digoxin with very high affinity and the complex is excreted in urine, reversing arrhythmia and hyperkalaemia within an hour. Calcium is specifically avoided in digoxin toxicity because it can produce a sustained refractory ventricular contraction, and atropine is only a holding measure for bradyarrhythmia.`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-9`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`Sodium bicarbonate is infused in salicylate poisoning in order to:`,options:[`Neutralise salicylic acid remaining in the stomach`,`Chelate salicylate in the plasma`,`Alkalinise the urine so ionised salicylate is trapped and excreted`,`Reduce further absorption by raising gastric pH`],answer:2,explanation:`Salicylic acid is a weak acid with a pKa near 3, so in alkaline urine it is almost entirely ionised and cannot diffuse back across the tubular membrane, which multiplies its excretion; raising plasma pH also draws salicylate out of the central nervous system. Hypokalaemia must be corrected first, because the kidney cannot excrete an alkaline urine while potassium depleted. Haemodialysis is reserved for very high levels or coma.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-10`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A toddler who swallowed ferrous sulfate tablets has vomiting, haematemesis and metabolic acidosis. The chelator of choice is:`,options:[`Repeated doses of activated charcoal`,`Dimercaprol`,`Desferrioxamine`,`Sodium calcium edetate`],answer:2,explanation:`Desferrioxamine binds ferric iron to form ferrioxamine, which is excreted in urine and imparts a characteristic vin rose colour used to gauge the response. Charcoal does not adsorb iron at all, so whole-bowel irrigation is the decontamination method. Dimercaprol is the antidote for arsenic, mercury and gold, and calcium disodium edetate for lead.`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-11`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A family rescued from a closed room with a smouldering coal fire is drowsy with headache and nausea, yet pulse oximetry reads 98 percent. The correct treatment is:`,options:[`High-flow 100 percent oxygen, with hyperbaric oxygen if severe`,`Intravenous methylene blue`,`Sodium thiosulfate infusion`,`Naloxone`],answer:0,explanation:`Carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin with over 200 times the affinity of oxygen and shifts the dissociation curve to the left, and an ordinary pulse oximeter cannot distinguish carboxyhaemoglobin from oxyhaemoglobin, so the saturation reading is falsely reassuring. High-flow oxygen shortens the carboxyhaemoglobin half-life from about 4 hours to under an hour. Methylene blue treats methaemoglobinaemia and thiosulfate is part of cyanide treatment.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-12`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`intermediate`,difficulty:`medium`,stem:`A young man who ate Datura seeds is delirious, with dry flushed skin, widely dilated pupils, fever and urinary retention. The specific antidote is:`,options:[`Neostigmine`,`Physostigmine`,`Pralidoxime`,`Atropine`],answer:1,explanation:`Physostigmine is a tertiary carbamate that crosses the blood-brain barrier, so it raises acetylcholine centrally as well as peripherally and reverses the anticholinergic delirium. Neostigmine is a quaternary compound and stays outside the central nervous system, so it cannot touch the delirium; atropine would deepen the poisoning and pralidoxime is used only in organophosphate poisoning. Cooling and benzodiazepines are the supportive measures.`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-13`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`Which poisoning is effectively treated by haemodialysis?`,options:[`Methanol`,`Digoxin`,`Amitriptyline`,`Diazepam`],answer:0,explanation:`Dialysis can only remove a substance with low molecular weight, low protein binding, water solubility and above all a small volume of distribution; methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium and salicylate meet all four. Digoxin has a volume of distribution of 6 to 7 L/kg and tricyclics such as amitriptyline over 10 L/kg, so almost all of the drug sits in tissue, and diazepam is more than 95 percent protein bound, so cleaning the plasma achieves nothing.`,tag:`Frequently asked`},{id:`q-ph-toxicology-14`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,level:`advanced`,difficulty:`hard`,stem:`In amitriptyline overdose with a QRS duration of 140 ms and hypotension, sodium bicarbonate helps because:`,options:[`It traps the drug in the urine as an ionised salt`,`It converts amitriptyline to an inactive metabolite`,`The sodium load plus alkalinisation overcomes blockade of the cardiac fast sodium channel`,`It chelates the tricyclic in plasma`],answer:2,explanation:`Tricyclics block the fast sodium channel of the myocardium, which widens the QRS and predisposes to ventricular arrhythmia; a hypertonic sodium load steepens the electrochemical gradient while alkalinisation reduces drug binding to the channel, so the QRS narrows and blood pressure improves. This is a pharmacodynamic rescue and quite different from the ion trapping used for salicylate, and since tricyclics have a very large volume of distribution, forced diuresis and dialysis are useless.`}],n=[{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-1`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`Which 5-HT receptor is a ligand-gated ion channel?`,back:`5-HT3. All the others are G-protein coupled.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-2`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`Antidote for paracetamol poisoning and how it works?`,back:`N-acetylcysteine; it replenishes hepatic glutathione to detoxify NAPQI. Most effective within 8 to 10 hours.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-3`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`Antiplatelet dose of aspirin and the reason it is irreversible?`,back:`75 to 150 mg daily. It acetylates Ser530 of platelet COX-1, and platelets have no nucleus to resynthesise the enzyme.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-4`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`Why is allopurinol not started during an acute gout attack?`,back:`A sudden fall in plasma urate mobilises crystals from tophi and prolongs the attack. Start 2 to 3 weeks later under colchicine or NSAID cover.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-5`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`Where do steroids act in the eicosanoid cascade, and where do NSAIDs?`,back:`Steroids inhibit phospholipase A2 via lipocortin, blocking both COX and LOX limbs. NSAIDs inhibit only COX.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-6`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`Mechanism of colchicine in gout?`,back:`Binds tubulin, prevents microtubule polymerisation, so neutrophils cannot migrate into the joint. Dose-limiting effect is diarrhoea.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-1`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`Which antidiabetic classes cause hypoglycaemia when used alone?`,back:`Only the insulin secretagogues: sulfonylureas and meglitinides (plus insulin itself).`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-2`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`Molecular target of sulfonylureas?`,back:`The SUR1 subunit of the beta cell ATP-sensitive potassium channel; closing it depolarises the cell and releases insulin.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-3`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`Which insulins can be given intravenously?`,back:`Regular (soluble) insulin and the rapid-acting analogues. Never NPH, glargine or detemir.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-4`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`Which antithyroid drug also blocks peripheral T4 to T3 conversion?`,back:`Propylthiouracil. It is therefore preferred in thyroid storm and in the first trimester.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-5`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`What is the Wolff-Chaikoff effect?`,back:`Large doses of iodide acutely inhibit thyroid hormone organification and release. The block lasts only 10 to 14 days.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-6`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`Why is metformin withheld in renal impairment?`,back:`It is excreted unchanged by the kidney; accumulation plus reduced hepatic lactate clearance risks lactic acidosis. Avoid below eGFR 30 mL/min/1.73 m2.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-1`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Equivalent anti-inflammatory doses: hydrocortisone, prednisolone, dexamethasone?`,back:`20 mg hydrocortisone = 5 mg prednisolone = 0.75 mg dexamethasone.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-2`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Which steroids have no mineralocorticoid activity?`,back:`Dexamethasone, betamethasone and triamcinolone, because of 16-substitution on the D ring.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-3`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Why must alternate-day steroid therapy use prednisolone, not dexamethasone?`,back:`Only an intermediate-acting steroid leaves a drug-free interval for the HPA axis to recover; long-acting drugs suppress it continuously.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-4`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Tamoxifen versus raloxifene at the endometrium?`,back:`Tamoxifen is a partial agonist and raises endometrial cancer risk; raloxifene is endometrium-neutral.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-5`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Why is methylergometrine never used before delivery of the baby?`,back:`It causes a sustained tonic uterine contraction. Oxytocin gives rhythmic contractions and is used to augment labour.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-6`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Mechanism of bisphosphonates?`,back:`They inhibit farnesyl pyrophosphate synthase in osteoclasts, causing apoptosis and reducing bone resorption.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-1`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Why are PPIs taken 30 to 60 minutes before food?`,back:`They are acid-activated prodrugs that can only bind actively secreting proton pumps, so peak plasma level must coincide with a meal.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-2`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Standard triple therapy for H. pylori?`,back:`PPI twice daily plus amoxicillin 1 g twice daily plus clarithromycin 500 mg twice daily, for 14 days.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-3`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Metoclopramide versus domperidone: key difference?`,back:`Metoclopramide crosses into the CNS and causes extrapyramidal reactions; domperidone does not, but it prolongs the QT interval.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-4`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Therapeutic plasma range of theophylline?`,back:`10 to 20 micrograms/mL. Above 20 causes nausea and vomiting; above 35 to 40 causes arrhythmias and seizures.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-5`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Why must a LABA never be used alone in asthma?`,back:`It masks worsening inflammation and is associated with increased asthma deaths. Always combine with an inhaled corticosteroid.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-6`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Two therapeutic roles of acetylcysteine?`,back:`Mucolytic (cleaves mucus disulfide bonds) and antidote in paracetamol poisoning (replenishes glutathione).`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-1`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Nephron site and transporter for loop diuretics and thiazides?`,back:`Loop: thick ascending limb, Na-K-2Cl (NKCC2). Thiazide: distal convoluted tubule, Na-Cl (NCC).`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-2`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Effect of loop diuretics versus thiazides on urinary calcium?`,back:`Loops increase calcium excretion (used in hypercalcaemia). Thiazides decrease it (used to prevent calcium stones).`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-3`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Which diuretics cause hyperkalaemia?`,back:`Aldosterone antagonists (spironolactone, eplerenone) and ENaC blockers (amiloride, triamterene).`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-4`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Acid-base effect of acetazolamide?`,back:`Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis with alkaline urine. The acidosis makes the diuresis self-limiting after 2 to 3 days.`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-5`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Treatment of central versus nephrogenic diabetes insipidus?`,back:`Central: desmopressin. Nephrogenic: thiazide plus salt restriction, or amiloride when caused by lithium.`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-6`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Which loop diuretic is safe in sulfonamide allergy?`,back:`Ethacrynic acid, the only non-sulfonamide loop diuretic. It is also the most ototoxic.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-1`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Which protein synthesis inhibitors act on 30S and which on 50S?`,back:`30S: aminoglycosides and tetracyclines. 50S: chloramphenicol, clindamycin, macrolides, linezolid.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-2`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Mechanism of vancomycin?`,back:`Binds the D-alanyl-D-alanine terminus of the peptidoglycan precursor, blocking transglycosylation and transpeptidation.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-3`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Why is cotrimoxazole bactericidal when each component is bacteriostatic?`,back:`Sequential blockade: sulfamethoxazole inhibits dihydropteroate synthase and trimethoprim inhibits dihydrofolate reductase.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-4`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Genetic basis of MRSA?`,back:`The mecA gene encodes PBP2a, a transpeptidase with very low affinity for all beta-lactams.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-5`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`What is the post-antibiotic effect and which drugs show it strongly?`,back:`Continued suppression of bacterial growth after the level falls below MIC. Marked with aminoglycosides and fluoroquinolones.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-6`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Why is chloramphenicol dangerous in neonates?`,back:`Immature glucuronidation and renal excretion cause accumulation, giving grey baby syndrome with hypothermia and cardiovascular collapse.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-1`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Signature adverse effect of each first-line antitubercular drug?`,back:`Isoniazid: neuropathy. Rifampicin: hepatitis and orange urine. Pyrazinamide: hyperuricaemia. Ethambutol: optic neuritis. Streptomycin: ototoxicity.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-2`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Which enzyme activates isoniazid, and what does its mutation cause?`,back:`The mycobacterial catalase-peroxidase KatG. katG mutation is the commonest cause of isoniazid resistance.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-3`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Target of the echinocandins?`,back:`Beta-1,3-glucan synthase in the fungal cell wall. Every other antifungal class targets the membrane or the nucleus.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-4`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Five antiretroviral classes?`,back:`NRTI, NNRTI, protease inhibitor, integrase inhibitor, and entry or fusion inhibitor. HAART uses at least three drugs from two classes.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-5`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Why is artemisinin never given as monotherapy?`,back:`Its half-life is very short, so residual parasites survive. It is paired with a long-acting partner drug as ACT to prevent resistance.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-6`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Which antimalarial must be screened for G6PD deficiency?`,back:`Primaquine (and tafenoquine); their oxidative metabolites cause haemolysis.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-1`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Vinca alkaloids versus taxanes: mechanism?`,back:`Vincas prevent microtubule polymerisation; taxanes prevent depolymerisation. Both arrest the cell in metaphase.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-2`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Signature toxicities: cyclophosphamide, doxorubicin, bleomycin, cisplatin, vincristine?`,back:`Haemorrhagic cystitis, cardiomyopathy, pulmonary fibrosis, nephrotoxicity, peripheral neuropathy.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-3`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Why folinic acid and not folic acid after high-dose methotrexate?`,back:`Folinic acid is already reduced and bypasses the blocked dihydrofolate reductase; folic acid still needs that enzyme.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-4`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Which anticancer drugs are cell cycle non-specific?`,back:`Alkylating agents, nitrosoureas, cisplatin, doxorubicin and dactinomycin. They act in any phase, including G0.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-5`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Targets of imatinib, trastuzumab and rituximab?`,back:`BCR-ABL tyrosine kinase and c-KIT; HER2 receptor; CD20 on B cells.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-6`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Mechanism of ciclosporin versus sirolimus?`,back:`Ciclosporin binds cyclophilin and inhibits calcineurin, cutting IL-2. Sirolimus binds FKBP-12 and inhibits mTOR.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-1`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`Antidotes for organophosphate, cyanide and iron poisoning?`,back:`Atropine plus pralidoxime; sodium nitrite plus sodium thiosulfate (or hydroxocobalamin); desferrioxamine.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-2`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`When is gastric lavage contraindicated?`,back:`Corrosive acids and alkalis, petroleum products such as kerosene, convulsions, and an unprotected airway.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-3`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`What poisons does activated charcoal NOT adsorb?`,back:`Pesticides, hydrocarbons, acids and alkalis, iron, lithium, and alcohols and solvents.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-4`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`What is ageing in organophosphate poisoning?`,back:`Irreversible dealkylation of the enzyme-phosphate bond, after which no oxime can reactivate cholinesterase. Give pralidoxime early.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-5`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`Which EDTA salt is used for lead chelation and why?`,back:`Calcium disodium EDTA. Plain disodium EDTA chelates plasma calcium and can cause fatal hypocalcaemic tetany.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-6`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`Clinical signature of lead versus arsenic poisoning?`,back:`Lead: blue gum line, wrist drop, colic, basophilic stippling. Arsenic: rice-water stools, garlic breath, Mees lines on nails.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-7`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`Dose-dependent effects of aspirin?`,back:`75 to 150 mg antiplatelet; 0.3 to 0.6 g analgesic and antipyretic; 3 to 5 g anti-inflammatory. Below 2 g it retains urate, above 5 g it is uricosuric.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-8`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`What is the Samter triad?`,back:`Asthma, nasal polyps and aspirin or NSAID sensitivity. COX-1 block shunts arachidonate to leukotrienes; montelukast helps.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-9`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`Why must the thiopurine dose be cut when allopurinol is added?`,back:`Allopurinol blocks xanthine oxidase, the main route that inactivates 6-mercaptopurine. Give about 25 percent of the usual azathioprine dose, or avoid the pair.`},{id:`f-ph-autacoids-nsaids-10`,topicId:`ph-autacoids-nsaids`,front:`First drug, dose and route in anaphylaxis?`,back:`Adrenaline 0.5 mg of 1 in 1000 intramuscularly into the anterolateral thigh, repeated every 5 to 15 minutes if needed.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-7`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`The 1800 rule and the 500 rule?`,back:`Correction factor (mg/dL per unit) = 1800 / total daily insulin dose, or 1500 for regular insulin. Carbohydrate ratio (g per unit) = 500 / total daily dose.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-8`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`Antithyroid drug choice in pregnancy?`,back:`Propylthiouracil in the first trimester (methimazole embryopathy), carbimazole or methimazole later (PTU hepatotoxicity). Radioiodine is contraindicated throughout.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-9`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`What symptom must a patient on carbimazole report at once?`,back:`Sore throat, mouth ulcers or fever — possible agranulocytosis. Stop the drug and check the white cell count.`},{id:`f-ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid-10`,topicId:`ph-endocrine-diabetes-thyroid`,front:`Which antidiabetics cause weight gain and which cause weight loss?`,back:`Gain: insulin, sulfonylureas, meglitinides, pioglitazone. Loss: metformin, GLP-1 agonists, SGLT2 inhibitors. Acarbose and gliptins are roughly neutral.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-7`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Which corticosteroids are used to mature the fetal lung, and why those?`,back:`Betamethasone and dexamethasone. They escape placental 11-beta-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2, so they reach the fetus; prednisolone and hydrocortisone do not.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-8`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Which uterotonic is barred in asthma and which in hypertension?`,back:`Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2-alpha) in asthma; methylergometrine in hypertension and pre-eclampsia. Oxytocin and misoprostol are acceptable in both.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-9`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Why is testosterone inactive orally, and how is that solved?`,back:`Near-complete first-pass metabolism. Use depot esters or transdermal patches; 17-alpha-alkylated androgens are orally active but hepatotoxic.`},{id:`f-ph-steroids-reproductive-10`,topicId:`ph-steroids-reproductive`,front:`Clomiphene versus letrozole for ovulation induction?`,back:`Clomiphene blocks hypothalamic oestrogen receptors and removes negative feedback; letrozole inhibits aromatase and lowers oestrogen. Both raise FSH and LH.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-7`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Which PPIs should be avoided with clopidogrel?`,back:`Omeprazole and esomeprazole — strong CYP2C19 inhibitors, so clopidogrel is not activated. Use pantoprazole or rabeprazole.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-8`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Why is sucralfate taken on an empty stomach?`,back:`It needs a pH below about 4 to polymerise into a paste over the ulcer base. Antacids, PPIs and food defeat it, and it binds other drugs in the gut.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-9`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Loading dose formula and the aminophylline salt factor?`,back:`Loading dose = target Cp x Vd / salt factor. Salt factor of aminophylline is 0.8, so 450 mg of theophylline base needs about 563 mg of aminophylline.`},{id:`f-ph-git-respiratory-10`,topicId:`ph-git-respiratory`,front:`Acute versus delayed chemotherapy-induced vomiting: drug of choice?`,back:`Acute, first 24 hours: 5-HT3 blocker such as ondansetron plus dexamethasone. Delayed, days 2 to 5: NK1 antagonist aprepitant plus dexamethasone.`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-7`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Cockcroft-Gault equation for creatinine clearance?`,back:`CrCl (mL/min) = (140 - age) x weight in kg / (72 x serum creatinine in mg/dL). Multiply by 0.85 for a woman.`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-8`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Spironolactone versus eplerenone?`,back:`Both block the mineralocorticoid receptor. Spironolactone also hits androgen and progesterone receptors, causing gynaecomastia; eplerenone is selective.`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-9`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`What is sequential nephron blockade?`,back:`Adding a thiazide such as metolazone to a loop diuretic to block the hypertrophied distal tubule in diuretic resistance. Very powerful; watch for hypokalaemia.`},{id:`f-ph-diuretics-renal-10`,topicId:`ph-diuretics-renal`,front:`Which diuretic corrects diuretic-induced metabolic alkalosis?`,back:`Acetazolamide, because it makes the kidney excrete bicarbonate. Replace potassium alongside it.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-7`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Mechanism and three signature toxicities of fluoroquinolones?`,back:`Inhibit DNA gyrase and topoisomerase IV. Tendinitis and rupture, QT prolongation, peripheral neuropathy. Avoid in children and pregnancy.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-8`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Which drugs give a disulfiram-like reaction with alcohol?`,back:`Metronidazole and tinidazole, cefoperazone, chlorpropamide and griseofulvin. Acetaldehyde accumulates through aldehyde dehydrogenase inhibition.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-9`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Which antibiotic can precipitate serotonin syndrome with an SSRI?`,back:`Linezolid — it is a reversible non-selective monoamine oxidase inhibitor as well as a 50S protein synthesis inhibitor.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-antibacterial-10`,topicId:`ph-chemo-antibacterial`,front:`Why is ceftriaxone avoided in neonates?`,back:`It precipitates with intravenous calcium and displaces bilirubin from albumin, risking kernicterus. Cefotaxime is used instead.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-7`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Which antitubercular acts only at acidic pH, and what does it cost?`,back:`Pyrazinamide — sterilises bacilli inside macrophages and shortened therapy to 6 months. Costs: hepatotoxicity and hyperuricaemia.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-8`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Rifampicin or rifabutin in a patient on a protease inhibitor?`,back:`Rifabutin. Rifampicin is a strong CYP3A4 inducer and drives protease inhibitor levels to subtherapeutic.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-9`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Which test is mandatory before starting abacavir?`,back:`HLA-B*5701 typing. Carriers develop a severe, sometimes fatal hypersensitivity reaction, and rechallenge is forbidden.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral-10`,topicId:`ph-chemo-tb-fungal-viral`,front:`Mechanism of terbinafine and why it suits nail infection?`,back:`Inhibits squalene epoxidase, so ergosterol is lost and fungicidal squalene accumulates. It concentrates in keratin, nail and sebum.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-7`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Mosteller formula for body surface area?`,back:`BSA in m2 = square root of (height in cm x weight in kg / 3600). A 160 cm, 60 kg adult is about 1.63 m2.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-8`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Match the protective agent: mesna, dexrazoxane, amifostine, folinic acid.`,back:`Cyclophosphamide cystitis; anthracycline cardiotoxicity; cisplatin nephrotoxicity; methotrexate rescue.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-9`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Why is an NSAID risky with low-dose methotrexate?`,back:`NSAIDs compete for renal tubular secretion and cut renal perfusion, so methotrexate accumulates — mucositis and pancytopenia.`},{id:`f-ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno-10`,topicId:`ph-chemo-anticancer-immuno`,front:`Ciclosporin versus tacrolimus: which adverse effects separate them?`,back:`Ciclosporin: gum hypertrophy and hirsutism. Tacrolimus: new-onset diabetes, alopecia, more neurotoxicity. Both: nephrotoxicity and hypertension.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-7`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`Which poisonings respond to urinary alkalinisation?`,back:`Weak acids: salicylate, phenobarbitone, methotrexate, 2,4-D. Give sodium bicarbonate and correct potassium first.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-8`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`Four properties that make a poison dialysable?`,back:`Low molecular weight, low protein binding, water soluble, small volume of distribution. Methanol, ethylene glycol, lithium, salicylate qualify; digoxin and tricyclics do not.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-9`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`Antidote in Datura or atropine poisoning, and why not neostigmine?`,back:`Physostigmine, a tertiary carbamate that crosses the blood-brain barrier. Neostigmine is quaternary and cannot reach the central delirium.`},{id:`f-ph-toxicology-10`,topicId:`ph-toxicology`,front:`Why is calcium avoided in digoxin toxicity, and what is given instead?`,back:`Calcium can precipitate a refractory ventricular contraction. Use digoxin-specific Fab fragments, correct potassium, atropine for bradycardia.`}];export{n as flashcards,e as notes,t as questions};